window.DOMINANCE_TOPICS = [{"slug":"cv-introduction","title":"Introduction to the Heart and Circulation","group":"Heart","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"1-6","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"1-why-do-we-need-a-circulatory-system","title":"1. Why do we need a circulatory system?"},{"id":"2-the-two-major-circulatory-pathways","title":"2. The two major circulatory pathways"},{"id":"3-the-heart-and-its-four-chambers","title":"3. The heart and its four chambers"},{"id":"4-why-is-the-left-ventricular-wall-thicker-than-the-right","title":"4. Why is the left ventricular wall thicker than the right?"},{"id":"5-systole-and-diastole","title":"5. Systole and diastole"},{"id":"6-cardiac-muscle-and-the-conducting-system","title":"6. Cardiac muscle and the conducting system"},{"id":"understanding-check","title":"Understanding Check"}],"html":"<h1>Introduction to the Heart and Circulation</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-why-do-we-need-a-circulatory-system\">1. Why do we need a circulatory system?</h2>\n<p>Every cell in the body requires oxygen and nutrients to function. At the same time, cells produce metabolic waste products, including carbon dioxide, which must be removed.</p>\n<p>The <strong>circulatory system</strong> makes this continuous transport possible.</p>\n<p>It consists of three major components:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Heart:</strong> Pumps blood through the circulation.</li>\n<li><strong>Blood vessels:</strong> Provide pathways through which blood travels.</li>\n<li><strong>Blood:</strong> Transports oxygen, nutrients, hormones, waste products and heat.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>For example, during exercise, skeletal muscles require more oxygen and nutrients to meet their increased energy demands. The circulatory system helps deliver these substances while transporting carbon dioxide and other metabolic products away.</p>\n<h2 id=\"2-the-two-major-circulatory-pathways\">2. The two major circulatory pathways</h2>\n<p>Blood circulates through two connected pathways:</p>\n<p><strong>Pulmonary circulation:</strong> Carries blood from the right side of the heart to the lungs, where carbon dioxide is released and oxygen is taken up. The oxygenated blood then returns to the left side of the heart.</p>\n<p><strong>Systemic circulation:</strong> Carries oxygenated blood from the left side of the heart to tissues throughout the body. After delivering oxygen and collecting carbon dioxide, the blood returns to the right side of the heart.</p>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration\" id=\"cvs01-figure-01\">\n  <a href=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f2/2101_Blood_Flow_Through_the_Heart.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" aria-label=\"Open full-size pulmonary and systemic circulation diagram\">\n    <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/f/f2/2101_Blood_Flow_Through_the_Heart.jpg\" alt=\"Diagram of systemic and pulmonary circulation, showing blood travelling between the right heart and lungs, then the left heart and body organs.\" />\n  </a>\n  <figcaption><strong>Figure 01 — Pulmonary and systemic circulation.</strong> Follow the blood flow through the lungs and body tissues. Tap to enlarge. <span class=\"figure-credit\">OpenStax College, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2101_Blood_Flow_Through_the_Heart.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">original illustration</a>, <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY 3.0</a>; unmodified.</span></figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n<p>These two circuits are arranged <strong>in series</strong>, meaning blood passes through one circuit and then the other in a continuous pathway.</p>\n<p>Consequently, under stable conditions, the right and left ventricles must pump approximately equal volumes of blood per minute. Otherwise, blood would progressively accumulate in one circulation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"3-the-heart-and-its-four-chambers\">3. The heart and its four chambers</h2>\n<p>The heart is a muscular organ that functions as two coordinated pumps.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Right atrium:</strong> Receives blood returning from the body through the superior and inferior venae cavae.</li>\n<li><strong>Right ventricle:</strong> Pumps blood towards the lungs through the pulmonary trunk and pulmonary arteries.</li>\n<li><strong>Left atrium:</strong> Receives oxygenated blood returning from the lungs through the pulmonary veins.</li>\n<li><strong>Left ventricle:</strong> Pumps oxygenated blood into the aorta for distribution throughout the body.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>atria primarily receive blood and assist ventricular filling</strong>, while the <strong>ventricles generate the pressure needed to pump blood into the arteries</strong>.</p>\n<figure class=\"teaching-illustration\" id=\"cvs01-figure-02\">\n  <a href=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/12/Blausen_0457_Heart_SectionalAnatomy.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" aria-label=\"Open full-size labelled heart anatomy diagram\">\n    <img loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/12/Blausen_0457_Heart_SectionalAnatomy.png\" alt=\"Sectional anatomy of the human heart showing four chambers, cardiac valves, great vessels, interventricular septum, and the thicker left ventricular wall.\" />\n  </a>\n  <figcaption><strong>Figure 02 — Internal structure of the heart.</strong> Identify the chambers, valves, major vessels and differences in ventricular wall thickness. Tap to enlarge. <span class=\"figure-credit\">Blausen Medical Communications, Inc., <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blausen_0457_Heart_SectionalAnatomy.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">original illustration</a>, <a href=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">CC BY 3.0</a>; unmodified.</span></figcaption>\n</figure>\n\n<h3>How does blood flow through the heart?</h3>\n<p>Follow the pathway:</p>\n<p><strong>Body tissues → Venae cavae → Right atrium → Tricuspid valve → Right ventricle → Pulmonary valve → Pulmonary trunk and arteries → Lungs → Pulmonary veins → Left atrium → Mitral valve → Left ventricle → Aortic valve → Aorta → Body tissues.</strong></p>\n<p>The cardiac valves help maintain one-way blood flow by preventing blood from flowing backwards.</p>\n<p>Notice that the pulmonary arteries carry relatively deoxygenated blood, while the pulmonary veins carry oxygenated blood.</p>\n<p>This is because <strong>arteries carry blood away from the heart, whereas veins carry blood towards the heart</strong>, regardless of oxygen content.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-why-is-the-left-ventricular-wall-thicker-than-the-right\">4. Why is the left ventricular wall thicker than the right?</h2>\n<p>Although both ventricles pump approximately the same volume of blood per minute, they must generate different pressures.</p>\n<p>To understand why, consider <strong>vascular resistance</strong>, which refers to the opposition that blood vessels offer to blood flow.</p>\n<p>Imagine water flowing through a pipe. When the pipe narrows, it becomes harder to maintain the same flow unless a greater pressure difference is applied.</p>\n<p>A similar principle applies to blood vessels.</p>\n<p>The systemic circulation contains many small arteries and arterioles whose narrow lumens and muscular walls help regulate blood flow to different organs. Contraction or relaxation of their vascular smooth muscle changes the vessel diameter and therefore its resistance to blood flow.</p>\n<p>Collectively, the systemic vascular network offers substantially more resistance than the pulmonary vascular network.</p>\n<p>The pulmonary circulation, by comparison, has a relatively low-resistance network of vessels that allows blood to pass through the lungs at much lower pressures.</p>\n<p><strong>What does this mean for the ventricles?</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>left ventricle</strong> must generate higher pressure to maintain blood flow through the systemic circulation. Its muscular wall is therefore thicker.</p>\n<p>The <strong>right ventricle</strong> pumps blood through the lower-resistance pulmonary circulation, so it normally requires much less pressure and has a thinner muscular wall.</p>\n<p>The lower pressure in the pulmonary circulation is also beneficial because the lungs contain delicate capillaries surrounding the alveoli. Excessively high pressure in these capillaries can force more fluid into the surrounding lung tissue, potentially causing <strong>pulmonary oedema (fluid accumulation in the lungs)</strong> and interfering with gas exchange.</p>\n<p><strong>Key distinction:</strong> The left ventricle normally generates greater pressure, not a greater volume of blood per minute.</p>\n<p><em>The factors determining vascular resistance will be explored further in Blood Flow and Hemodynamics.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"5-systole-and-diastole\">5. Systole and diastole</h2>\n<p>The pumping activity of the heart involves alternating periods of contraction and relaxation.</p>\n<p><strong>Systole</strong> refers to the period of contraction of a heart chamber, while <strong>diastole</strong> refers to its period of relaxation.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Atrial systole:</strong> The atria contract, contributing the final portion of blood needed to complete ventricular filling.</li>\n<li><strong>Atrial diastole:</strong> The atria relax and receive blood returning from the body and lungs. During ventricular filling, blood can also pass from the relaxed atria into the ventricles.</li>\n<li><strong>Ventricular systole:</strong> The ventricles contract and generate pressure, allowing blood to be ejected into the pulmonary trunk (right ventricle) and aorta (left ventricle).</li>\n<li><strong>Ventricular diastole:</strong> The ventricles relax and fill with blood in preparation for the next contraction.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Importantly, <strong>atrial systole normally occurs towards the end of ventricular diastole</strong>. The atria complete their contribution to ventricular filling before the ventricles contract.</p>\n<p><em>The detailed sequence of pressure changes, valve movements and phases of the heartbeat will be explained in the Cardiac Cycle chapter.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"6-cardiac-muscle-and-the-conducting-system\">6. Cardiac muscle and the conducting system</h2>\n<p>For the heart to pump blood effectively, it must generate sufficient contractile force, and its chambers must contract in a coordinated sequence.</p>\n<p>Two important components make this possible:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cardiac muscle (myocardium):</strong> Produces the force required for contraction and blood ejection.</li>\n<li><strong>Specialised cardiac conducting system:</strong> Generates and distributes electrical impulses that coordinate the heartbeat.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Electrical activation normally precedes contraction, allowing the atria and ventricles to pump in an organised sequence.</p>\n<p><em>The cellular mechanisms of contraction and the electrical conducting pathways will be covered in the Cardiac Muscle Physiology and Cardiac Conduction chapters.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"understanding-check\">Understanding Check</h2>\n<p><strong>1. Why must the right and left ventricles pump approximately equal volumes of blood per minute?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>The pulmonary and systemic circulations are connected in series. Blood pumped by the right ventricle passes through the lungs before reaching the left ventricle. If their outputs remained unequal, blood would progressively accumulate in one circulation rather than maintaining a stable distribution.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2. Why does the left ventricle have a thicker muscular wall than the right ventricle?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>The systemic circulation offers greater resistance to blood flow than the pulmonary circulation. Consequently, the left ventricle must generate higher pressure to maintain blood flow through the body, requiring a thicker muscular wall. The right ventricle pumps blood through the lower-resistance pulmonary circulation and therefore operates at much lower pressure.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3. A blood vessel carries oxygenated blood towards the heart. Is it an artery or a vein? Explain.</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>It is a vein. Blood vessels are classified by the direction in which they carry blood relative to the heart, not by oxygen content. Pulmonary veins, for example, carry oxygenated blood from the lungs towards the left atrium.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>4. Does ventricular contraction always mean blood is being ejected?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>No. At the beginning of ventricular systole, the ventricles first develop pressure while their outflow valves remain closed. Blood is ejected only when ventricular pressure exceeds the pressure in the corresponding outflow artery. This initial period is called <em>isovolumetric contraction</em> and will be discussed in the Cardiac Cycle chapter.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>5. During atrial systole, are the ventricles also in systole?</strong></p>\n<details><summary>Reveal explanation</summary><p>Normally, no. Atrial systole occurs near the end of ventricular diastole, allowing atrial contraction to contribute to ventricular filling before ventricular systole begins.</p></details>\n\n<hr />\n<p><em>Lecture connection: CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf, slides 1–6. The resistance analogy and fluid-filtration explanation are supplementary foundations; detailed hemodynamics and cardiac-cycle mechanisms appear in their corresponding chapters.</em></p>\n","plain":" Introduction to the Heart and Circulation \n 1. Why do we need a circulatory system? \n Every cell in the body requires oxygen and nutrients to function. At the same time, cells produce metabolic waste products, including carbon dioxide, which must be removed. \n The  circulatory system  makes this continuous transport possible. \n It consists of three major components: \n \n  Heart:  Pumps blood through the circulation. \n  Blood vessels:  Provide pathways through which blood travels. \n  Blood:  Transports oxygen, nutrients, hormones, waste products and heat. \n \n For example, during exercise, skeletal muscles require more oxygen and nutrients to meet their increased energy demands. The circulatory system helps deliver these substances while transporting carbon dioxide and other metabolic products away. \n 2. The two major circulatory pathways \n Blood circulates through two connected pathways: \n  Pulmonary circulation:  Carries blood from the right side of the heart to the lungs, where carbon dioxide is released and oxygen is taken up. The oxygenated blood then returns to the left side of the heart. \n  Systemic circulation:  Carries oxygenated blood from the left side of the heart to tissues throughout the body. After delivering oxygen and collecting carbon dioxide, the blood returns to the right side of the heart. \n \n   \n     \n   \n    Figure 01 — Pulmonary and systemic circulation.  Follow the blood flow through the lungs and body tissues. Tap to enlarge.  OpenStax College,  original illustration ,  CC BY 3.0 ; unmodified.  \n \n\n These two circuits are arranged  in series , meaning blood passes through one circuit and then the other in a continuous pathway. \n Consequently, under stable conditions, the right and left ventricles must pump approximately equal volumes of blood per minute. Otherwise, blood would progressively accumulate in one circulation. \n 3. The heart and its four chambers \n The heart is a muscular organ that functions as two coordinated pumps. \n \n  Right atrium:  Receives blood returning from the body through the superior and inferior venae cavae. \n  Right ventricle:  Pumps blood towards the lungs through the pulmonary trunk and pulmonary arteries. \n  Left atrium:  Receives oxygenated blood returning from the lungs through the pulmonary veins. \n  Left ventricle:  Pumps oxygenated blood into the aorta for distribution throughout the body. \n \n The  atria primarily receive blood and assist ventricular filling , while the  ventricles generate the pressure needed to pump blood into the arteries . \n \n   \n     \n   \n    Figure 02 — Internal structure of the heart.  Identify the chambers, valves, major vessels and differences in ventricular wall thickness. Tap to enlarge.  Blausen Medical Communications, Inc.,  original illustration ,  CC BY 3.0 ; unmodified.  \n \n\n How does blood flow through the heart? \n Follow the pathway: \n  Body tissues → Venae cavae → Right atrium → Tricuspid valve → Right ventricle → Pulmonary valve → Pulmonary trunk and arteries → Lungs → Pulmonary veins → Left atrium → Mitral valve → Left ventricle → Aortic valve → Aorta → Body tissues.  \n The cardiac valves help maintain one-way blood flow by preventing blood from flowing backwards. \n Notice that the pulmonary arteries carry relatively deoxygenated blood, while the pulmonary veins carry oxygenated blood. \n This is because  arteries carry blood away from the heart, whereas veins carry blood towards the heart , regardless of oxygen content. \n 4. Why is the left ventricular wall thicker than the right? \n Although both ventricles pump approximately the same volume of blood per minute, they must generate different pressures. \n To understand why, consider  vascular resistance , which refers to the opposition that blood vessels offer to blood flow. \n Imagine water flowing through a pipe. When the pipe narrows, it becomes harder to maintain the same flow unless a greater pressure difference is applied. \n A similar principle applies to blood vessels. \n The systemic circulation contains many small arteries and arterioles whose narrow lumens and muscular walls help regulate blood flow to different organs. Contraction or relaxation of their vascular smooth muscle changes the vessel diameter and therefore its resistance to blood flow. \n Collectively, the systemic vascular network offers substantially more resistance than the pulmonary vascular network. \n The pulmonary circulation, by comparison, has a relatively low-resistance network of vessels that allows blood to pass through the lungs at much lower pressures. \n  What does this mean for the ventricles?  \n The  left ventricle  must generate higher pressure to maintain blood flow through the systemic circulation. Its muscular wall is therefore thicker. \n The  right ventricle  pumps blood through the lower-resistance pulmonary circulation, so it normally requires much less pressure and has a thinner muscular wall. \n The lower pressure in the pulmonary circulation is also beneficial because the lungs contain delicate capillaries surrounding the alveoli. Excessively high pressure in these capillaries can force more fluid into the surrounding lung tissue, potentially causing  pulmonary oedema (fluid accumulation in the lungs)  and interfering with gas exchange. \n  Key distinction:  The left ventricle normally generates greater pressure, not a greater volume of blood per minute. \n  The factors determining vascular resistance will be explored further in Blood Flow and Hemodynamics.  \n 5. Systole and diastole \n The pumping activity of the heart involves alternating periods of contraction and relaxation. \n  Systole  refers to the period of contraction of a heart chamber, while  diastole  refers to its period of relaxation. \n \n  Atrial systole:  The atria contract, contributing the final portion of blood needed to complete ventricular filling. \n  Atrial diastole:  The atria relax and receive blood returning from the body and lungs. During ventricular filling, blood can also pass from the relaxed atria into the ventricles. \n  Ventricular systole:  The ventricles contract and generate pressure, allowing blood to be ejected into the pulmonary trunk (right ventricle) and aorta (left ventricle). \n  Ventricular diastole:  The ventricles relax and fill with blood in preparation for the next contraction. \n \n Importantly,  atrial systole normally occurs towards the end of ventricular diastole . The atria complete their contribution to ventricular filling before the ventricles contract. \n  The detailed sequence of pressure changes, valve movements and phases of the heartbeat will be explained in the Cardiac Cycle chapter.  \n 6. Cardiac muscle and the conducting system \n For the heart to pump blood effectively, it must generate sufficient contractile force, and its chambers must contract in a coordinated sequence. \n Two important components make this possible: \n \n  Cardiac muscle (myocardium):  Produces the force required for contraction and blood ejection. \n  Specialised cardiac conducting system:  Generates and distributes electrical impulses that coordinate the heartbeat. \n \n Electrical activation normally precedes contraction, allowing the atria and ventricles to pump in an organised sequence. \n  The cellular mechanisms of contraction and the electrical conducting pathways will be covered in the Cardiac Muscle Physiology and Cardiac Conduction chapters.  \n Understanding Check \n  1. Why must the right and left ventricles pump approximately equal volumes of blood per minute?  \n  Reveal explanation  The pulmonary and systemic circulations are connected in series. Blood pumped by the right ventricle passes through the lungs before reaching the left ventricle. If their outputs remained unequal, blood would progressively accumulate in one circulation rather than maintaining a stable distribution.  \n\n  2. Why does the left ventricle have a thicker muscular wall than the right ventricle?  \n  Reveal explanation  The systemic circulation offers greater resistance to blood flow than the pulmonary circulation. Consequently, the left ventricle must generate higher pressure to maintain blood flow through the body, requiring a thicker muscular wall. The right ventricle pumps blood through the lower-resistance pulmonary circulation and therefore operates at much lower pressure.  \n\n  3. A blood vessel carries oxygenated blood towards the heart. Is it an artery or a vein? Explain.  \n  Reveal explanation  It is a vein. Blood vessels are classified by the direction in which they carry blood relative to the heart, not by oxygen content. Pulmonary veins, for example, carry oxygenated blood from the lungs towards the left atrium.  \n\n  4. Does ventricular contraction always mean blood is being ejected?  \n  Reveal explanation  No. At the beginning of ventricular systole, the ventricles first develop pressure while their outflow valves remain closed. Blood is ejected only when ventricular pressure exceeds the pressure in the corresponding outflow artery. This initial period is called  isovolumetric contraction  and will be discussed in the Cardiac Cycle chapter.  \n\n  5. During atrial systole, are the ventricles also in systole?  \n  Reveal explanation  Normally, no. Atrial systole occurs near the end of ventricular diastole, allowing atrial contraction to contribute to ventricular filling before ventricular systole begins.  \n\n \n  Lecture connection: CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf, slides 1–6. The resistance analogy and fluid-filtration explanation are supplementary foundations; detailed hemodynamics and cardiac-cycle mechanisms appear in their corresponding chapters.  \n"},{"slug":"cardiac-muscle","title":"Cardiac Muscle Physiology","group":"Heart","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"4 and 18-20; added foundations","coverage":"supplemented","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"what-makes-cardiac-muscle-different","title":"What makes cardiac muscle different?"},{"id":"ventricular-muscle-action-potential","title":"Ventricular muscle action potential"},{"id":"excitation-contraction-coupling-electrical-signal-becomes-force","title":"Excitation–contraction coupling: electrical signal becomes force"},{"id":"why-this-matters","title":"Why this matters"},{"id":"cells-intercalated-discs-and-functional-syncytia","title":"Cells, intercalated discs and functional syncytia"},{"id":"contrast-pacemaker-and-ventricular-action-potentials","title":"Contrast pacemaker and ventricular action potentials"},{"id":"explain-relaxation-as-well-as-contraction","title":"Explain relaxation as well as contraction"}],"html":"<h1>Cardiac Muscle Physiology</h1>\n<h2 id=\"what-makes-cardiac-muscle-different\">What makes cardiac muscle different?</h2>\n<p>The myocardium contains short, branched, striated cells linked by <strong>intercalated discs</strong>. <strong>Fascia adherens</strong> and <strong>desmosomes</strong> transmit mechanical force and resist separation; <strong>gap junctions</strong> allow ionic current to pass between cells. Thus, the myocardium behaves as a coordinated functional syncytium, while individual cells remain separate.</p>\n<p>Cardiac contractile cells require an electrical signal before contracting, whereas nodal pacemaker cells can depolarize spontaneously. The atria and ventricles are electrically insulated by the fibrous skeleton except through the normal AV conduction route, ensuring sequence rather than simultaneous contraction.</p>\n<h2 id=\"ventricular-muscle-action-potential\">Ventricular muscle action potential</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Phase 0:</strong> rapid depolarization through voltage-gated fast Na⁺ channels.</li>\n<li><strong>Phase 1:</strong> brief initial repolarization, partly due to transient K⁺ outward current.</li>\n<li><strong>Phase 2 (plateau):</strong> inward L-type Ca²⁺ current is balanced by outward K⁺ current.</li>\n<li><strong>Phase 3:</strong> Ca²⁺ entry declines and K⁺ efflux drives repolarization.</li>\n<li><strong>Phase 4:</strong> relatively stable resting membrane potential, maintained chiefly by K⁺ permeability.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The plateau contributes to the prolonged refractory period. <strong>Cardiac muscle cannot normally develop tetanus</strong> because refractoriness overlaps most of the mechanical contraction; relaxation and refilling remain possible between beats.</p>\n<h2 id=\"excitation-contraction-coupling-electrical-signal-becomes-force\">Excitation–contraction coupling: electrical signal becomes force</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>The action potential opens <strong>L-type Ca²⁺ channels</strong> in the T-tubule membrane.</li>\n<li>Entering Ca²⁺ triggers larger Ca²⁺ release from sarcoplasmic reticulum through <strong>ryanodine receptors (RyR2)</strong>: calcium-induced calcium release.</li>\n<li>Cytosolic Ca²⁺ binds <strong>troponin C</strong>, permitting actin–myosin cycling and contraction.</li>\n<li><strong>SERCA2a</strong> pumps Ca²⁺ back into sarcoplasmic reticulum; the Na⁺–Ca²⁺ exchanger and membrane Ca²⁺ pumps assist removal, allowing relaxation.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Sympathetic β₁ stimulation increases cAMP/PKA signalling, improving Ca²⁺ entry, release, reuptake and therefore contraction and relaxation. <strong>Positive inotropy</strong> means stronger contraction at a given preload and afterload; <strong>positive lusitropy</strong> means faster relaxation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"why-this-matters\">Why this matters</h2>\n<p>A prolonged refractory period prevents the heart from remaining continuously contracted. Reduced SERCA activity impairs relaxation and can compromise ventricular filling. Blocking RyR2-mediated SR release reduces available activator Ca²⁺ and contractile strength.</p>\n<p><strong>Check:</strong> Why does a long cardiac action potential help pumping rather than merely slowing the heart?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary>Its long refractory period prevents rapid summation/tetanus. Coordinated contraction must be followed by relaxation so the chambers can fill again.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"cells-intercalated-discs-and-functional-syncytia\">Cells, intercalated discs and functional syncytia</h2>\n<p>Cardiac muscle cells are short, branched, striated cells connected end to end by <strong>intercalated discs</strong>. <strong>Fascia adherens</strong> anchor actin and transmit contractile force; <strong>desmosomes</strong> resist separation; <strong>gap junctions</strong> allow ions to pass between neighbouring cardiomyocytes and support electrical spread. Atria and ventricles behave as separate functional electrical syncytia, ordinarily linked by the specialised AV conduction system.</p>\n<p>The <strong>T-tubule–sarcoplasmic reticulum system</strong> couples membrane depolarisation to calcium release. Cardiac muscle depends on both extracellular Ca²⁺ entry and SR Ca²⁺ release, unlike skeletal muscle's primarily mechanical DHPR–RyR coupling.</p>\n<h2 id=\"contrast-pacemaker-and-ventricular-action-potentials\">Contrast pacemaker and ventricular action potentials</h2>\n<p>Working ventricular cells have a relatively stable resting membrane potential near <strong>−90 mV</strong>. Phase 0 is rapid Na⁺ entry; phase 1 brief initial repolarisation; phase 2 plateau reflects L-type Ca²⁺ entry balanced against K⁺ exit; phase 3 mainly K⁺-mediated repolarisation; phase 4 is a stable resting period. <strong>Fast inward Na⁺ channels</strong> account for the steep working-myocyte upstroke, not for the SA nodal upstroke.</p>\n<p>SA and AV nodal pacemaker cells lack a stable resting plateau: their phase 4 gradually depolarises through the <strong>funny current (If)</strong>, declining outward K⁺ current and other inward currents; phase 0 relies primarily on <strong>L-type Ca²⁺ channels</strong>. They repolarise through K⁺ efflux. Thus the SA node initiates impulses without a preceding external nerve impulse.</p>\n<h2 id=\"explain-relaxation-as-well-as-contraction\">Explain relaxation as well as contraction</h2>\n<p>Ca²⁺ entering through L-type channels triggers ryanodine receptors (<strong>RyR2</strong>) to release more Ca²⁺ from SR: <strong>calcium-induced calcium release</strong>. Ca²⁺ binding to troponin C permits actin–myosin cycling. Relaxation requires lowering cytosolic Ca²⁺: <strong>SERCA2a</strong> returns most Ca²⁺ to SR, while the Na⁺–Ca²⁺ exchanger expels substantial Ca²⁺ from the cell. Phospholamban normally inhibits SERCA; phosphorylation reduces this inhibition and accelerates relaxation.</p>\n<p>The long cardiac action potential creates a refractory period overlapping most of the twitch. This prevents normal tetanic summation so the heart can relax and refill. A shorter refractory period can increase susceptibility to premature excitation or re-entry, but does <strong>not</strong> mean a normal heart can undergo useful skeletal-type tetanus.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> If the RyR2 channels failed to open after L-type Ca²⁺ influx, force would fall because the larger secondary SR release would be absent. If SERCA were impaired, relaxation would slow and SR Ca²⁺ loading for subsequent beats could decrease.</p>\n","plain":" Cardiac Muscle Physiology \n What makes cardiac muscle different? \n The myocardium contains short, branched, striated cells linked by  intercalated discs .  Fascia adherens  and  desmosomes  transmit mechanical force and resist separation;  gap junctions  allow ionic current to pass between cells. Thus, the myocardium behaves as a coordinated functional syncytium, while individual cells remain separate. \n Cardiac contractile cells require an electrical signal before contracting, whereas nodal pacemaker cells can depolarize spontaneously. The atria and ventricles are electrically insulated by the fibrous skeleton except through the normal AV conduction route, ensuring sequence rather than simultaneous contraction. \n Ventricular muscle action potential \n \n  Phase 0:  rapid depolarization through voltage-gated fast Na⁺ channels. \n  Phase 1:  brief initial repolarization, partly due to transient K⁺ outward current. \n  Phase 2 (plateau):  inward L-type Ca²⁺ current is balanced by outward K⁺ current. \n  Phase 3:  Ca²⁺ entry declines and K⁺ efflux drives repolarization. \n  Phase 4:  relatively stable resting membrane potential, maintained chiefly by K⁺ permeability. \n \n The plateau contributes to the prolonged refractory period.  Cardiac muscle cannot normally develop tetanus  because refractoriness overlaps most of the mechanical contraction; relaxation and refilling remain possible between beats. \n Excitation–contraction coupling: electrical signal becomes force \n \n The action potential opens  L-type Ca²⁺ channels  in the T-tubule membrane. \n Entering Ca²⁺ triggers larger Ca²⁺ release from sarcoplasmic reticulum through  ryanodine receptors (RyR2) : calcium-induced calcium release. \n Cytosolic Ca²⁺ binds  troponin C , permitting actin–myosin cycling and contraction. \n  SERCA2a  pumps Ca²⁺ back into sarcoplasmic reticulum; the Na⁺–Ca²⁺ exchanger and membrane Ca²⁺ pumps assist removal, allowing relaxation. \n \n Sympathetic β₁ stimulation increases cAMP/PKA signalling, improving Ca²⁺ entry, release, reuptake and therefore contraction and relaxation.  Positive inotropy  means stronger contraction at a given preload and afterload;  positive lusitropy  means faster relaxation. \n Why this matters \n A prolonged refractory period prevents the heart from remaining continuously contracted. Reduced SERCA activity impairs relaxation and can compromise ventricular filling. Blocking RyR2-mediated SR release reduces available activator Ca²⁺ and contractile strength. \n  Check:  Why does a long cardiac action potential help pumping rather than merely slowing the heart? \n  Answer Its long refractory period prevents rapid summation/tetanus. Coordinated contraction must be followed by relaxation so the chambers can fill again. \n\n Cells, intercalated discs and functional syncytia \n Cardiac muscle cells are short, branched, striated cells connected end to end by  intercalated discs .  Fascia adherens  anchor actin and transmit contractile force;  desmosomes  resist separation;  gap junctions  allow ions to pass between neighbouring cardiomyocytes and support electrical spread. Atria and ventricles behave as separate functional electrical syncytia, ordinarily linked by the specialised AV conduction system. \n The  T-tubule–sarcoplasmic reticulum system  couples membrane depolarisation to calcium release. Cardiac muscle depends on both extracellular Ca²⁺ entry and SR Ca²⁺ release, unlike skeletal muscle's primarily mechanical DHPR–RyR coupling. \n Contrast pacemaker and ventricular action potentials \n Working ventricular cells have a relatively stable resting membrane potential near  −90 mV . Phase 0 is rapid Na⁺ entry; phase 1 brief initial repolarisation; phase 2 plateau reflects L-type Ca²⁺ entry balanced against K⁺ exit; phase 3 mainly K⁺-mediated repolarisation; phase 4 is a stable resting period.  Fast inward Na⁺ channels  account for the steep working-myocyte upstroke, not for the SA nodal upstroke. \n SA and AV nodal pacemaker cells lack a stable resting plateau: their phase 4 gradually depolarises through the  funny current (If) , declining outward K⁺ current and other inward currents; phase 0 relies primarily on  L-type Ca²⁺ channels . They repolarise through K⁺ efflux. Thus the SA node initiates impulses without a preceding external nerve impulse. \n Explain relaxation as well as contraction \n Ca²⁺ entering through L-type channels triggers ryanodine receptors ( RyR2 ) to release more Ca²⁺ from SR:  calcium-induced calcium release . Ca²⁺ binding to troponin C permits actin–myosin cycling. Relaxation requires lowering cytosolic Ca²⁺:  SERCA2a  returns most Ca²⁺ to SR, while the Na⁺–Ca²⁺ exchanger expels substantial Ca²⁺ from the cell. Phospholamban normally inhibits SERCA; phosphorylation reduces this inhibition and accelerates relaxation. \n The long cardiac action potential creates a refractory period overlapping most of the twitch. This prevents normal tetanic summation so the heart can relax and refill. A shorter refractory period can increase susceptibility to premature excitation or re-entry, but does  not  mean a normal heart can undergo useful skeletal-type tetanus. \n  Practice:  If the RyR2 channels failed to open after L-type Ca²⁺ influx, force would fall because the larger secondary SR release would be absent. If SERCA were impaired, relaxation would slow and SR Ca²⁺ loading for subsequent beats could decrease. \n"},{"slug":"cardiac-conduction","title":"Cardiac Conduction and Automaticity","group":"Heart","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"7-10","coverage":"lecturer supplemented","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"the-normal-pathway","title":"The normal pathway"},{"id":"why-does-the-sa-node-fire-on-its-own","title":"Why does the SA node fire on its own?"},{"id":"autonomic-control","title":"Autonomic control"},{"id":"where-the-structures-lie-and-why-sequence-matters","title":"Where the structures lie and why sequence matters"},{"id":"automaticity-and-different-pacemaker-rates","title":"Automaticity and different pacemaker rates"}],"html":"<h1>Cardiac Conduction and Automaticity</h1>\n<h2 id=\"the-normal-pathway\">The normal pathway</h2>\n<p>The <strong>sinoatrial (SA) node</strong>, near the superior vena cava–right atrial junction, usually sets the rhythm. Depolarization spreads through the atria, reaches the <strong>atrioventricular (AV) node</strong>, passes through the <strong>bundle of His</strong>, right and left bundle branches, then the <strong>Purkinje network</strong> to activate ventricular myocardium.</p>\n<p>The AV node delays conduction. This gives the atria time to contract before ventricular systole, assisting filling. Purkinje fibres conduct rapidly so the ventricles activate efficiently, usually from apex towards base.</p>\n<h2 id=\"why-does-the-sa-node-fire-on-its-own\">Why does the SA node fire on its own?</h2>\n<p>SA nodal cells do not have a stable resting membrane potential. During <strong>phase 4</strong>, net inward depolarizing current includes the hyperpolarization-activated <strong>funny current (I_f)</strong>, contributions from Ca²⁺ currents, and changing K⁺ conductance. Reaching threshold opens predominantly <strong>L-type Ca²⁺ channels</strong> for phase 0; K⁺ efflux mediates repolarization.</p>\n<p>Do <strong>not</strong> confuse this nodal Ca²⁺ upstroke with the fast Na⁺ upstroke of ventricular working muscle.</p>\n<h2 id=\"autonomic-control\">Autonomic control</h2>\n<p>Sympathetic noradrenaline acting at <strong>β₁ receptors</strong> steepens pacemaker depolarization and increases heart rate. Parasympathetic acetylcholine acting at <strong>M₂ receptors</strong> slows nodal firing and AV conduction, partly through GIRK K⁺ channels and reduced cAMP.</p>\n<p>If the SA node fails, slower subsidiary pacemakers may take over. AV conduction disturbances may produce delayed conduction or dropped ventricular beats; interpreting them requires the ECG.</p>\n<p><strong>Check:</strong> Why does AV nodal delay improve cardiac performance?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary>It allows atrial depolarization/contraction to occur before ventricular activation, improving ventricular filling rather than making both chambers contract together.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"where-the-structures-lie-and-why-sequence-matters\">Where the structures lie and why sequence matters</h2>\n<p>The <strong>SA node</strong> is located in the right atrial wall near the superior vena cava opening; the <strong>AV node</strong> lies low in the interatrial septum near the coronary sinus and septal tricuspid leaflet. Impulses spread through atrial myocardium and preferential atrial conduction routes. The <strong>Bachmann bundle</strong> supports rapid conduction to the left atrium. Older teaching names the anterior, middle (Wenckebach) and posterior (Thorel) internodal tracts; treat these as traditional descriptions rather than three universally discrete insulated bundles.</p>\n<p>From the AV node, impulses traverse the <strong>bundle of His</strong>, right and left bundle branches and rapidly conducting <strong>Purkinje fibres</strong>. The fibrous cardiac skeleton electrically separates most atrial from ventricular working myocardium; the AV conduction pathway is normally the connection. Slow AV nodal conduction creates a delay so atrial contraction occurs before ventricular systole. Rapid Purkinje propagation promotes coordinated ventricular activation from apical/endocardial regions toward the base.</p>\n<h2 id=\"automaticity-and-different-pacemaker-rates\">Automaticity and different pacemaker rates</h2>\n<p>Many specialised conducting cells can depolarise spontaneously. The SA node normally dominates because it fires faster, suppressing slower subsidiary pacemakers (<strong>overdrive suppression</strong>). If SA impulses fail to reach the ventricles, a junctional or ventricular escape rhythm may provide a slower backup; this is not equivalent to healthy coordinated sinus rhythm.</p>\n<p>Sympathetic norepinephrine acting on <strong>β₁ receptors</strong> increases cAMP-dependent pacemaker currents and usually steepens phase 4, raising heart rate (<strong>positive chronotropy</strong>) and AV conduction (<strong>positive dromotropy</strong>). Vagal acetylcholine acting at <strong>M₂ receptors</strong> reduces cAMP and increases K⁺ conductance, slowing SA nodal discharge and AV nodal conduction.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> The purpose of AV nodal delay is not to strengthen the electrical impulse. It preserves timing: the atria empty before the ventricles contract.</p>\n","plain":" Cardiac Conduction and Automaticity \n The normal pathway \n The  sinoatrial (SA) node , near the superior vena cava–right atrial junction, usually sets the rhythm. Depolarization spreads through the atria, reaches the  atrioventricular (AV) node , passes through the  bundle of His , right and left bundle branches, then the  Purkinje network  to activate ventricular myocardium. \n The AV node delays conduction. This gives the atria time to contract before ventricular systole, assisting filling. Purkinje fibres conduct rapidly so the ventricles activate efficiently, usually from apex towards base. \n Why does the SA node fire on its own? \n SA nodal cells do not have a stable resting membrane potential. During  phase 4 , net inward depolarizing current includes the hyperpolarization-activated  funny current (I_f) , contributions from Ca²⁺ currents, and changing K⁺ conductance. Reaching threshold opens predominantly  L-type Ca²⁺ channels  for phase 0; K⁺ efflux mediates repolarization. \n Do  not  confuse this nodal Ca²⁺ upstroke with the fast Na⁺ upstroke of ventricular working muscle. \n Autonomic control \n Sympathetic noradrenaline acting at  β₁ receptors  steepens pacemaker depolarization and increases heart rate. Parasympathetic acetylcholine acting at  M₂ receptors  slows nodal firing and AV conduction, partly through GIRK K⁺ channels and reduced cAMP. \n If the SA node fails, slower subsidiary pacemakers may take over. AV conduction disturbances may produce delayed conduction or dropped ventricular beats; interpreting them requires the ECG. \n  Check:  Why does AV nodal delay improve cardiac performance? \n  Answer It allows atrial depolarization/contraction to occur before ventricular activation, improving ventricular filling rather than making both chambers contract together. \n\n Where the structures lie and why sequence matters \n The  SA node  is located in the right atrial wall near the superior vena cava opening; the  AV node  lies low in the interatrial septum near the coronary sinus and septal tricuspid leaflet. Impulses spread through atrial myocardium and preferential atrial conduction routes. The  Bachmann bundle  supports rapid conduction to the left atrium. Older teaching names the anterior, middle (Wenckebach) and posterior (Thorel) internodal tracts; treat these as traditional descriptions rather than three universally discrete insulated bundles. \n From the AV node, impulses traverse the  bundle of His , right and left bundle branches and rapidly conducting  Purkinje fibres . The fibrous cardiac skeleton electrically separates most atrial from ventricular working myocardium; the AV conduction pathway is normally the connection. Slow AV nodal conduction creates a delay so atrial contraction occurs before ventricular systole. Rapid Purkinje propagation promotes coordinated ventricular activation from apical/endocardial regions toward the base. \n Automaticity and different pacemaker rates \n Many specialised conducting cells can depolarise spontaneously. The SA node normally dominates because it fires faster, suppressing slower subsidiary pacemakers ( overdrive suppression ). If SA impulses fail to reach the ventricles, a junctional or ventricular escape rhythm may provide a slower backup; this is not equivalent to healthy coordinated sinus rhythm. \n Sympathetic norepinephrine acting on  β₁ receptors  increases cAMP-dependent pacemaker currents and usually steepens phase 4, raising heart rate ( positive chronotropy ) and AV conduction ( positive dromotropy ). Vagal acetylcholine acting at  M₂ receptors  reduces cAMP and increases K⁺ conductance, slowing SA nodal discharge and AV nodal conduction. \n  Practice:  The purpose of AV nodal delay is not to strengthen the electrical impulse. It preserves timing: the atria empty before the ventricles contract. \n"},{"slug":"cardiac-cycle","title":"The Cardiac Cycle","group":"Heart","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"11-21","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"track-pressures-and-valves-not-memorized-lists","title":"Track pressures and valves, not memorized lists"},{"id":"sequence-of-phases","title":"Sequence of phases"},{"id":"volumes-and-relationships","title":"Volumes and relationships"},{"id":"one-useful-clinical-connection","title":"One useful clinical connection"},{"id":"read-the-wiggers-diagram-as-events-not-curves-to-memorise","title":"Read the Wiggers diagram as events, not curves to memorise"},{"id":"use-the-pressure-volume-loop-to-reason-through-disease","title":"Use the pressure–volume loop to reason through disease"}],"html":"<h1>The Cardiac Cycle</h1>\n<h2 id=\"track-pressures-and-valves-not-memorized-lists\">Track pressures and valves, not memorized lists</h2>\n<p>One heartbeat repeats phases of filling, pressure generation, ejection and relaxation. A valve opens only when pressure <strong>upstream exceeds downstream</strong>; it closes when the gradient reverses. Think of the Wiggers diagram as simultaneous traces of atrial, ventricular and aortic pressure, ventricular volume, ECG and heart sounds.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sequence-of-phases\">Sequence of phases</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Ventricular filling:</strong> AV valves open; semilunar valves closed. Most filling is passive in a resting healthy adult.</li>\n<li><strong>Atrial systole:</strong> the atria add the final volume (often around 10–30% at rest, variable with age, rate and ventricular stiffness).</li>\n<li><strong>Isovolumetric contraction:</strong> ventricular depolarization precedes contraction; ventricular pressure rises, AV valves close (<strong>S₁</strong>) and semilunar valves remain closed. Volume does not change because all valves are closed.</li>\n<li><strong>Ejection:</strong> when ventricular pressure exceeds aortic/pulmonary pressure, semilunar valves open. <strong>Rapid ejection</strong> is followed by reduced ejection; ventricular volume falls.</li>\n<li><strong>Isovolumetric relaxation:</strong> when arterial pressure exceeds ventricular pressure, semilunar valves close (<strong>S₂</strong>). All valves are shut while ventricular pressure drops at constant end-systolic volume.</li>\n<li><strong>Filling resumes</strong> once ventricular pressure falls below atrial pressure.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"volumes-and-relationships\">Volumes and relationships</h2>\n<p><strong>End-diastolic volume (EDV)</strong> is the volume before ejection; <strong>end-systolic volume (ESV)</strong> remains afterward. <strong>Stroke volume (SV) = EDV − ESV</strong>. <strong>Ejection fraction (EF) = SV/EDV × 100%</strong>. For example, EDV 120 mL and ESV 50 mL produce SV 70 mL and EF about 58%.</p>\n<p>Increasing heart rate shortens diastole disproportionately, so very rapid rhythms reduce filling and may compromise coronary perfusion.</p>\n<h2 id=\"one-useful-clinical-connection\">One useful clinical connection</h2>\n<p>During physiological inspiration, right ventricular filling/ejection can last longer, delaying pulmonary valve closure relative to aortic valve closure: <strong>physiological splitting of S₂</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Check:</strong> Ventricular pressure rises rapidly, but ventricular volume stays constant. Which phase is this, and why?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary>Isovolumetric contraction. Both AV and semilunar valves are closed, so contraction raises pressure without ejecting blood.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"read-the-wiggers-diagram-as-events-not-curves-to-memorise\">Read the Wiggers diagram as events, not curves to memorise</h2>\n<p>The lecturer's <strong>Wiggers diagram (Circulation 1, slide 21)</strong> aligns ECG, aortic pressure, ventricular pressure, atrial pressure, ventricular volume and heart sounds. The P wave precedes atrial contraction. The QRS precedes rapid ventricular pressure rise. The <strong>first heart sound (S1)</strong> accompanies AV valve closure near the beginning of ventricular systole; the <strong>second sound (S2)</strong> accompanies semilunar closure at the end of ejection. The T wave occurs around late ventricular systole as ventricles repolarise.</p>\n<p><strong>Isovolumetric contraction:</strong> all four valves are closed; ventricular pressure rises while volume remains near end-diastolic volume (EDV). This begins when ventricular pressure exceeds atrial pressure and closes the AV valves. <strong>Rapid ejection</strong> begins once ventricular pressure exceeds aortic/pulmonary arterial pressure, followed by <strong>reduced ejection</strong>. The lecture slide labelled ejection initially slow then rapid; the typical sequence is <strong>rapid then reduced</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Isovolumetric relaxation:</strong> both sets of valves again are closed; ventricular pressure falls without a volume change. Once ventricular pressure falls below atrial pressure, AV valves open and <strong>rapid filling</strong>, <strong>diastasis</strong> (reduced filling) and <strong>atrial systole</strong> follow. Atrial contraction ordinarily adds a <strong>minority</strong> of ventricular filling at rest, but its contribution grows in some states such as increased heart rate or impaired relaxation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"use-the-pressure-volume-loop-to-reason-through-disease\">Use the pressure–volume loop to reason through disease</h2>\n<p>Loop width is <strong>stroke volume = EDV − ESV</strong>. Loop area approximates external stroke work. Increased <strong>preload</strong> with preserved function increases EDV and usually SV through Frank–Starling. Increased <strong>afterload</strong> makes the ventricle eject against a higher pressure, tending to increase ESV and reduce SV at a fixed contractile state. Increased <strong>contractility</strong> decreases ESV and shifts the end-systolic relation up/left, increasing SV.</p>\n<p>At high heart rates, <strong>diastole shortens disproportionately</strong>, reducing filling time and the time available for left coronary perfusion. Therefore extreme tachycardia can reduce SV even though HR increases.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> At the instant just after AV valves close but before aortic opening, what changes? <strong>Pressure rises, volume does not</strong>: isovolumetric contraction.</p>\n","plain":" The Cardiac Cycle \n Track pressures and valves, not memorized lists \n One heartbeat repeats phases of filling, pressure generation, ejection and relaxation. A valve opens only when pressure  upstream exceeds downstream ; it closes when the gradient reverses. Think of the Wiggers diagram as simultaneous traces of atrial, ventricular and aortic pressure, ventricular volume, ECG and heart sounds. \n Sequence of phases \n \n  Ventricular filling:  AV valves open; semilunar valves closed. Most filling is passive in a resting healthy adult. \n  Atrial systole:  the atria add the final volume (often around 10–30% at rest, variable with age, rate and ventricular stiffness). \n  Isovolumetric contraction:  ventricular depolarization precedes contraction; ventricular pressure rises, AV valves close ( S₁ ) and semilunar valves remain closed. Volume does not change because all valves are closed. \n  Ejection:  when ventricular pressure exceeds aortic/pulmonary pressure, semilunar valves open.  Rapid ejection  is followed by reduced ejection; ventricular volume falls. \n  Isovolumetric relaxation:  when arterial pressure exceeds ventricular pressure, semilunar valves close ( S₂ ). All valves are shut while ventricular pressure drops at constant end-systolic volume. \n  Filling resumes  once ventricular pressure falls below atrial pressure. \n \n Volumes and relationships \n  End-diastolic volume (EDV)  is the volume before ejection;  end-systolic volume (ESV)  remains afterward.  Stroke volume (SV) = EDV − ESV .  Ejection fraction (EF) = SV/EDV × 100% . For example, EDV 120 mL and ESV 50 mL produce SV 70 mL and EF about 58%. \n Increasing heart rate shortens diastole disproportionately, so very rapid rhythms reduce filling and may compromise coronary perfusion. \n One useful clinical connection \n During physiological inspiration, right ventricular filling/ejection can last longer, delaying pulmonary valve closure relative to aortic valve closure:  physiological splitting of S₂ . \n  Check:  Ventricular pressure rises rapidly, but ventricular volume stays constant. Which phase is this, and why? \n  Answer Isovolumetric contraction. Both AV and semilunar valves are closed, so contraction raises pressure without ejecting blood. \n\n Read the Wiggers diagram as events, not curves to memorise \n The lecturer's  Wiggers diagram (Circulation 1, slide 21)  aligns ECG, aortic pressure, ventricular pressure, atrial pressure, ventricular volume and heart sounds. The P wave precedes atrial contraction. The QRS precedes rapid ventricular pressure rise. The  first heart sound (S1)  accompanies AV valve closure near the beginning of ventricular systole; the  second sound (S2)  accompanies semilunar closure at the end of ejection. The T wave occurs around late ventricular systole as ventricles repolarise. \n  Isovolumetric contraction:  all four valves are closed; ventricular pressure rises while volume remains near end-diastolic volume (EDV). This begins when ventricular pressure exceeds atrial pressure and closes the AV valves.  Rapid ejection  begins once ventricular pressure exceeds aortic/pulmonary arterial pressure, followed by  reduced ejection . The lecture slide labelled ejection initially slow then rapid; the typical sequence is  rapid then reduced . \n  Isovolumetric relaxation:  both sets of valves again are closed; ventricular pressure falls without a volume change. Once ventricular pressure falls below atrial pressure, AV valves open and  rapid filling ,  diastasis  (reduced filling) and  atrial systole  follow. Atrial contraction ordinarily adds a  minority  of ventricular filling at rest, but its contribution grows in some states such as increased heart rate or impaired relaxation. \n Use the pressure–volume loop to reason through disease \n Loop width is  stroke volume = EDV − ESV . Loop area approximates external stroke work. Increased  preload  with preserved function increases EDV and usually SV through Frank–Starling. Increased  afterload  makes the ventricle eject against a higher pressure, tending to increase ESV and reduce SV at a fixed contractile state. Increased  contractility  decreases ESV and shifts the end-systolic relation up/left, increasing SV. \n At high heart rates,  diastole shortens disproportionately , reducing filling time and the time available for left coronary perfusion. Therefore extreme tachycardia can reduce SV even though HR increases. \n  Practice:  At the instant just after AV valves close but before aortic opening, what changes?  Pressure rises, volume does not : isovolumetric contraction. \n"},{"slug":"heart-sounds","title":"Heart Sounds and Murmurs","group":"Heart","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"18-19 and 27-34","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"sounds-reflect-vibration-around-valve-events","title":"Sounds reflect vibration around valve events"},{"id":"how-to-reason-through-a-murmur","title":"How to reason through a murmur"},{"id":"inspiration-and-the-second-sound","title":"Inspiration and the second sound"},{"id":"four-heart-sounds-and-the-physiological-split","title":"Four heart sounds and the physiological split"},{"id":"predict-murmur-timing-from-pressure-gradients","title":"Predict murmur timing from pressure gradients"},{"id":"why-bruits-arise-beyond-heart-valves","title":"Why bruits arise beyond heart valves"}],"html":"<h1>Heart Sounds and Murmurs</h1>\n<h2 id=\"sounds-reflect-vibration-around-valve-events\">Sounds reflect vibration around valve events</h2>\n<p><strong>S₁</strong> is associated chiefly with closure of mitral and tricuspid valves as ventricular systole begins. <strong>S₂</strong> arises chiefly from aortic and pulmonary valve closure near its end. The sounds come from vibration of cardiac structures and blood associated with these events—not simply leaflets slapping together.</p>\n<p>An <strong>S₃</strong> occurs during rapid early diastolic filling; it may be physiological in younger people, but in an older symptomatic patient can reflect volume overload or ventricular dysfunction. An <strong>S₄</strong> is associated with atrial contraction against a stiff ventricle; it is absent in atrial fibrillation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"how-to-reason-through-a-murmur\">How to reason through a murmur</h2>\n<p>A murmur reflects audible turbulent or otherwise disturbed flow. Ask <strong>which valve</strong>, <strong>whether flow is forward or backward</strong>, and <strong>whether that flow happens during systole or diastole</strong>.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Lesion</th>\n  <th>Flow producing sound</th>\n  <th>Timing</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>Aortic stenosis</td>\n  <td>Ejection through narrowed aortic valve</td>\n  <td>Systolic</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Pulmonary stenosis</td>\n  <td>Ejection through narrowed pulmonary valve</td>\n  <td>Systolic</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Mitral regurgitation</td>\n  <td>LV to LA when LV contracts</td>\n  <td>Systolic</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Tricuspid regurgitation</td>\n  <td>RV to RA during systole</td>\n  <td>Systolic</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Aortic regurgitation</td>\n  <td>Aorta back into LV after systole</td>\n  <td>Diastolic</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Mitral stenosis</td>\n  <td>LA to LV through narrowed valve during filling</td>\n  <td>Diastolic</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Not every murmur means structural valve disease: high-flow states such as anaemia may produce functional murmurs. <strong>Bruits</strong> are analogous vascular sounds produced by disturbed blood flow.</p>\n<h2 id=\"inspiration-and-the-second-sound\">Inspiration and the second sound</h2>\n<p>Inspiration increases systemic venous return to the right ventricle and can delay P₂. This explains normal inspiratory widening of the aortic–pulmonary components of S₂.</p>\n<p><strong>Check:</strong> Why can mitral regurgitation produce a systolic murmur, but mitral stenosis a diastolic one?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary>During systole the LV normally contracts with the mitral valve closed, so incompetence permits backward flow. During diastole blood normally flows from LA into LV, so a narrowed mitral valve obstructs forward filling then.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"four-heart-sounds-and-the-physiological-split\">Four heart sounds and the physiological split</h2>\n<p><strong>S1</strong> chiefly follows mitral and tricuspid closure as ventricular contraction begins. <strong>S2</strong> chiefly follows aortic and pulmonary valve closure as ejection ends. <strong>S3</strong> occurs in early rapid filling and can be physiological in young people; in older adults it may suggest volume overload or heart failure. <strong>S4</strong> is associated with atrial contraction into a relatively stiff ventricle and is generally pathological in adults; it cannot occur without effective atrial contraction.</p>\n<p>During inspiration, increased systemic venous return tends to prolong right ventricular ejection, while changes in pulmonary venous return can advance left-sided closure. Therefore <strong>A2 precedes P2 by a larger interval</strong>: physiological splitting of S2. The two components are not guaranteed to close simultaneously during expiration.</p>\n<h2 id=\"predict-murmur-timing-from-pressure-gradients\">Predict murmur timing from pressure gradients</h2>\n<p>A stenotic <strong>aortic or pulmonary valve</strong> obstructs forward ejection during <strong>systole</strong>, producing a systolic ejection murmur. A regurgitant <strong>mitral or tricuspid valve</strong> leaks backward during ventricular <strong>systole</strong>, producing a systolic regurgitant murmur. A stenotic <strong>mitral or tricuspid valve</strong> obstructs ventricular filling during <strong>diastole</strong>; a regurgitant <strong>aortic or pulmonary valve</strong> leaks back into the ventricle during <strong>diastole</strong>. Murmur intensity alone does not consistently measure lesion severity.</p>\n<p>Standard auscultation areas: <strong>aortic</strong> second right intercostal space parasternal; <strong>pulmonary</strong> second left intercostal space; <strong>tricuspid</strong> left lower sternal border; <strong>mitral</strong> apex around fifth left intercostal space near the midclavicular line. These are optimal listening areas, <strong>not the anatomical positions of the valves</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"why-bruits-arise-beyond-heart-valves\">Why bruits arise beyond heart valves</h2>\n<p>Turbulent blood flow can generate vascular sounds over stenotic arteries, arteriovenous fistulas and aneurysms. Higher flow or lower viscosity (e.g. anaemia) can increase turbulence even without valve damage. A patent ductus arteriosus may produce a continuous murmur because aortic pressure exceeds pulmonary arterial pressure throughout much of the cardiac cycle.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Which valve lesion can cause a diastolic murmur with backward blood flow into the left ventricle? <strong>Aortic regurgitation</strong>, because the closed aortic valve fails to prevent aortic-to-LV flow during diastole.</p>\n","plain":" Heart Sounds and Murmurs \n Sounds reflect vibration around valve events \n  S₁  is associated chiefly with closure of mitral and tricuspid valves as ventricular systole begins.  S₂  arises chiefly from aortic and pulmonary valve closure near its end. The sounds come from vibration of cardiac structures and blood associated with these events—not simply leaflets slapping together. \n An  S₃  occurs during rapid early diastolic filling; it may be physiological in younger people, but in an older symptomatic patient can reflect volume overload or ventricular dysfunction. An  S₄  is associated with atrial contraction against a stiff ventricle; it is absent in atrial fibrillation. \n How to reason through a murmur \n A murmur reflects audible turbulent or otherwise disturbed flow. Ask  which valve ,  whether flow is forward or backward , and  whether that flow happens during systole or diastole . \n \n \n \n   Lesion \n   Flow producing sound \n   Timing \n \n \n \n \n   Aortic stenosis \n   Ejection through narrowed aortic valve \n   Systolic \n \n \n   Pulmonary stenosis \n   Ejection through narrowed pulmonary valve \n   Systolic \n \n \n   Mitral regurgitation \n   LV to LA when LV contracts \n   Systolic \n \n \n   Tricuspid regurgitation \n   RV to RA during systole \n   Systolic \n \n \n   Aortic regurgitation \n   Aorta back into LV after systole \n   Diastolic \n \n \n   Mitral stenosis \n   LA to LV through narrowed valve during filling \n   Diastolic \n \n \n \n Not every murmur means structural valve disease: high-flow states such as anaemia may produce functional murmurs.  Bruits  are analogous vascular sounds produced by disturbed blood flow. \n Inspiration and the second sound \n Inspiration increases systemic venous return to the right ventricle and can delay P₂. This explains normal inspiratory widening of the aortic–pulmonary components of S₂. \n  Check:  Why can mitral regurgitation produce a systolic murmur, but mitral stenosis a diastolic one? \n  Answer During systole the LV normally contracts with the mitral valve closed, so incompetence permits backward flow. During diastole blood normally flows from LA into LV, so a narrowed mitral valve obstructs forward filling then. \n\n Four heart sounds and the physiological split \n  S1  chiefly follows mitral and tricuspid closure as ventricular contraction begins.  S2  chiefly follows aortic and pulmonary valve closure as ejection ends.  S3  occurs in early rapid filling and can be physiological in young people; in older adults it may suggest volume overload or heart failure.  S4  is associated with atrial contraction into a relatively stiff ventricle and is generally pathological in adults; it cannot occur without effective atrial contraction. \n During inspiration, increased systemic venous return tends to prolong right ventricular ejection, while changes in pulmonary venous return can advance left-sided closure. Therefore  A2 precedes P2 by a larger interval : physiological splitting of S2. The two components are not guaranteed to close simultaneously during expiration. \n Predict murmur timing from pressure gradients \n A stenotic  aortic or pulmonary valve  obstructs forward ejection during  systole , producing a systolic ejection murmur. A regurgitant  mitral or tricuspid valve  leaks backward during ventricular  systole , producing a systolic regurgitant murmur. A stenotic  mitral or tricuspid valve  obstructs ventricular filling during  diastole ; a regurgitant  aortic or pulmonary valve  leaks back into the ventricle during  diastole . Murmur intensity alone does not consistently measure lesion severity. \n Standard auscultation areas:  aortic  second right intercostal space parasternal;  pulmonary  second left intercostal space;  tricuspid  left lower sternal border;  mitral  apex around fifth left intercostal space near the midclavicular line. These are optimal listening areas,  not the anatomical positions of the valves . \n Why bruits arise beyond heart valves \n Turbulent blood flow can generate vascular sounds over stenotic arteries, arteriovenous fistulas and aneurysms. Higher flow or lower viscosity (e.g. anaemia) can increase turbulence even without valve damage. A patent ductus arteriosus may produce a continuous murmur because aortic pressure exceeds pulmonary arterial pressure throughout much of the cardiac cycle. \n  Practice:  Which valve lesion can cause a diastolic murmur with backward blood flow into the left ventricle?  Aortic regurgitation , because the closed aortic valve fails to prevent aortic-to-LV flow during diastole. \n"},{"slug":"ecg","title":"Electrocardiography and Basic Rhythms","group":"Heart","source":"Lecture 7 ECG 2.pdf","pages":"1-14; extra diagrams Circulation 3 35-38","coverage":"lecturer supplemented","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"what-the-tracing-measures","title":"What the tracing measures"},{"id":"waves-and-intervals","title":"Waves and intervals"},{"id":"ecg-paper-and-heart-rate-calculation","title":"ECG paper and heart-rate calculation"},{"id":"basic-conduction-problems","title":"Basic conduction problems"},{"id":"understand-limb-leads-instead-of-memorising-letter-names","title":"Understand limb leads instead of memorising letter names"},{"id":"distinguish-waves-segments-and-intervals","title":"Distinguish waves, segments and intervals"},{"id":"work-a-paper-speed-example","title":"Work a paper-speed example"}],"html":"<h1>Electrocardiography and Basic Rhythms</h1>\n<h2 id=\"what-the-tracing-measures\">What the tracing measures</h2>\n<p>The surface <strong>electrocardiogram (ECG)</strong> records voltage differences generated as cardiac electrical activation and recovery spread through the body. It does not directly measure contraction, stroke volume or blood pressure.</p>\n<p>A standard 12-lead ECG uses electrodes to produce <strong>12 views</strong>: bipolar limb leads I, II, III; augmented limb leads aVR, aVL, aVF; and chest leads V₁–V₆. Each lead views depolarization along a different direction. A depolarization vector generally travelling toward a positive recording electrode gives a positive deflection.</p>\n<h2 id=\"waves-and-intervals\">Waves and intervals</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Feature</th>\n  <th>Meaning</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>P wave</td>\n  <td>Atrial depolarization</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>PR interval</td>\n  <td>Onset of atrial to onset of ventricular depolarization, normally about 0.12–0.20 s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>QRS complex</td>\n  <td>Ventricular depolarization, typically less than 0.12 s</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>ST segment</td>\n  <td>Period when much of ventricular myocardium is depolarized; significant deviation may indicate injury/ischemia in context</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>T wave</td>\n  <td>Ventricular repolarization</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>QT interval</td>\n  <td>Ventricular depolarization through repolarization; varies with heart rate</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Atrial repolarization is usually obscured by the larger QRS complex. The occasional U wave has more than one proposed physiological origin.</p>\n<h2 id=\"ecg-paper-and-heart-rate-calculation\">ECG paper and heart-rate calculation</h2>\n<p>At <strong>25 mm/s</strong>, each 1 mm horizontal square is <strong>0.04 s</strong>, each 5 mm large square <strong>0.20 s</strong>. Standard gain is <strong>10 mm/mV</strong>; thus 1 small vertical square is <strong>0.1 mV</strong>.</p>\n<p>For a regular rhythm, heart rate ≈ <strong>300 ÷ number of large squares between R waves</strong>, or <strong>1500 ÷ small squares</strong>. If R–R separation is 4 large squares, HR ≈ 75 beats/min. Irregular rhythms require a longer sampled time window and appropriate averaging.</p>\n<h2 id=\"basic-conduction-problems\">Basic conduction problems</h2>\n<p><strong>First-degree AV block</strong> has prolonged PR without dropped QRS complexes. <strong>Second-degree</strong> block intermittently fails to conduct atrial impulses. <strong>Third-degree</strong> block involves AV dissociation. These illustrate conduction principles and are supplementary to the supplied introductory ECG slides.</p>\n<p><strong>Check:</strong> A regular trace has 20 small squares between R waves at 25 mm/s. What is the rate?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary>1500 ÷ 20 = 75 beats/min. Each square is 0.04 s, so 20 squares are 0.8 s/beat; 60 ÷ 0.8 = 75.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"understand-limb-leads-instead-of-memorising-letter-names\">Understand limb leads instead of memorising letter names</h2>\n<p><strong>Lead I = left arm (+) − right arm (−)</strong>. <strong>Lead II = left leg (+) − right arm (−)</strong>. <strong>Lead III = left leg (+) − left arm (−)</strong>. This yields <strong>Einthoven's law: II = I + III</strong> for simultaneously recorded limb lead voltages. The augmented unipolar limb leads are <strong>aVR</strong> (views from right arm), <strong>aVL</strong> (left arm) and <strong>aVF</strong> (foot/left leg). The right-leg electrode ordinarily serves as a ground/reference, not a 12-lead recording viewpoint.</p>\n<p>The six chest electrodes sample the horizontal plane: <strong>V1</strong> fourth right intercostal space at sternal border; <strong>V2</strong> fourth left intercostal space; <strong>V4</strong> fifth left intercostal space at midclavicular line; <strong>V3</strong> halfway between V2 and V4; <strong>V5</strong> anterior axillary line level with V4; <strong>V6</strong> midaxillary line level with V4. In practice position V4 before V3. These locations matter because placement errors can change the apparent tracing.</p>\n<h2 id=\"distinguish-waves-segments-and-intervals\">Distinguish waves, segments and intervals</h2>\n<p>The <strong>PR interval</strong> spans P onset to QRS onset and includes atrial depolarisation plus AV transmission; PR prolongation may indicate slowed AV conduction. The <strong>PR segment</strong> is the relatively flat period after the P wave before QRS. <strong>QRS</strong> reflects ventricular depolarisation; simultaneous atrial repolarisation is usually concealed. The <strong>ST segment</strong> lies between the QRS and T wave and roughly corresponds to a period during which much ventricular myocardium remains depolarised. The <strong>QT interval</strong> includes ventricular depolarisation and repolarisation.</p>\n<p>A <strong>corrected QT (QTc)</strong> adjusts for rate. The Bazett approximation is <strong>QTc = QT/√RR</strong>, with QT and RR expressed in seconds. It can overcorrect at high rates and undercorrect at low rates, so interpretation needs context; do not treat an uncorrected QT duration as universally normal at every HR. The <strong>U wave</strong>, when visible, has debated origins.</p>\n<h2 id=\"work-a-paper-speed-example\">Work a paper-speed example</h2>\n<p>At <strong>25 mm/s</strong>, each small horizontal square = <strong>0.04 seconds</strong>, each large square = <strong>0.20 seconds</strong>. At <strong>10 mm/mV</strong>, each small vertical square = <strong>0.1 mV</strong>. A regular rhythm with R waves separated by <strong>22 small squares</strong> has HR ≈ <strong>1500/22 = 68 beats/min</strong> (the lecturer's example). For irregular rhythms, counting complexes over a longer strip gives a more useful average than a single R–R interval.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Why is the ECG not equivalent to a pressure or contraction graph? It records <strong>electrical potentials</strong>, which precede and coordinate the mechanical contraction but are not the mechanical event itself.</p>\n","plain":" Electrocardiography and Basic Rhythms \n What the tracing measures \n The surface  electrocardiogram (ECG)  records voltage differences generated as cardiac electrical activation and recovery spread through the body. It does not directly measure contraction, stroke volume or blood pressure. \n A standard 12-lead ECG uses electrodes to produce  12 views : bipolar limb leads I, II, III; augmented limb leads aVR, aVL, aVF; and chest leads V₁–V₆. Each lead views depolarization along a different direction. A depolarization vector generally travelling toward a positive recording electrode gives a positive deflection. \n Waves and intervals \n \n \n \n   Feature \n   Meaning \n \n \n \n \n   P wave \n   Atrial depolarization \n \n \n   PR interval \n   Onset of atrial to onset of ventricular depolarization, normally about 0.12–0.20 s \n \n \n   QRS complex \n   Ventricular depolarization, typically less than 0.12 s \n \n \n   ST segment \n   Period when much of ventricular myocardium is depolarized; significant deviation may indicate injury/ischemia in context \n \n \n   T wave \n   Ventricular repolarization \n \n \n   QT interval \n   Ventricular depolarization through repolarization; varies with heart rate \n \n \n \n Atrial repolarization is usually obscured by the larger QRS complex. The occasional U wave has more than one proposed physiological origin. \n ECG paper and heart-rate calculation \n At  25 mm/s , each 1 mm horizontal square is  0.04 s , each 5 mm large square  0.20 s . Standard gain is  10 mm/mV ; thus 1 small vertical square is  0.1 mV . \n For a regular rhythm, heart rate ≈  300 ÷ number of large squares between R waves , or  1500 ÷ small squares . If R–R separation is 4 large squares, HR ≈ 75 beats/min. Irregular rhythms require a longer sampled time window and appropriate averaging. \n Basic conduction problems \n  First-degree AV block  has prolonged PR without dropped QRS complexes.  Second-degree  block intermittently fails to conduct atrial impulses.  Third-degree  block involves AV dissociation. These illustrate conduction principles and are supplementary to the supplied introductory ECG slides. \n  Check:  A regular trace has 20 small squares between R waves at 25 mm/s. What is the rate? \n  Answer 1500 ÷ 20 = 75 beats/min. Each square is 0.04 s, so 20 squares are 0.8 s/beat; 60 ÷ 0.8 = 75. \n\n Understand limb leads instead of memorising letter names \n  Lead I = left arm (+) − right arm (−) .  Lead II = left leg (+) − right arm (−) .  Lead III = left leg (+) − left arm (−) . This yields  Einthoven's law: II = I + III  for simultaneously recorded limb lead voltages. The augmented unipolar limb leads are  aVR  (views from right arm),  aVL  (left arm) and  aVF  (foot/left leg). The right-leg electrode ordinarily serves as a ground/reference, not a 12-lead recording viewpoint. \n The six chest electrodes sample the horizontal plane:  V1  fourth right intercostal space at sternal border;  V2  fourth left intercostal space;  V4  fifth left intercostal space at midclavicular line;  V3  halfway between V2 and V4;  V5  anterior axillary line level with V4;  V6  midaxillary line level with V4. In practice position V4 before V3. These locations matter because placement errors can change the apparent tracing. \n Distinguish waves, segments and intervals \n The  PR interval  spans P onset to QRS onset and includes atrial depolarisation plus AV transmission; PR prolongation may indicate slowed AV conduction. The  PR segment  is the relatively flat period after the P wave before QRS.  QRS  reflects ventricular depolarisation; simultaneous atrial repolarisation is usually concealed. The  ST segment  lies between the QRS and T wave and roughly corresponds to a period during which much ventricular myocardium remains depolarised. The  QT interval  includes ventricular depolarisation and repolarisation. \n A  corrected QT (QTc)  adjusts for rate. The Bazett approximation is  QTc = QT/√RR , with QT and RR expressed in seconds. It can overcorrect at high rates and undercorrect at low rates, so interpretation needs context; do not treat an uncorrected QT duration as universally normal at every HR. The  U wave , when visible, has debated origins. \n Work a paper-speed example \n At  25 mm/s , each small horizontal square =  0.04 seconds , each large square =  0.20 seconds . At  10 mm/mV , each small vertical square =  0.1 mV . A regular rhythm with R waves separated by  22 small squares  has HR ≈  1500/22 = 68 beats/min  (the lecturer's example). For irregular rhythms, counting complexes over a longer strip gives a more useful average than a single R–R interval. \n  Practice:  Why is the ECG not equivalent to a pressure or contraction graph? It records  electrical potentials , which precede and coordinate the mechanical contraction but are not the mechanical event itself. \n"},{"slug":"cardiac-output","title":"Cardiac Output and Frank-Starling Law","group":"Vessels","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"35-51","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"definitions-and-calculations","title":"Definitions and calculations"},{"id":"what-determines-stroke-volume","title":"What determines stroke volume?"},{"id":"frank-starling-why-filling-affects-ejection","title":"Frank–Starling: why filling affects ejection"},{"id":"sympathetic-stimulation","title":"Sympathetic stimulation"},{"id":"frank-starling-curves-are-families-not-one-fixed-line","title":"Frank–Starling curves are families, not one fixed line"},{"id":"what-changes-edv-and-what-changes-contractility","title":"What changes EDV and what changes contractility?"}],"html":"<h1>Cardiac Output and the Frank–Starling Mechanism</h1>\n<h2 id=\"definitions-and-calculations\">Definitions and calculations</h2>\n<p><strong>Cardiac output (CO)</strong> is blood pumped by one ventricle per minute: <strong>CO = heart rate × stroke volume</strong>. At HR 70/min and SV 70 mL, CO ≈ 4.9 L/min. The <strong>cardiac index</strong> expresses output per body-surface area. Over time the outputs of the right and left ventricles are essentially equal.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-determines-stroke-volume\">What determines stroke volume?</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Preload</strong>: initial ventricular fibre stretch before contraction, approximated clinically by end-diastolic volume/pressure (with limitations).</li>\n<li><strong>Contractility (inotropy)</strong>: intrinsic ability to develop force independent of changes in preload and afterload.</li>\n<li><strong>Afterload</strong>: load opposing ejection, related to arterial pressure, vascular impedance and ventricular wall stress.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"frank-starling-why-filling-affects-ejection\">Frank–Starling: why filling affects ejection</h2>\n<p>Within physiological limits, more ventricular filling stretches cardiac fibres, improving force generation and stroke volume. This helps the heart match output to venous return. On a plot of stroke volume versus end-diastolic volume, increased contractility shifts the curve <strong>upward</strong>; reduced contractility shifts it <strong>downward</strong>. Do not treat changes in contractility as simply the same thing as more preload.</p>\n<h2 id=\"sympathetic-stimulation\">Sympathetic stimulation</h2>\n<p>β₁-adrenoceptor signalling increases pacemaker activity and intracellular Ca²⁺ cycling, producing positive <strong>chronotropy</strong> (higher HR) and positive <strong>inotropy</strong> (stronger contraction). Extreme tachycardia, however, shortens filling time and can <strong>reduce</strong> SV enough to offset the rise in HR.</p>\n<p>Exercise often increases CO via both HR and SV. Heart failure may blunt the ability to increase SV despite high filling pressures.</p>\n<p><strong>Check:</strong> Venous return falls sharply but HR rises. Must cardiac output increase?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary>No. CO = HR × SV. The fall in preload can markedly lower SV; compensatory tachycardia may be insufficient and very high HR can further shorten filling.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"frank-starling-curves-are-families-not-one-fixed-line\">Frank–Starling curves are families, not one fixed line</h2>\n<p>The diagrams in Circulation 1 <strong>slides 39–41</strong> plot ventricular performance against filling (often EDV or atrial pressure). As filling increases within a physiological range, increased sarcomere length improves force development, so the heart ejects more blood. <strong>Higher contractility</strong> shifts the ventricular function curve upward: for the same filling, SV/CO is greater. <strong>Heart failure</strong> usually places performance on a lower curve. Moving <strong>along</strong> a curve by changing preload is different from shifting the curve by changing inotropic state.</p>\n<p>The steep ascending limb is most useful for ordinary clinical reasoning. The descending portion sometimes drawn at extreme stretch should not be assumed to occur routinely in an intact human heart.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-changes-edv-and-what-changes-contractility\">What changes EDV and what changes contractility?</h2>\n<p>EDV rises with increased circulating volume, venoconstriction, enhanced skeletal-muscle or respiratory pumping and longer filling time, provided the ventricle can relax and expand. It falls with blood loss, standing-related venous pooling, increased intrapericardial pressure (e.g. tamponade) or reduced ventricular compliance.</p>\n<p><strong>β₁-adrenoceptor</strong> stimulation activates Gs → adenylyl cyclase → cAMP → PKA, increasing L-type Ca²⁺ current and SR Ca²⁺ cycling. This produces <strong>positive inotropy</strong> and often faster relaxation (<strong>positive lusitropy</strong>). Hypoxia, marked acidosis and myocardial damage may depress contractility. <em>Inotropy</em> (force at matched loading), <em>chronotropy</em> (rate) and <em>dromotropy</em> (conduction speed) are different effects.</p>\n<p>A rise in afterload generally makes LV ejection more difficult; cardiac oxygen demand rises with wall stress. During exercise, HR, contractility and venous return increase, and active-muscle arterioles dilate; the combination can markedly increase CO.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> If two ventricles have the same EDV but one produces a larger SV, a difference in <strong>contractility or afterload</strong>, rather than filling alone, may explain it.</p>\n","plain":" Cardiac Output and the Frank–Starling Mechanism \n Definitions and calculations \n  Cardiac output (CO)  is blood pumped by one ventricle per minute:  CO = heart rate × stroke volume . At HR 70/min and SV 70 mL, CO ≈ 4.9 L/min. The  cardiac index  expresses output per body-surface area. Over time the outputs of the right and left ventricles are essentially equal. \n What determines stroke volume? \n \n  Preload : initial ventricular fibre stretch before contraction, approximated clinically by end-diastolic volume/pressure (with limitations). \n  Contractility (inotropy) : intrinsic ability to develop force independent of changes in preload and afterload. \n  Afterload : load opposing ejection, related to arterial pressure, vascular impedance and ventricular wall stress. \n \n Frank–Starling: why filling affects ejection \n Within physiological limits, more ventricular filling stretches cardiac fibres, improving force generation and stroke volume. This helps the heart match output to venous return. On a plot of stroke volume versus end-diastolic volume, increased contractility shifts the curve  upward ; reduced contractility shifts it  downward . Do not treat changes in contractility as simply the same thing as more preload. \n Sympathetic stimulation \n β₁-adrenoceptor signalling increases pacemaker activity and intracellular Ca²⁺ cycling, producing positive  chronotropy  (higher HR) and positive  inotropy  (stronger contraction). Extreme tachycardia, however, shortens filling time and can  reduce  SV enough to offset the rise in HR. \n Exercise often increases CO via both HR and SV. Heart failure may blunt the ability to increase SV despite high filling pressures. \n  Check:  Venous return falls sharply but HR rises. Must cardiac output increase? \n  Answer No. CO = HR × SV. The fall in preload can markedly lower SV; compensatory tachycardia may be insufficient and very high HR can further shorten filling. \n\n Frank–Starling curves are families, not one fixed line \n The diagrams in Circulation 1  slides 39–41  plot ventricular performance against filling (often EDV or atrial pressure). As filling increases within a physiological range, increased sarcomere length improves force development, so the heart ejects more blood.  Higher contractility  shifts the ventricular function curve upward: for the same filling, SV/CO is greater.  Heart failure  usually places performance on a lower curve. Moving  along  a curve by changing preload is different from shifting the curve by changing inotropic state. \n The steep ascending limb is most useful for ordinary clinical reasoning. The descending portion sometimes drawn at extreme stretch should not be assumed to occur routinely in an intact human heart. \n What changes EDV and what changes contractility? \n EDV rises with increased circulating volume, venoconstriction, enhanced skeletal-muscle or respiratory pumping and longer filling time, provided the ventricle can relax and expand. It falls with blood loss, standing-related venous pooling, increased intrapericardial pressure (e.g. tamponade) or reduced ventricular compliance. \n  β₁-adrenoceptor  stimulation activates Gs → adenylyl cyclase → cAMP → PKA, increasing L-type Ca²⁺ current and SR Ca²⁺ cycling. This produces  positive inotropy  and often faster relaxation ( positive lusitropy ). Hypoxia, marked acidosis and myocardial damage may depress contractility.  Inotropy  (force at matched loading),  chronotropy  (rate) and  dromotropy  (conduction speed) are different effects. \n A rise in afterload generally makes LV ejection more difficult; cardiac oxygen demand rises with wall stress. During exercise, HR, contractility and venous return increase, and active-muscle arterioles dilate; the combination can markedly increase CO. \n  Practice:  If two ventricles have the same EDV but one produces a larger SV, a difference in  contractility or afterload , rather than filling alone, may explain it. \n"},{"slug":"co-measurement","title":"Cardiac Output Measurement and Work","group":"Vessels","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"52-54","coverage":"lecturer supplemented","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"fick-principle","title":"Fick principle"},{"id":"indicator-dilution-and-thermodilution","title":"Indicator dilution and thermodilution"},{"id":"work-performed-by-the-heart","title":"Work performed by the heart"},{"id":"distinguish-output-measurement-from-local-blood-flow-measurement","title":"Distinguish output measurement from local blood-flow measurement"},{"id":"understand-cardiac-work-and-oxygen-demand","title":"Understand cardiac work and oxygen demand"}],"html":"<h1>Measurement of Cardiac Output and Cardiac Work</h1>\n<h2 id=\"fick-principle\">Fick principle</h2>\n<p>The amount of oxygen removed from blood each minute equals blood flow times the arterial–mixed venous oxygen content difference: <strong>CO = VO₂ ÷ (CaO₂ − CvO₂)</strong>. Use consistent units: if oxygen consumption is 250 mL/min and the content difference is 50 mL/L, CO = <strong>5 L/min</strong>.</p>\n<p>The venous sample for systemic Fick cardiac output must approximate <strong>mixed venous blood</strong>, traditionally drawn from the pulmonary artery—not merely any peripheral vein. Whole-body oxygen consumption and paired oxygen content are required.</p>\n<h2 id=\"indicator-dilution-and-thermodilution\">Indicator dilution and thermodilution</h2>\n<p>An indicator injected upstream appears downstream as a concentration–time curve. After correcting for dose and units, <strong>more rapid dilution</strong> generally reflects greater flow. <strong>Thermodilution</strong> uses a temperature change instead of dye and can estimate CO through pulmonary-artery catheter measurements. Measurements have limitations in settings such as major tricuspid regurgitation or shunts.</p>\n<p><strong>Echocardiography</strong> can estimate SV from left ventricular outflow tract cross-sectional area × velocity–time integral; then CO = SV × HR.</p>\n<h2 id=\"work-performed-by-the-heart\">Work performed by the heart</h2>\n<p>Ventricular <strong>stroke work</strong> is approximately the area of the pressure–volume loop (∮P dV). A useful approximation is ejected volume times the pressure against which it ejects, though true work depends on the changing pressure throughout the beat. Myocardial oxygen demand rises with heart rate, contractility and wall stress.</p>\n<p><strong>Check:</strong> Why must the Fick method know oxygen extraction as well as oxygen consumption?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary>The same VO₂ could arise from high flow with little oxygen removed per litre or lower flow with more oxygen removed. Their ratio separates the two.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"distinguish-output-measurement-from-local-blood-flow-measurement\">Distinguish output measurement from local blood-flow measurement</h2>\n<p><strong>Fick's principle</strong> is a mass-balance relation: oxygen consumption per unit time equals blood flow × the difference between entering and leaving oxygen content. To estimate whole-body CO, the oxygen content difference must use <strong>systemic arterial and mixed venous</strong> samples. The pulmonary artery conveniently contains mixed systemic venous blood.</p>\n<p>For <strong>indicator dilution</strong>, known indicator quantity injected upstream is related to the integrated downstream concentration–time curve; with constant dose and proper correction, a smaller integrated concentration area corresponds to larger flow. <strong>Thermodilution</strong> substitutes a temperature-change curve, but mixing, valve regurgitation and shunts can introduce error.</p>\n<p>The lecturer also names three methods for <strong>regional blood flow</strong> (Circulation 1, slide 62): an <strong>electromagnetic flowmeter</strong> senses induced voltage when conducting blood moves in a magnetic field; <strong>Doppler ultrasound</strong> estimates velocity from reflected frequency shift; and <strong>plethysmography</strong> detects volume changes in a tissue or limb as an indirect index of blood flow. They are not interchangeable with measuring whole-body cardiac output.</p>\n<h2 id=\"understand-cardiac-work-and-oxygen-demand\">Understand cardiac work and oxygen demand</h2>\n<p>Approximate external <strong>stroke work ≈ mean ejection pressure × stroke volume</strong>, whereas actual LV pressure–volume loop area integrates the varying pressure through contraction/ejection. Raising <strong>afterload</strong> tends to increase pressure work and wall stress; pressure-overload lesions such as aortic stenosis can strongly increase myocardial oxygen demand. Coronary venous blood already has low oxygen content because myocardium extracts a large fraction at rest, so increased oxygen demand is met substantially by <strong>increased coronary flow</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> A patient has the same SV but a much higher aortic pressure. Pressure work and myocardial oxygen requirements generally rise, even though the amount of blood ejected per beat is unchanged.</p>\n","plain":" Measurement of Cardiac Output and Cardiac Work \n Fick principle \n The amount of oxygen removed from blood each minute equals blood flow times the arterial–mixed venous oxygen content difference:  CO = VO₂ ÷ (CaO₂ − CvO₂) . Use consistent units: if oxygen consumption is 250 mL/min and the content difference is 50 mL/L, CO =  5 L/min . \n The venous sample for systemic Fick cardiac output must approximate  mixed venous blood , traditionally drawn from the pulmonary artery—not merely any peripheral vein. Whole-body oxygen consumption and paired oxygen content are required. \n Indicator dilution and thermodilution \n An indicator injected upstream appears downstream as a concentration–time curve. After correcting for dose and units,  more rapid dilution  generally reflects greater flow.  Thermodilution  uses a temperature change instead of dye and can estimate CO through pulmonary-artery catheter measurements. Measurements have limitations in settings such as major tricuspid regurgitation or shunts. \n  Echocardiography  can estimate SV from left ventricular outflow tract cross-sectional area × velocity–time integral; then CO = SV × HR. \n Work performed by the heart \n Ventricular  stroke work  is approximately the area of the pressure–volume loop (∮P dV). A useful approximation is ejected volume times the pressure against which it ejects, though true work depends on the changing pressure throughout the beat. Myocardial oxygen demand rises with heart rate, contractility and wall stress. \n  Check:  Why must the Fick method know oxygen extraction as well as oxygen consumption? \n  Answer The same VO₂ could arise from high flow with little oxygen removed per litre or lower flow with more oxygen removed. Their ratio separates the two. \n\n Distinguish output measurement from local blood-flow measurement \n  Fick's principle  is a mass-balance relation: oxygen consumption per unit time equals blood flow × the difference between entering and leaving oxygen content. To estimate whole-body CO, the oxygen content difference must use  systemic arterial and mixed venous  samples. The pulmonary artery conveniently contains mixed systemic venous blood. \n For  indicator dilution , known indicator quantity injected upstream is related to the integrated downstream concentration–time curve; with constant dose and proper correction, a smaller integrated concentration area corresponds to larger flow.  Thermodilution  substitutes a temperature-change curve, but mixing, valve regurgitation and shunts can introduce error. \n The lecturer also names three methods for  regional blood flow  (Circulation 1, slide 62): an  electromagnetic flowmeter  senses induced voltage when conducting blood moves in a magnetic field;  Doppler ultrasound  estimates velocity from reflected frequency shift; and  plethysmography  detects volume changes in a tissue or limb as an indirect index of blood flow. They are not interchangeable with measuring whole-body cardiac output. \n Understand cardiac work and oxygen demand \n Approximate external  stroke work ≈ mean ejection pressure × stroke volume , whereas actual LV pressure–volume loop area integrates the varying pressure through contraction/ejection. Raising  afterload  tends to increase pressure work and wall stress; pressure-overload lesions such as aortic stenosis can strongly increase myocardial oxygen demand. Coronary venous blood already has low oxygen content because myocardium extracts a large fraction at rest, so increased oxygen demand is met substantially by  increased coronary flow . \n  Practice:  A patient has the same SV but a much higher aortic pressure. Pressure work and myocardial oxygen requirements generally rise, even though the amount of blood ejected per beat is unchanged. \n"},{"slug":"hemodynamics","title":"Blood Flow and Hemodynamics","group":"Vessels","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"55-74","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"flow-velocity-and-pressure-are-not-synonyms","title":"Flow, velocity and pressure are not synonyms"},{"id":"why-radius-dominates-resistance","title":"Why radius dominates resistance"},{"id":"laminar-versus-turbulent-flow","title":"Laminar versus turbulent flow"},{"id":"compliance-and-blood-storage","title":"Compliance and blood storage"},{"id":"distinguish-velocity-from-volumetric-flow-in-one-vascular-tree","title":"Distinguish velocity from volumetric flow in one vascular tree"},{"id":"where-turbulent-sounds-come-from","title":"Where turbulent sounds come from"},{"id":"blood-is-not-a-simple-fluid","title":"Blood is not a simple fluid"}],"html":"<h1>Blood Flow and Hemodynamics</h1>\n<h2 id=\"flow-velocity-and-pressure-are-not-synonyms\">Flow, velocity and pressure are not synonyms</h2>\n<p><strong>Volume flow (Q)</strong> is volume per time (mL/s); <strong>mean velocity (v)</strong> is distance per time (cm/s). They relate through <strong>Q = v × cross-sectional area</strong>. Since total cross-sectional area is greatest in systemic capillaries, average blood velocity there is low even though the entire capillary network carries the cardiac output.</p>\n<p>For a vascular segment <strong>Q = ΔP/R</strong>. Pressure falls along the circulation as energy is dissipated against resistance; arterioles account for a major pressure drop and are key resistance vessels.</p>\n<h2 id=\"why-radius-dominates-resistance\">Why radius dominates resistance</h2>\n<p>For laminar flow of a Newtonian fluid in a rigid cylindrical tube, <strong>Poiseuille's law</strong> gives <strong>R = 8ηL/(πr⁴)</strong>, where η = viscosity, L = length and r = radius. Halving radius increases resistance about <strong>16-fold</strong> under these idealised assumptions. Real blood vessels are compliant and blood is non-Newtonian, so the equation illustrates the strong effect of arteriolar calibre rather than predicting every biological flow precisely.</p>\n<h2 id=\"laminar-versus-turbulent-flow\">Laminar versus turbulent flow</h2>\n<p>Laminar flow has organised layers; axial flow is generally fastest near the centre. Turbulence becomes more likely with increased velocity, increased diameter or decreased viscosity and can generate murmurs or bruits. The <strong>Reynolds number</strong> compares inertial and viscous effects; it is a guide, not a universal cutoff in pulsatile vascular networks.</p>\n<h2 id=\"compliance-and-blood-storage\">Compliance and blood storage</h2>\n<p><strong>Compliance = change in volume/change in pressure</strong>. Veins are highly compliant <strong>capacitance vessels</strong> and hold much of the circulating blood at rest. Large elastic arteries store energy during systole and release it during diastole, smoothing flow (Windkessel effect).</p>\n<p><strong>Check:</strong> Two arterioles have the same length and viscosity. One doubles its radius. How does idealised resistance change?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary>Resistance becomes 1/16 of its original value because R is inversely proportional to r⁴. In living vessels, autoregulation and other factors also act.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"distinguish-velocity-from-volumetric-flow-in-one-vascular-tree\">Distinguish velocity from volumetric flow in one vascular tree</h2>\n<p>In the ideal series circulation, the <strong>same mean volume of blood per minute</strong> passes the aorta and combined systemic capillaries. The capillary network has an enormous <strong>total cross-sectional area</strong>, so its mean velocity is low despite unchanged total Q. Vessel branching does not automatically mean a smaller <em>total</em> flow. A pulsatile elastic aorta stores part of systolic stroke volume and recoils in diastole (<strong>Windkessel effect</strong>).</p>\n<h2 id=\"where-turbulent-sounds-come-from\">Where turbulent sounds come from</h2>\n<p>For idealised tube flow, <strong>Re = ρvD/η</strong>, where ρ is fluid density, v mean velocity, D tube diameter, and η viscosity. A narrowing can make the local velocity rise enough to favour turbulence, and a fall in viscosity in anaemia can also favour turbulence. This is why turbulent <strong>Korotkoff sounds</strong> appear as an external cuff partly compresses a brachial artery. For a particular total flow, the local constricted segment can have high velocity even though its diameter has decreased.</p>\n<p><strong>Hagen–Poiseuille:</strong> <strong>Q = (πr⁴ ΔP)/(8ηL)</strong>, so <strong>R = 8ηL/(πr⁴)</strong> under laminar, rigid, cylindrical assumptions. If radius falls from 2 mm to 1 mm and ΔP is held constant, theoretical flow becomes <strong>one-sixteenth</strong>. Real vascular beds adapt through active tone and parallel branching.</p>\n<h2 id=\"blood-is-not-a-simple-fluid\">Blood is not a simple fluid</h2>\n<p>Apparent blood viscosity depends on <strong>haematocrit</strong>, plasma proteins, red-cell deformability, temperature and vessel size/flow conditions. <strong>Polycythaemia</strong> increases viscosity and may raise circulatory workload; anaemia lowers viscosity but reduces oxygen delivery, often eliciting a higher cardiac output. In small vessels, red-cell axial migration and the Fåhræus–Lindqvist phenomenon modify apparent viscosity. The lecturer includes artery–vein compliance contrasts (slide 74): a much larger increase in <strong>venous volume</strong> can occur with only a small pressure rise, whereas arteries are relatively stiff.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Why are capillaries the slow-flow region although each capillary is narrow? Because their <strong>combined cross-sectional area</strong> is much larger than the aortic area.</p>\n","plain":" Blood Flow and Hemodynamics \n Flow, velocity and pressure are not synonyms \n  Volume flow (Q)  is volume per time (mL/s);  mean velocity (v)  is distance per time (cm/s). They relate through  Q = v × cross-sectional area . Since total cross-sectional area is greatest in systemic capillaries, average blood velocity there is low even though the entire capillary network carries the cardiac output. \n For a vascular segment  Q = ΔP/R . Pressure falls along the circulation as energy is dissipated against resistance; arterioles account for a major pressure drop and are key resistance vessels. \n Why radius dominates resistance \n For laminar flow of a Newtonian fluid in a rigid cylindrical tube,  Poiseuille's law  gives  R = 8ηL/(πr⁴) , where η = viscosity, L = length and r = radius. Halving radius increases resistance about  16-fold  under these idealised assumptions. Real blood vessels are compliant and blood is non-Newtonian, so the equation illustrates the strong effect of arteriolar calibre rather than predicting every biological flow precisely. \n Laminar versus turbulent flow \n Laminar flow has organised layers; axial flow is generally fastest near the centre. Turbulence becomes more likely with increased velocity, increased diameter or decreased viscosity and can generate murmurs or bruits. The  Reynolds number  compares inertial and viscous effects; it is a guide, not a universal cutoff in pulsatile vascular networks. \n Compliance and blood storage \n  Compliance = change in volume/change in pressure . Veins are highly compliant  capacitance vessels  and hold much of the circulating blood at rest. Large elastic arteries store energy during systole and release it during diastole, smoothing flow (Windkessel effect). \n  Check:  Two arterioles have the same length and viscosity. One doubles its radius. How does idealised resistance change? \n  Answer Resistance becomes 1/16 of its original value because R is inversely proportional to r⁴. In living vessels, autoregulation and other factors also act. \n\n Distinguish velocity from volumetric flow in one vascular tree \n In the ideal series circulation, the  same mean volume of blood per minute  passes the aorta and combined systemic capillaries. The capillary network has an enormous  total cross-sectional area , so its mean velocity is low despite unchanged total Q. Vessel branching does not automatically mean a smaller  total  flow. A pulsatile elastic aorta stores part of systolic stroke volume and recoils in diastole ( Windkessel effect ). \n Where turbulent sounds come from \n For idealised tube flow,  Re = ρvD/η , where ρ is fluid density, v mean velocity, D tube diameter, and η viscosity. A narrowing can make the local velocity rise enough to favour turbulence, and a fall in viscosity in anaemia can also favour turbulence. This is why turbulent  Korotkoff sounds  appear as an external cuff partly compresses a brachial artery. For a particular total flow, the local constricted segment can have high velocity even though its diameter has decreased. \n  Hagen–Poiseuille:   Q = (πr⁴ ΔP)/(8ηL) , so  R = 8ηL/(πr⁴)  under laminar, rigid, cylindrical assumptions. If radius falls from 2 mm to 1 mm and ΔP is held constant, theoretical flow becomes  one-sixteenth . Real vascular beds adapt through active tone and parallel branching. \n Blood is not a simple fluid \n Apparent blood viscosity depends on  haematocrit , plasma proteins, red-cell deformability, temperature and vessel size/flow conditions.  Polycythaemia  increases viscosity and may raise circulatory workload; anaemia lowers viscosity but reduces oxygen delivery, often eliciting a higher cardiac output. In small vessels, red-cell axial migration and the Fåhræus–Lindqvist phenomenon modify apparent viscosity. The lecturer includes artery–vein compliance contrasts (slide 74): a much larger increase in  venous volume  can occur with only a small pressure rise, whereas arteries are relatively stiff. \n  Practice:  Why are capillaries the slow-flow region although each capillary is narrow? Because their  combined cross-sectional area  is much larger than the aortic area. \n"},{"slug":"blood-pressure","title":"Arterial Blood Pressure","group":"Vessels","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"76-83","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"what-the-numbers-mean","title":"What the numbers mean"},{"id":"where-pressure-comes-from","title":"Where pressure comes from"},{"id":"measurement-principles","title":"Measurement principles"},{"id":"clinical-reasoning","title":"Clinical reasoning"},{"id":"correct-cuff-technique-and-gravity-effects","title":"Correct cuff technique and gravity effects"},{"id":"interpret-arterial-pressure-through-the-circulation","title":"Interpret arterial pressure through the circulation"}],"html":"<h1>Arterial Blood Pressure</h1>\n<h2 id=\"what-the-numbers-mean\">What the numbers mean</h2>\n<p><strong>Systolic blood pressure (SBP)</strong> is peak arterial pressure during ventricular ejection. <strong>Diastolic blood pressure (DBP)</strong> is the lowest pressure before the next ejection. <strong>Pulse pressure (PP) = SBP − DBP</strong>; for 120/80 mmHg, PP = 40 mmHg.</p>\n<p>Mean arterial pressure (<strong>MAP</strong>) is the cycle-average arterial pressure. At ordinary heart rates <strong>MAP ≈ DBP + ⅓(PP)</strong>, so for 120/80 it is about 93 mmHg. This approximation becomes less accurate with major changes in heart rate or waveform shape.</p>\n<h2 id=\"where-pressure-comes-from\">Where pressure comes from</h2>\n<p>In the systemic circuit <strong>MAP − right atrial pressure ≈ CO × systemic vascular resistance</strong>. Thus BP depends not just on HR, but on SV/CO, vascular resistance, arterial compliance and blood volume. Aortic elasticity maintains pressure between ventricular beats.</p>\n<h2 id=\"measurement-principles\">Measurement principles</h2>\n<p>A cuff is inflated above systolic pressure and then slowly deflated. The first characteristic <strong>Korotkoff sound</strong> is used for systolic pressure and disappearance of sounds (phase V) usually indicates diastolic pressure in adults. Use an appropriately sized cuff, a supported arm at heart level, and proper rest; wrong cuff size and arm position bias the reading.</p>\n<h2 id=\"clinical-reasoning\">Clinical reasoning</h2>\n<p>An increased stroke volume or stiffer large arteries may widen pulse pressure. Strong arteriolar constriction raises resistance, potentially raising MAP even if cardiac output falls.</p>\n<p><strong>Check:</strong> If HR rises but SV falls more sharply, what happens to CO and must BP rise?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary>CO may fall, and BP need not rise. Systemic arterial pressure depends on both CO and systemic vascular resistance.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"correct-cuff-technique-and-gravity-effects\">Correct cuff technique and gravity effects</h2>\n<p>The reference BP is reported for the cuff <strong>near heart level</strong>. Hydrostatic pressure changes by roughly <strong>0.77 mmHg per vertical centimetre</strong> in a static blood column; keeping the arm much lower than the heart can give a falsely elevated reading. A cuff too <strong>small</strong> tends to overestimate BP, while a cuff too <strong>large</strong> can underestimate it. The patient should rest, avoid talking, support the arm and use the appropriate cuff size.</p>\n<p><strong>Palpation</strong> can estimate systolic pressure from reappearance of the distal pulse while deflating the cuff, but cannot directly establish diastolic pressure. With <strong>auscultation</strong>, the first clear Korotkoff sound estimates systolic, and disappearance (usually phase V in adults) estimates diastolic. Sounds arise when cuff pressure partly compresses the artery and causes disturbed/intermittent flow.</p>\n<h2 id=\"interpret-arterial-pressure-through-the-circulation\">Interpret arterial pressure through the circulation</h2>\n<p>Aortic pressure oscillates because the heart ejects periodically, but <strong>elastic arterial recoil</strong> maintains flow during diastole. MAP falls substantially across resistance vessels, particularly arterioles; the pressure profile in the lecturer's diagram (slide 83) illustrates why capillaries are protected from high arterial pressure. <strong>Pulse pressure</strong> depends strongly on stroke volume and arterial compliance. <strong>MAP</strong> is more strongly related to CO × peripheral resistance (with central venous pressure taken into account).</p>\n<p>If a patient loses blood, the <strong>baroreflex</strong> can temporarily raise resistance enough to maintain MAP even with falling SV. This explains why a near-normal BP cannot exclude compensated shock.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Why could two people have the same MAP but different pulse pressures? Their arterial compliance and SV/wave reflection may differ even when the time-averaged pressure is similar.</p>\n","plain":" Arterial Blood Pressure \n What the numbers mean \n  Systolic blood pressure (SBP)  is peak arterial pressure during ventricular ejection.  Diastolic blood pressure (DBP)  is the lowest pressure before the next ejection.  Pulse pressure (PP) = SBP − DBP ; for 120/80 mmHg, PP = 40 mmHg. \n Mean arterial pressure ( MAP ) is the cycle-average arterial pressure. At ordinary heart rates  MAP ≈ DBP + ⅓(PP) , so for 120/80 it is about 93 mmHg. This approximation becomes less accurate with major changes in heart rate or waveform shape. \n Where pressure comes from \n In the systemic circuit  MAP − right atrial pressure ≈ CO × systemic vascular resistance . Thus BP depends not just on HR, but on SV/CO, vascular resistance, arterial compliance and blood volume. Aortic elasticity maintains pressure between ventricular beats. \n Measurement principles \n A cuff is inflated above systolic pressure and then slowly deflated. The first characteristic  Korotkoff sound  is used for systolic pressure and disappearance of sounds (phase V) usually indicates diastolic pressure in adults. Use an appropriately sized cuff, a supported arm at heart level, and proper rest; wrong cuff size and arm position bias the reading. \n Clinical reasoning \n An increased stroke volume or stiffer large arteries may widen pulse pressure. Strong arteriolar constriction raises resistance, potentially raising MAP even if cardiac output falls. \n  Check:  If HR rises but SV falls more sharply, what happens to CO and must BP rise? \n  Answer CO may fall, and BP need not rise. Systemic arterial pressure depends on both CO and systemic vascular resistance. \n\n Correct cuff technique and gravity effects \n The reference BP is reported for the cuff  near heart level . Hydrostatic pressure changes by roughly  0.77 mmHg per vertical centimetre  in a static blood column; keeping the arm much lower than the heart can give a falsely elevated reading. A cuff too  small  tends to overestimate BP, while a cuff too  large  can underestimate it. The patient should rest, avoid talking, support the arm and use the appropriate cuff size. \n  Palpation  can estimate systolic pressure from reappearance of the distal pulse while deflating the cuff, but cannot directly establish diastolic pressure. With  auscultation , the first clear Korotkoff sound estimates systolic, and disappearance (usually phase V in adults) estimates diastolic. Sounds arise when cuff pressure partly compresses the artery and causes disturbed/intermittent flow. \n Interpret arterial pressure through the circulation \n Aortic pressure oscillates because the heart ejects periodically, but  elastic arterial recoil  maintains flow during diastole. MAP falls substantially across resistance vessels, particularly arterioles; the pressure profile in the lecturer's diagram (slide 83) illustrates why capillaries are protected from high arterial pressure.  Pulse pressure  depends strongly on stroke volume and arterial compliance.  MAP  is more strongly related to CO × peripheral resistance (with central venous pressure taken into account). \n If a patient loses blood, the  baroreflex  can temporarily raise resistance enough to maintain MAP even with falling SV. This explains why a near-normal BP cannot exclude compensated shock. \n  Practice:  Why could two people have the same MAP but different pulse pressures? Their arterial compliance and SV/wave reflection may differ even when the time-averaged pressure is similar. \n"},{"slug":"pulses","title":"Arterial Pulse and Jugular Venous Pulse","group":"Vessels","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"22-26","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"arterial-pulse","title":"Arterial pulse"},{"id":"jugular-venous-pulse-jvp","title":"Jugular venous pulse (JVP)"},{"id":"distinguish-the-arterial-pulse-from-actual-flow","title":"Distinguish the arterial pulse from actual flow"},{"id":"place-jvp-waves-in-the-cardiac-cycle","title":"Place JVP waves in the cardiac cycle"}],"html":"<h1>Arterial Pulse and Jugular Venous Pulse</h1>\n<h2 id=\"arterial-pulse\">Arterial pulse</h2>\n<p>The palpable arterial pulse is a <strong>pressure/distension wave</strong>, not a bolus of blood travelling at the same speed down the artery. Its propagation speed generally rises with arterial stiffness. Assess <strong>rate, rhythm, volume, contour/character, and symmetry</strong>.</p>\n<p>Pulse pressure tends to increase when stroke volume increases or arterial compliance decreases; the relationship is modified by wave reflection and peripheral resistance.</p>\n<h2 id=\"jugular-venous-pulse-jvp\">Jugular venous pulse (JVP)</h2>\n<p>The internal jugular venous pulsation transmits changes in <strong>right atrial pressure</strong>. Unlike a carotid arterial pulse, it is normally <strong>non-palpable</strong>, varies with posture/respiration and has more than one wave per cycle. JVP is clinically estimated from its vertical height above the sternal angle with appropriate patient positioning.</p>\n<p>The major JVP features are:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>a wave:</strong> right atrial contraction.</li>\n<li><strong>c wave:</strong> early ventricular systole, including tricuspid bulging/adjacent arterial transmission.</li>\n<li><strong>x descent:</strong> atrial relaxation and downward tricuspid motion.</li>\n<li><strong>v wave:</strong> right atrial filling against a closed tricuspid valve.</li>\n<li><strong>y descent:</strong> opening of the tricuspid valve and rapid ventricular filling.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Large <strong>v waves</strong> may occur with tricuspid regurgitation. <strong>Cannon a waves</strong> can occur when atria contract against a closed tricuspid valve, including AV dissociation. These are not interchangeable.</p>\n<p><strong>Check:</strong> Why can the neck show a venous pressure wave even though venous blood flows toward the heart?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary>Right atrial pressure fluctuations propagate backward through the compliant venous column; the pulsation is a pressure signal, not necessarily retrograde bulk flow.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"distinguish-the-arterial-pulse-from-actual-flow\">Distinguish the arterial pulse from actual flow</h2>\n<p>Each systolic ejection creates a <strong>pressure/distension wave</strong> that travels much faster than the blood's average forward velocity. Wave transmission increases as arteries stiffen with age; pulse quality may be assessed through rate, regularity, amplitude, contour, symmetry and location. The origin is LV ejection, but the palpable pulse is <strong>not an individual packet of blood</strong> moving from the aorta to the wrist.</p>\n<h2 id=\"place-jvp-waves-in-the-cardiac-cycle\">Place JVP waves in the cardiac cycle</h2>\n<p>The <strong>a wave</strong> follows right atrial contraction; the <strong>c wave</strong> occurs around early ventricular systole; <strong>x descent</strong> includes atrial relaxation and downward tricuspid displacement; the <strong>v wave</strong> reflects filling of the right atrium while the tricuspid valve remains closed; and the <strong>y descent</strong> accompanies tricuspid opening and right ventricular filling. The three positive waves are <strong>a, c and v</strong>; the descents are <strong>x and y</strong>.</p>\n<p>Different abnormalities enlarge different waves: <strong>large v waves</strong> are characteristic of tricuspid regurgitation; <strong>cannon a waves</strong> arise when the atrium contracts against a closed tricuspid valve, as can occur with AV dissociation. Raised JVP suggests elevated right atrial pressure; compare with a <strong>carotid arterial pulse</strong>, which is palpable, has one dominant systolic upstroke and is less dependent on posture.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Why does JVP often fall with inspiration? Negative intrathoracic pressure encourages systemic venous return to the thorax; the normal observed response is downward movement of the venous column (with important pathological exceptions).</p>\n","plain":" Arterial Pulse and Jugular Venous Pulse \n Arterial pulse \n The palpable arterial pulse is a  pressure/distension wave , not a bolus of blood travelling at the same speed down the artery. Its propagation speed generally rises with arterial stiffness. Assess  rate, rhythm, volume, contour/character, and symmetry . \n Pulse pressure tends to increase when stroke volume increases or arterial compliance decreases; the relationship is modified by wave reflection and peripheral resistance. \n Jugular venous pulse (JVP) \n The internal jugular venous pulsation transmits changes in  right atrial pressure . Unlike a carotid arterial pulse, it is normally  non-palpable , varies with posture/respiration and has more than one wave per cycle. JVP is clinically estimated from its vertical height above the sternal angle with appropriate patient positioning. \n The major JVP features are: \n \n  a wave:  right atrial contraction. \n  c wave:  early ventricular systole, including tricuspid bulging/adjacent arterial transmission. \n  x descent:  atrial relaxation and downward tricuspid motion. \n  v wave:  right atrial filling against a closed tricuspid valve. \n  y descent:  opening of the tricuspid valve and rapid ventricular filling. \n \n Large  v waves  may occur with tricuspid regurgitation.  Cannon a waves  can occur when atria contract against a closed tricuspid valve, including AV dissociation. These are not interchangeable. \n  Check:  Why can the neck show a venous pressure wave even though venous blood flows toward the heart? \n  Answer Right atrial pressure fluctuations propagate backward through the compliant venous column; the pulsation is a pressure signal, not necessarily retrograde bulk flow. \n\n Distinguish the arterial pulse from actual flow \n Each systolic ejection creates a  pressure/distension wave  that travels much faster than the blood's average forward velocity. Wave transmission increases as arteries stiffen with age; pulse quality may be assessed through rate, regularity, amplitude, contour, symmetry and location. The origin is LV ejection, but the palpable pulse is  not an individual packet of blood  moving from the aorta to the wrist. \n Place JVP waves in the cardiac cycle \n The  a wave  follows right atrial contraction; the  c wave  occurs around early ventricular systole;  x descent  includes atrial relaxation and downward tricuspid displacement; the  v wave  reflects filling of the right atrium while the tricuspid valve remains closed; and the  y descent  accompanies tricuspid opening and right ventricular filling. The three positive waves are  a, c and v ; the descents are  x and y . \n Different abnormalities enlarge different waves:  large v waves  are characteristic of tricuspid regurgitation;  cannon a waves  arise when the atrium contracts against a closed tricuspid valve, as can occur with AV dissociation. Raised JVP suggests elevated right atrial pressure; compare with a  carotid arterial pulse , which is palpable, has one dominant systolic upstroke and is less dependent on posture. \n  Practice:  Why does JVP often fall with inspiration? Negative intrathoracic pressure encourages systemic venous return to the thorax; the normal observed response is downward movement of the venous column (with important pathological exceptions). \n"},{"slug":"venous-return","title":"Veins and Venous Return","group":"Vessels","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"43-44 and 73-75 and 97-98","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"venous-return-determines-how-much-is-available-to-pump","title":"Venous return determines how much is available to pump"},{"id":"pressure-gradients-posture-and-muscle-pumping","title":"Pressure gradients, posture and muscle pumping"},{"id":"pressure-compliance-and-venous-reservoir","title":"Pressure, compliance and venous reservoir"},{"id":"pumps-and-valves","title":"Pumps and valves"}],"html":"<h1>Veins and Venous Return</h1>\n<h2 id=\"venous-return-determines-how-much-is-available-to-pump\">Venous return determines how much is available to pump</h2>\n<p><strong>Venous return (VR)</strong> is blood flowing back to the right atrium per unit time. In a steady state, <strong>venous return equals cardiac output</strong>. When venous return falls abruptly, right ventricular filling drops and—after a delay—left ventricular preload and stroke volume fall.</p>\n<p>Veins hold a major share of blood volume because they are compliant. Increased sympathetic venous tone reduces venous capacitance and mobilizes blood toward the heart. The skeletal-muscle pump, valves and respiratory pressure changes facilitate return from dependent limbs.</p>\n<h2 id=\"pressure-gradients-posture-and-muscle-pumping\">Pressure gradients, posture and muscle pumping</h2>\n<p>Blood flows toward the right atrium along a driving pressure gradient. A useful conceptual Guyton relationship is <strong>VR = (mean systemic filling pressure − right atrial pressure)/resistance to venous return</strong>, interpreted with attention to vascular collapse and physiological limitations.</p>\n<p>When standing still, gravity promotes venous pooling in the legs. Walking or subtle calf contractions compress veins; valves promote one-way movement. This explains why prolonged motionless standing can cause light-headedness and why soldiers subtly contract their leg muscles while standing.</p>\n<p>Inspiration usually lowers intrathoracic pressure, facilitating systemic venous return to the right heart; effects on left-heart filling and BP are more complex and may be delayed.</p>\n<p><strong>Check:</strong> Why can a very strong heartbeat fail to maintain output in severe haemorrhage?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary>Myocardial contractility cannot eject blood that never fills the heart. Low circulating volume causes reduced VR, EDV and ultimately stroke volume.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"pressure-compliance-and-venous-reservoir\">Pressure, compliance and venous reservoir</h2>\n<p>Veins are <strong>capacitance vessels</strong>: they contain a large proportion of resting circulating blood because they can accommodate substantial volume at low pressure. Venoconstriction decreases venous capacity and tends to shift blood toward the central circulation, raising <strong>mean systemic filling pressure</strong> and venous return when other conditions permit.</p>\n<p>Venous return depends on the pressure difference between peripheral veins/mean systemic filling pressure and the right atrium, divided by resistance to venous return. In steady state <strong>venous return = cardiac output</strong> because blood cannot accumulate continuously in either side of a closed system. A normal right atrial/central venous pressure is generally low (often approximately 0–5 mmHg in a resting adult); avoid accepting every old slide's absolute value without the measurement context.</p>\n<h2 id=\"pumps-and-valves\">Pumps and valves</h2>\n<p>Leg venous valves prevent backflow when the <strong>skeletal-muscle pump</strong> compresses deep veins, including during subtle contractions while standing. Inspiration reduces intrathoracic pressure, helping draw blood toward the thorax, although abdominal pressure and mechanical ventilation modify the effect. Arterial pulsation can aid nearby venous movement. Prolonged immobility permits pooling and may impair venous return.</p>\n<p>In the head, collapsible extracranial veins can narrow under low pressure, but <strong>dural venous sinuses</strong> are held open by dural attachments. An injured/open venous sinus may admit air, causing venous air embolism.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Why can venous return decrease even if blood volume has not changed? Increased venous pooling or right atrial pressure can reduce the pressure gradient driving return.</p>\n","plain":" Veins and Venous Return \n Venous return determines how much is available to pump \n  Venous return (VR)  is blood flowing back to the right atrium per unit time. In a steady state,  venous return equals cardiac output . When venous return falls abruptly, right ventricular filling drops and—after a delay—left ventricular preload and stroke volume fall. \n Veins hold a major share of blood volume because they are compliant. Increased sympathetic venous tone reduces venous capacitance and mobilizes blood toward the heart. The skeletal-muscle pump, valves and respiratory pressure changes facilitate return from dependent limbs. \n Pressure gradients, posture and muscle pumping \n Blood flows toward the right atrium along a driving pressure gradient. A useful conceptual Guyton relationship is  VR = (mean systemic filling pressure − right atrial pressure)/resistance to venous return , interpreted with attention to vascular collapse and physiological limitations. \n When standing still, gravity promotes venous pooling in the legs. Walking or subtle calf contractions compress veins; valves promote one-way movement. This explains why prolonged motionless standing can cause light-headedness and why soldiers subtly contract their leg muscles while standing. \n Inspiration usually lowers intrathoracic pressure, facilitating systemic venous return to the right heart; effects on left-heart filling and BP are more complex and may be delayed. \n  Check:  Why can a very strong heartbeat fail to maintain output in severe haemorrhage? \n  Answer Myocardial contractility cannot eject blood that never fills the heart. Low circulating volume causes reduced VR, EDV and ultimately stroke volume. \n\n Pressure, compliance and venous reservoir \n Veins are  capacitance vessels : they contain a large proportion of resting circulating blood because they can accommodate substantial volume at low pressure. Venoconstriction decreases venous capacity and tends to shift blood toward the central circulation, raising  mean systemic filling pressure  and venous return when other conditions permit. \n Venous return depends on the pressure difference between peripheral veins/mean systemic filling pressure and the right atrium, divided by resistance to venous return. In steady state  venous return = cardiac output  because blood cannot accumulate continuously in either side of a closed system. A normal right atrial/central venous pressure is generally low (often approximately 0–5 mmHg in a resting adult); avoid accepting every old slide's absolute value without the measurement context. \n Pumps and valves \n Leg venous valves prevent backflow when the  skeletal-muscle pump  compresses deep veins, including during subtle contractions while standing. Inspiration reduces intrathoracic pressure, helping draw blood toward the thorax, although abdominal pressure and mechanical ventilation modify the effect. Arterial pulsation can aid nearby venous movement. Prolonged immobility permits pooling and may impair venous return. \n In the head, collapsible extracranial veins can narrow under low pressure, but  dural venous sinuses  are held open by dural attachments. An injured/open venous sinus may admit air, causing venous air embolism. \n  Practice:  Why can venous return decrease even if blood volume has not changed? Increased venous pooling or right atrial pressure can reduce the pressure gradient driving return. \n"},{"slug":"microcirculation","title":"Microcirculation and Capillary Exchange","group":"Vessels","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"57-60 and 84-89","coverage":"lecturer supplemented","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"structure-matches-function","title":"Structure matches function"},{"id":"diffusion-versus-bulk-fluid-movement","title":"Diffusion versus bulk fluid movement"},{"id":"what-changes-exchange","title":"What changes exchange?"},{"id":"capillary-wall-specialisations","title":"Capillary wall specialisations"},{"id":"routes-of-exchange","title":"Routes of exchange"}],"html":"<h1>Microcirculation and Capillary Exchange</h1>\n<h2 id=\"structure-matches-function\">Structure matches function</h2>\n<p>Capillaries consist principally of endothelial cells, a <strong>basal lamina</strong> and associated pericytes, with no typical three-layered arterial wall. Their small diameter and thin exchange surface facilitate diffusion. <strong>Continuous</strong>, <strong>fenestrated</strong> and <strong>sinusoidal/discontinuous</strong> capillaries differ in permeability and location.</p>\n<p>Arterioles regulate incoming resistance and perfused surface area. Capillary recruitment improves exchange area in active tissues. Precapillary sphincter-like regulation varies between vascular beds and should not be assumed identical everywhere.</p>\n<h2 id=\"diffusion-versus-bulk-fluid-movement\">Diffusion versus bulk fluid movement</h2>\n<p><strong>Diffusion</strong> is driven primarily by concentration/partial-pressure gradients and is central for O₂, CO₂ and many solutes. <strong>Bulk filtration</strong> reflects hydrostatic and oncotic pressure differences across vascular barriers. Proteins remaining in plasma contribute to colloid osmotic pressure and oppose outward water movement.</p>\n<p>The classic Starling expression relates net fluid flux to capillary/interstitial hydrostatic and oncotic pressures. Modern refinements recognize the <strong>endothelial glycocalyx</strong> and subglycocalyx protein environment; in many continuous capillary beds there is modest net filtration along much of the vessel, with lymphatics returning interstitial fluid rather than routine large venous-end reabsorption.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-changes-exchange\">What changes exchange?</h2>\n<p>Greater capillary pressure or permeability promotes filtration; lower plasma oncotic pressure also favours tissue-fluid accumulation. Increased capillary surface area can accelerate exchange.</p>\n<p><strong>Check:</strong> Do capillaries need a thick subendothelial connective-tissue layer to have a basement membrane?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary>No. The basal lamina is specialised extracellular matrix closely associated with endothelial cells. A thick subendothelial connective-tissue layer is not required.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"capillary-wall-specialisations\">Capillary wall specialisations</h2>\n<p><strong>Continuous capillaries</strong> have an uninterrupted endothelial lining and comparatively controlled permeability; they are typical in muscle and CNS (the CNS has especially tight junctions). <strong>Fenestrated capillaries</strong> have small pores that favour exchange in tissues such as intestinal villi, endocrine glands and renal glomeruli (glomerular filtration also depends on a specialised basement membrane and podocytes). <strong>Sinusoidal/discontinuous capillaries</strong> have larger, irregular gaps and occur in liver, spleen and bone marrow. All are lined by endothelium supported by a <strong>basal lamina</strong> to varying degrees; capillaries do not have a true muscular tunica media.</p>\n<p>The lecture's diagram describing capillary pores in <strong>millimetres</strong> must not be copied literally: physiological fenestrations are commonly on the <strong>nanometre scale</strong>, not tens of millimetres. <strong>Precapillary sphincters</strong> are described in classical microcirculatory models, but not every microvascular bed has discrete anatomically identical sphincters. Arteriolar tone and local microvascular mechanisms regulate perfused capillary number.</p>\n<h2 id=\"routes-of-exchange\">Routes of exchange</h2>\n<p><strong>Diffusion</strong> carries oxygen, carbon dioxide and many small solutes across short concentration gradients; lipid-soluble gases cross cell membranes readily. <strong>Paracellular pathways/fenestrations</strong> permit varying movement of water and hydrophilic solutes. <strong>Vesicular transport</strong> contributes to selected larger-molecule transfer, depending on tissue. <strong>Bulk fluid filtration</strong> is driven by hydrostatic and oncotic pressures and the endothelial barrier; it is not the same process as individual solute diffusion.</p>\n<p>As a tissue becomes metabolically active, local mediators decrease arteriolar resistance and recruit better-perfused exchange routes, matching delivery to demand. Capillary blood normally travels slowly because the <strong>entire capillary bed has large cross-sectional area</strong>, promoting exchange time.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Why are endocrine tissues often supplied with fenestrated capillaries? Their vascular structure supports relatively rapid exchange of circulating hormones and solutes.</p>\n","plain":" Microcirculation and Capillary Exchange \n Structure matches function \n Capillaries consist principally of endothelial cells, a  basal lamina  and associated pericytes, with no typical three-layered arterial wall. Their small diameter and thin exchange surface facilitate diffusion.  Continuous ,  fenestrated  and  sinusoidal/discontinuous  capillaries differ in permeability and location. \n Arterioles regulate incoming resistance and perfused surface area. Capillary recruitment improves exchange area in active tissues. Precapillary sphincter-like regulation varies between vascular beds and should not be assumed identical everywhere. \n Diffusion versus bulk fluid movement \n  Diffusion  is driven primarily by concentration/partial-pressure gradients and is central for O₂, CO₂ and many solutes.  Bulk filtration  reflects hydrostatic and oncotic pressure differences across vascular barriers. Proteins remaining in plasma contribute to colloid osmotic pressure and oppose outward water movement. \n The classic Starling expression relates net fluid flux to capillary/interstitial hydrostatic and oncotic pressures. Modern refinements recognize the  endothelial glycocalyx  and subglycocalyx protein environment; in many continuous capillary beds there is modest net filtration along much of the vessel, with lymphatics returning interstitial fluid rather than routine large venous-end reabsorption. \n What changes exchange? \n Greater capillary pressure or permeability promotes filtration; lower plasma oncotic pressure also favours tissue-fluid accumulation. Increased capillary surface area can accelerate exchange. \n  Check:  Do capillaries need a thick subendothelial connective-tissue layer to have a basement membrane? \n  Answer No. The basal lamina is specialised extracellular matrix closely associated with endothelial cells. A thick subendothelial connective-tissue layer is not required. \n\n Capillary wall specialisations \n  Continuous capillaries  have an uninterrupted endothelial lining and comparatively controlled permeability; they are typical in muscle and CNS (the CNS has especially tight junctions).  Fenestrated capillaries  have small pores that favour exchange in tissues such as intestinal villi, endocrine glands and renal glomeruli (glomerular filtration also depends on a specialised basement membrane and podocytes).  Sinusoidal/discontinuous capillaries  have larger, irregular gaps and occur in liver, spleen and bone marrow. All are lined by endothelium supported by a  basal lamina  to varying degrees; capillaries do not have a true muscular tunica media. \n The lecture's diagram describing capillary pores in  millimetres  must not be copied literally: physiological fenestrations are commonly on the  nanometre scale , not tens of millimetres.  Precapillary sphincters  are described in classical microcirculatory models, but not every microvascular bed has discrete anatomically identical sphincters. Arteriolar tone and local microvascular mechanisms regulate perfused capillary number. \n Routes of exchange \n  Diffusion  carries oxygen, carbon dioxide and many small solutes across short concentration gradients; lipid-soluble gases cross cell membranes readily.  Paracellular pathways/fenestrations  permit varying movement of water and hydrophilic solutes.  Vesicular transport  contributes to selected larger-molecule transfer, depending on tissue.  Bulk fluid filtration  is driven by hydrostatic and oncotic pressures and the endothelial barrier; it is not the same process as individual solute diffusion. \n As a tissue becomes metabolically active, local mediators decrease arteriolar resistance and recruit better-perfused exchange routes, matching delivery to demand. Capillary blood normally travels slowly because the  entire capillary bed has large cross-sectional area , promoting exchange time. \n  Practice:  Why are endocrine tissues often supplied with fenestrated capillaries? Their vascular structure supports relatively rapid exchange of circulating hormones and solutes. \n"},{"slug":"edema","title":"Formation of Oedema","group":"Vessels","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"89-95","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"oedema-is-excessive-interstitial-fluid","title":"Oedema is excessive interstitial fluid"},{"id":"main-mechanisms","title":"Main mechanisms"},{"id":"why-location-matters","title":"Why location matters"},{"id":"put-starling-s-forces-into-one-equation","title":"Put Starling's forces into one equation"},{"id":"predict-the-distribution-of-oedema","title":"Predict the distribution of oedema"}],"html":"<h1>Formation of Oedema</h1>\n<h2 id=\"oedema-is-excessive-interstitial-fluid\">Oedema is excessive interstitial fluid</h2>\n<p>Interstitial fluid volume depends on fluid leaving the microvessels, its return through lymphatics, and tissue properties. Oedema results when filtration exceeds lymphatic drainage capacity, or when drainage is impaired.</p>\n<h2 id=\"main-mechanisms\">Main mechanisms</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Increased capillary hydrostatic pressure</strong> — e.g. venous obstruction, prolonged standing, some forms of heart failure.</li>\n<li><strong>Reduced plasma oncotic pressure</strong> — e.g. marked hypoalbuminaemia from liver disease, nephrotic protein loss or severe malnutrition.</li>\n<li><strong>Increased endothelial permeability</strong> — e.g. inflammation, burns, allergic mediator release.</li>\n<li><strong>Lymphatic obstruction/dysfunction</strong> — e.g. lymph node removal, tumour, filariasis.</li>\n<li><strong>Renal sodium/water retention</strong> — expands extracellular fluid and can increase microvascular hydrostatic pressures.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2 id=\"why-location-matters\">Why location matters</h2>\n<p>Dependent ankle oedema increases after prolonged standing because gravitational hydrostatic pressure rises in leg veins. Pulmonary oedema can occur when elevated pulmonary venous pressure drives excess filtration into lung interstitium and alveoli, disrupting gas exchange.</p>\n<p>Pitting oedema usually reflects free interstitial fluid; established lymphoedema may become less pitting due to fibrosis. The distinction is useful but not absolute.</p>\n<p><strong>Check:</strong> Why does reduced plasma albumin increase the risk of oedema?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary>Lower plasma colloid osmotic pressure reduces the inward force opposing filtration, favouring accumulation of interstitial fluid, especially when lymphatic return is exceeded.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"put-starling-s-forces-into-one-equation\">Put Starling's forces into one equation</h2>\n<p>A useful traditional expression is <strong>Jv = Kf[(Pc − Pi) − σ(πc − πi)]</strong>, where Jv is net fluid movement, Kf filtration coefficient, Pc/Pi hydrostatic pressures inside capillary/interstitium, πc/πi their colloid osmotic pressures, and σ the protein reflection coefficient. Increased capillary hydrostatic pressure (<strong>venous obstruction or heart failure</strong>), reduced plasma oncotic pressure (<strong>hypoalbuminaemia</strong>), increased barrier permeability (<strong>inflammation</strong>) or obstructed lymphatics can all produce interstitial fluid accumulation.</p>\n<p>The lecturer's <strong>venous-end reabsorption diagram (Circulation 1, slide 87)</strong> represents a classic teaching model. The modern <strong>endothelial glycocalyx</strong> model places the main relevant oncotic gradient across the glycocalyx and recognises that many continuous capillaries have persistent small net filtration at steady state, with filtered fluid returned by <strong>lymphatics</strong>. Transient reabsorption can still occur after a fall in capillary hydrostatic pressure; the old universal '85% reabsorbed at venous end' formulation is <strong>not a universal rule</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"predict-the-distribution-of-oedema\">Predict the distribution of oedema</h2>\n<p>Raised systemic venous pressure causes dependent oedema, often most evident at ankles while upright. Left heart failure can raise <strong>pulmonary venous pressure</strong>, causing pulmonary congestion/oedema. Hypoalbuminaemia lowers the force retaining fluid intravascularly and can produce more widespread oedema. Lymphatic obstruction often produces chronic local swelling with tissue remodelling and may become relatively <strong>non-pitting</strong>, while early lymphoedema can still pit.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> How can prolonged standing cause ankle swelling? Gravity increases dependent venous/capillary hydrostatic pressure, raising filtration; without sufficient muscle/lymph pumping, fluid accumulates.</p>\n","plain":" Formation of Oedema \n Oedema is excessive interstitial fluid \n Interstitial fluid volume depends on fluid leaving the microvessels, its return through lymphatics, and tissue properties. Oedema results when filtration exceeds lymphatic drainage capacity, or when drainage is impaired. \n Main mechanisms \n \n  Increased capillary hydrostatic pressure  — e.g. venous obstruction, prolonged standing, some forms of heart failure. \n  Reduced plasma oncotic pressure  — e.g. marked hypoalbuminaemia from liver disease, nephrotic protein loss or severe malnutrition. \n  Increased endothelial permeability  — e.g. inflammation, burns, allergic mediator release. \n  Lymphatic obstruction/dysfunction  — e.g. lymph node removal, tumour, filariasis. \n  Renal sodium/water retention  — expands extracellular fluid and can increase microvascular hydrostatic pressures. \n \n Why location matters \n Dependent ankle oedema increases after prolonged standing because gravitational hydrostatic pressure rises in leg veins. Pulmonary oedema can occur when elevated pulmonary venous pressure drives excess filtration into lung interstitium and alveoli, disrupting gas exchange. \n Pitting oedema usually reflects free interstitial fluid; established lymphoedema may become less pitting due to fibrosis. The distinction is useful but not absolute. \n  Check:  Why does reduced plasma albumin increase the risk of oedema? \n  Answer Lower plasma colloid osmotic pressure reduces the inward force opposing filtration, favouring accumulation of interstitial fluid, especially when lymphatic return is exceeded. \n\n Put Starling's forces into one equation \n A useful traditional expression is  Jv = Kf[(Pc − Pi) − σ(πc − πi)] , where Jv is net fluid movement, Kf filtration coefficient, Pc/Pi hydrostatic pressures inside capillary/interstitium, πc/πi their colloid osmotic pressures, and σ the protein reflection coefficient. Increased capillary hydrostatic pressure ( venous obstruction or heart failure ), reduced plasma oncotic pressure ( hypoalbuminaemia ), increased barrier permeability ( inflammation ) or obstructed lymphatics can all produce interstitial fluid accumulation. \n The lecturer's  venous-end reabsorption diagram (Circulation 1, slide 87)  represents a classic teaching model. The modern  endothelial glycocalyx  model places the main relevant oncotic gradient across the glycocalyx and recognises that many continuous capillaries have persistent small net filtration at steady state, with filtered fluid returned by  lymphatics . Transient reabsorption can still occur after a fall in capillary hydrostatic pressure; the old universal '85% reabsorbed at venous end' formulation is  not a universal rule . \n Predict the distribution of oedema \n Raised systemic venous pressure causes dependent oedema, often most evident at ankles while upright. Left heart failure can raise  pulmonary venous pressure , causing pulmonary congestion/oedema. Hypoalbuminaemia lowers the force retaining fluid intravascularly and can produce more widespread oedema. Lymphatic obstruction often produces chronic local swelling with tissue remodelling and may become relatively  non-pitting , while early lymphoedema can still pit. \n  Practice:  How can prolonged standing cause ankle swelling? Gravity increases dependent venous/capillary hydrostatic pressure, raising filtration; without sufficient muscle/lymph pumping, fluid accumulates. \n"},{"slug":"lymphatics","title":"Lymphatic Circulation","group":"Vessels","source":"CIRCULATION 1(4).pdf","pages":"60 and 94-95; Regional Circulation 47-48","coverage":"lecturer supplemented","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"a-return-route-for-interstitial-fluid-and-proteins","title":"A return route for interstitial fluid and proteins"},{"id":"why-lymphatic-flow-matters-to-starling-balance","title":"Why lymphatic flow matters to Starling balance"},{"id":"map-lymphatic-return-to-venous-blood","title":"Map lymphatic return to venous blood"},{"id":"why-lymphatic-obstruction-causes-swelling","title":"Why lymphatic obstruction causes swelling"}],"html":"<h1>Lymphatic Circulation</h1>\n<h2 id=\"a-return-route-for-interstitial-fluid-and-proteins\">A return route for interstitial fluid and proteins</h2>\n<p>Lymphatic capillaries begin blindly in tissues and collect water, proteins and other materials that cannot simply remain in the interstitium. Collecting lymphatics contain valves and contractile segments; skeletal-muscle activity and external pressure help lymph move toward the venous circulation.</p>\n<p>Most of the body drains via the <strong>thoracic duct</strong> into the left venous angle near the internal jugular–subclavian junction. The <strong>right lymphatic duct</strong> drains a smaller right-upper quadrant territory to the right venous angle. Lymph nodes also support immune surveillance.</p>\n<h2 id=\"why-lymphatic-flow-matters-to-starling-balance\">Why lymphatic flow matters to Starling balance</h2>\n<p>Microvessels continually filter some fluid into tissue spaces. Lymphatics return excess fluid and leaked proteins to the circulation. If filtration rises sufficiently, lymph flow initially increases; when capacity is overwhelmed, oedema results.</p>\n<p>Blockage after node surgery, malignancy or filarial infection can cause lymphoedema. Lymphatic obstruction can yield protein-rich interstitial fluid, leading over time to tissue inflammation and fibrosis.</p>\n<p><strong>Check:</strong> Why does lymphatic obstruction cause swelling even with normal cardiac output?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary>Net microvascular filtration continues, but its main return pathway is impaired; fluid and proteins accumulate in the interstitial space.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"map-lymphatic-return-to-venous-blood\">Map lymphatic return to venous blood</h2>\n<p>Initial lymphatic capillaries are blind-ended channels with overlapping endothelial flaps that admit fluid, proteins and some particles from interstitium. Collecting vessels have <strong>valves</strong> and rhythmic smooth-muscle activity; skeletal-muscle movement, breathing and nearby arterial pulsation help propel lymph toward the venous angles.</p>\n<p>The <strong>thoracic duct</strong> drains most of the body, usually entering the junction of left internal jugular and left subclavian veins. The <strong>right lymphatic duct</strong> drains the right upper quadrant into the corresponding right venous angle; anatomy varies. Lymph passes through <strong>lymph nodes</strong>, where immune surveillance occurs. Specialized intestinal lymphatics called <strong>lacteals</strong> transport absorbed long-chain dietary lipids as <strong>chylomicrons</strong>; they do not directly absorb all lipids by the same route.</p>\n<h2 id=\"why-lymphatic-obstruction-causes-swelling\">Why lymphatic obstruction causes swelling</h2>\n<p>When capillaries filter fluid and leak proteins into the interstitium, lymphatics return that material to blood. An obstruction (e.g. <strong>filariasis</strong> or surgery affecting lymph drainage) reduces clearance of interstitial fluid and protein, contributing to chronic swelling and potentially fibrosis. This connects directly to the lecturer's lymphoedema examples (Circulation 1, slide 95) and the lymphatic diagrams in Regional Circulation slides 47–48.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Why doesn't filtered capillary fluid normally accumulate progressively in the legs? Lymphatic removal, aided by movement and local mechanisms, returns most surplus filtered fluid to the venous system.</p>\n","plain":" Lymphatic Circulation \n A return route for interstitial fluid and proteins \n Lymphatic capillaries begin blindly in tissues and collect water, proteins and other materials that cannot simply remain in the interstitium. Collecting lymphatics contain valves and contractile segments; skeletal-muscle activity and external pressure help lymph move toward the venous circulation. \n Most of the body drains via the  thoracic duct  into the left venous angle near the internal jugular–subclavian junction. The  right lymphatic duct  drains a smaller right-upper quadrant territory to the right venous angle. Lymph nodes also support immune surveillance. \n Why lymphatic flow matters to Starling balance \n Microvessels continually filter some fluid into tissue spaces. Lymphatics return excess fluid and leaked proteins to the circulation. If filtration rises sufficiently, lymph flow initially increases; when capacity is overwhelmed, oedema results. \n Blockage after node surgery, malignancy or filarial infection can cause lymphoedema. Lymphatic obstruction can yield protein-rich interstitial fluid, leading over time to tissue inflammation and fibrosis. \n  Check:  Why does lymphatic obstruction cause swelling even with normal cardiac output? \n  Answer Net microvascular filtration continues, but its main return pathway is impaired; fluid and proteins accumulate in the interstitial space. \n\n Map lymphatic return to venous blood \n Initial lymphatic capillaries are blind-ended channels with overlapping endothelial flaps that admit fluid, proteins and some particles from interstitium. Collecting vessels have  valves  and rhythmic smooth-muscle activity; skeletal-muscle movement, breathing and nearby arterial pulsation help propel lymph toward the venous angles. \n The  thoracic duct  drains most of the body, usually entering the junction of left internal jugular and left subclavian veins. The  right lymphatic duct  drains the right upper quadrant into the corresponding right venous angle; anatomy varies. Lymph passes through  lymph nodes , where immune surveillance occurs. Specialized intestinal lymphatics called  lacteals  transport absorbed long-chain dietary lipids as  chylomicrons ; they do not directly absorb all lipids by the same route. \n Why lymphatic obstruction causes swelling \n When capillaries filter fluid and leak proteins into the interstitium, lymphatics return that material to blood. An obstruction (e.g.  filariasis  or surgery affecting lymph drainage) reduces clearance of interstitial fluid and protein, contributing to chronic swelling and potentially fibrosis. This connects directly to the lecturer's lymphoedema examples (Circulation 1, slide 95) and the lymphatic diagrams in Regional Circulation slides 47–48. \n  Practice:  Why doesn't filtered capillary fluid normally accumulate progressively in the legs? Lymphatic removal, aided by movement and local mechanisms, returns most surplus filtered fluid to the venous system. \n"},{"slug":"local-regulation","title":"Local Control of Blood Flow","group":"Control","source":"CIRCULATION 2 REGULATION OF THE CVS(2).pdf","pages":"1-11","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"a-tissue-can-adjust-its-own-supply","title":"A tissue can adjust its own supply"},{"id":"metabolic-mechanism","title":"Metabolic mechanism"},{"id":"myogenic-mechanism","title":"Myogenic mechanism"},{"id":"endothelial-signals","title":"Endothelial signals"},{"id":"compare-metabolic-and-myogenic-autoregulation","title":"Compare metabolic and myogenic autoregulation"},{"id":"do-not-memorise-the-incorrect-laplace-formula","title":"Do not memorise the incorrect Laplace formula"},{"id":"endothelium-active-signalling-organ","title":"Endothelium: active signalling organ"}],"html":"<h1>Local Control of Blood Flow</h1>\n<h2 id=\"a-tissue-can-adjust-its-own-supply\">A tissue can adjust its own supply</h2>\n<p><strong>Autoregulation</strong> is a tissue's ability to keep flow relatively stable despite moderate changes in perfusion pressure. It is especially important in cerebral, renal and coronary circulations. A change in arteriole calibre alters local resistance: <strong>Q = ΔP/R</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"metabolic-mechanism\">Metabolic mechanism</h2>\n<p>When local tissue metabolism rises or perfusion falls, vasodilator signals tend to accumulate. Relevant signals can include increased CO₂/H⁺, K⁺, adenosine, lactate in some contexts, and reduced tissue O₂. Dilation increases flow, improves oxygen delivery and helps wash out signals. Different tissues do <strong>not</strong> use identical mixtures of mediators.</p>\n<p>Increased activity causes <strong>active hyperaemia</strong> (e.g. exercise in skeletal muscle). Transient vessel occlusion followed by a flow overshoot causes <strong>reactive hyperaemia</strong>, reflecting accumulated dilator signals and other mechanisms during reduced flow.</p>\n<h2 id=\"myogenic-mechanism\">Myogenic mechanism</h2>\n<p>An abrupt increase in intraluminal pressure stretches resistance-vessel smooth muscle, which responds by contracting. This increases resistance and helps prevent flow from rising excessively. Reduced stretch can favour relaxation. The myogenic response and metabolic control often act together.</p>\n<p><strong>Laplace's principle:</strong> a larger-radius thin-walled cylindrical vessel requires greater circumferential tension to oppose a given transmural pressure (approximately T = P × r per unit axial length). Different wall geometries use different mathematical forms. This helps explain why vessel radius matters to wall stress, though myogenic autoregulation involves active smooth-muscle signalling, not Laplace's law alone.</p>\n<h2 id=\"endothelial-signals\">Endothelial signals</h2>\n<p><strong>Nitric oxide (NO)</strong> produced by endothelium promotes smooth-muscle relaxation through cGMP. Endothelin is a powerful vasoconstrictor. Local endothelial, neural and metabolic influences collectively determine vessel calibre.</p>\n<p><strong>Check:</strong> A resistance arteriole is suddenly exposed to greater perfusion pressure. How can myogenic autoregulation oppose a large rise in flow?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary>Wall stretch activates smooth muscle contraction, reducing radius, raising resistance and opposing the tendency for pressure-driven flow to increase.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"compare-metabolic-and-myogenic-autoregulation\">Compare metabolic and myogenic autoregulation</h2>\n<p><strong>Metabolic theory:</strong> When local perfusion fails to match tissue activity, oxygen may fall while CO₂, H⁺, adenosine, K⁺ and other local signals accumulate. Arteriolar dilation increases blood delivery and helps wash out metabolites. This predicts <strong>active hyperaemia</strong> during heightened metabolism (for instance exercising skeletal muscle) and <strong>reactive hyperaemia</strong> following temporary arterial occlusion: the longer/deeper the interruption, the greater the transient unmet demand and typical flow overshoot when obstruction is released.</p>\n<p><strong>Myogenic mechanism:</strong> Higher <strong>transmural pressure = intravascular − surrounding pressure</strong> distends arteriolar smooth muscle, activating contraction and raising resistance; decreased pressure tends to reduce stretch-induced tone. This stabilises local flow despite moderate driving-pressure changes and is important, for example, in cerebral and renal vascular beds. Metabolic and myogenic explanations are <strong>complementary</strong>, not mutually exclusive.</p>\n<h2 id=\"do-not-memorise-the-incorrect-laplace-formula\">Do not memorise the incorrect Laplace formula</h2>\n<p>For an ideal <strong>thin-walled cylinder</strong>, circumferential tension per unit axial length is approximately <strong>T = Ptm × r</strong>. If describing average circumferential wall <strong>stress</strong>, <strong>σθ ≈ Ptm × r / wall thickness</strong>. For an ideal sphere, the corresponding membrane tension is <strong>T = Ptm × r / 2</strong>. These are model-dependent, and real arteries have thick, active walls; the inconsistent reciprocal/sum expressions on Regulation slides 9–10 should not be reproduced without fixing the definitions.</p>\n<h2 id=\"endothelium-active-signalling-organ\">Endothelium: active signalling organ</h2>\n<p><strong>Nitric oxide (NO)</strong> activates smooth-muscle soluble guanylyl cyclase → <strong>cGMP</strong> → relaxation. <strong>Endothelin-1</strong> is a potent constrictor. <strong>Histamine and bradykinin</strong> can promote vasodilatation and permeability changes through context-dependent pathways. Cold commonly favours cutaneous vasoconstriction. These responses depend on vascular bed and receptor distribution; do not infer one mediator affects every vessel identically.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> What happens to autoregulatory arteriolar tone if local perfusion pressure suddenly increases? Myogenic constriction tends to counter excessive flow; the metabolic response depends on local tissue needs.</p>\n","plain":" Local Control of Blood Flow \n A tissue can adjust its own supply \n  Autoregulation  is a tissue's ability to keep flow relatively stable despite moderate changes in perfusion pressure. It is especially important in cerebral, renal and coronary circulations. A change in arteriole calibre alters local resistance:  Q = ΔP/R . \n Metabolic mechanism \n When local tissue metabolism rises or perfusion falls, vasodilator signals tend to accumulate. Relevant signals can include increased CO₂/H⁺, K⁺, adenosine, lactate in some contexts, and reduced tissue O₂. Dilation increases flow, improves oxygen delivery and helps wash out signals. Different tissues do  not  use identical mixtures of mediators. \n Increased activity causes  active hyperaemia  (e.g. exercise in skeletal muscle). Transient vessel occlusion followed by a flow overshoot causes  reactive hyperaemia , reflecting accumulated dilator signals and other mechanisms during reduced flow. \n Myogenic mechanism \n An abrupt increase in intraluminal pressure stretches resistance-vessel smooth muscle, which responds by contracting. This increases resistance and helps prevent flow from rising excessively. Reduced stretch can favour relaxation. The myogenic response and metabolic control often act together. \n  Laplace's principle:  a larger-radius thin-walled cylindrical vessel requires greater circumferential tension to oppose a given transmural pressure (approximately T = P × r per unit axial length). Different wall geometries use different mathematical forms. This helps explain why vessel radius matters to wall stress, though myogenic autoregulation involves active smooth-muscle signalling, not Laplace's law alone. \n Endothelial signals \n  Nitric oxide (NO)  produced by endothelium promotes smooth-muscle relaxation through cGMP. Endothelin is a powerful vasoconstrictor. Local endothelial, neural and metabolic influences collectively determine vessel calibre. \n  Check:  A resistance arteriole is suddenly exposed to greater perfusion pressure. How can myogenic autoregulation oppose a large rise in flow? \n  Answer Wall stretch activates smooth muscle contraction, reducing radius, raising resistance and opposing the tendency for pressure-driven flow to increase. \n\n Compare metabolic and myogenic autoregulation \n  Metabolic theory:  When local perfusion fails to match tissue activity, oxygen may fall while CO₂, H⁺, adenosine, K⁺ and other local signals accumulate. Arteriolar dilation increases blood delivery and helps wash out metabolites. This predicts  active hyperaemia  during heightened metabolism (for instance exercising skeletal muscle) and  reactive hyperaemia  following temporary arterial occlusion: the longer/deeper the interruption, the greater the transient unmet demand and typical flow overshoot when obstruction is released. \n  Myogenic mechanism:  Higher  transmural pressure = intravascular − surrounding pressure  distends arteriolar smooth muscle, activating contraction and raising resistance; decreased pressure tends to reduce stretch-induced tone. This stabilises local flow despite moderate driving-pressure changes and is important, for example, in cerebral and renal vascular beds. Metabolic and myogenic explanations are  complementary , not mutually exclusive. \n Do not memorise the incorrect Laplace formula \n For an ideal  thin-walled cylinder , circumferential tension per unit axial length is approximately  T = Ptm × r . If describing average circumferential wall  stress ,  σθ ≈ Ptm × r / wall thickness . For an ideal sphere, the corresponding membrane tension is  T = Ptm × r / 2 . These are model-dependent, and real arteries have thick, active walls; the inconsistent reciprocal/sum expressions on Regulation slides 9–10 should not be reproduced without fixing the definitions. \n Endothelium: active signalling organ \n  Nitric oxide (NO)  activates smooth-muscle soluble guanylyl cyclase →  cGMP  → relaxation.  Endothelin-1  is a potent constrictor.  Histamine and bradykinin  can promote vasodilatation and permeability changes through context-dependent pathways. Cold commonly favours cutaneous vasoconstriction. These responses depend on vascular bed and receptor distribution; do not infer one mediator affects every vessel identically. \n  Practice:  What happens to autoregulatory arteriolar tone if local perfusion pressure suddenly increases? Myogenic constriction tends to counter excessive flow; the metabolic response depends on local tissue needs. \n"},{"slug":"neural-humoral","title":"Neural and Hormonal Regulation","group":"Control","source":"CIRCULATION 2 REGULATION OF THE CVS(2).pdf","pages":"12-30","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"the-short-term-neural-controller","title":"The short-term neural controller"},{"id":"why-bp-depends-on-two-linked-factors","title":"Why BP depends on two linked factors"},{"id":"humoral-regulation","title":"Humoral regulation"},{"id":"time-scale-is-important","title":"Time scale is important"},{"id":"trace-the-medullary-cardiovascular-network","title":"Trace the medullary cardiovascular network"},{"id":"separate-vascular-tone-from-heart-rate","title":"Separate vascular tone from heart rate"},{"id":"choose-the-correct-time-scale","title":"Choose the correct time scale"}],"html":"<h1>Neural and Hormonal Cardiovascular Regulation</h1>\n<h2 id=\"the-short-term-neural-controller\">The short-term neural controller</h2>\n<p>Brainstem cardiovascular networks (including medullary nuclei) coordinate sympathetic and parasympathetic output. Sympathetic nerves release mainly <strong>noradrenaline</strong> from postganglionic endings. <strong>α₁ receptors</strong> on many systemic resistance vessels cause vasoconstriction; sympathetic activation also constricts many veins, reducing capacitance. The heart responds mainly via <strong>β₁ receptors</strong>, increasing heart rate, conduction and contractility.</p>\n<p>Vagal <strong>acetylcholine–M₂</strong> signalling slows SA nodal rate and AV conduction. The vagus normally exerts considerable resting restraint on heart rate, so atropine or vagal withdrawal may raise it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"why-bp-depends-on-two-linked-factors\">Why BP depends on two linked factors</h2>\n<p>Approximately <strong>MAP ≈ CO × SVR</strong> if right atrial pressure is small. Cardiac sympathetic effects often increase CO; arteriolar α-mediated constriction increases systemic vascular resistance. Venoconstriction can also support preload, allowing output to rise when circulating volume is adequate.</p>\n<h2 id=\"humoral-regulation\">Humoral regulation</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Angiotensin II</strong> strongly constricts vessels and promotes aldosterone release.</li>\n<li><strong>Aldosterone</strong> helps retain sodium and water over hours/days.</li>\n<li><strong>Vasopressin (ADH)</strong> promotes renal water retention; at higher concentrations it can constrict vessels.</li>\n<li><strong>Adrenaline</strong> acts on different α and β receptors depending on dose and vascular bed.</li>\n<li><strong>Atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP)</strong> promotes natriuresis and tends to counter volume expansion.</li>\n<li>Endothelium-derived NO, endothelin and local autacoids modify regional tone.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"time-scale-is-important\">Time scale is important</h2>\n<p>Neural regulation can act in seconds. Renal sodium/water balance and RAAS contribute importantly over longer intervals. Both levels interact rather than operating independently.</p>\n<p><strong>Check:</strong> Why can sympathetic activation raise BP through vessels even if HR changes little?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary>Arteriolar vasoconstriction raises systemic vascular resistance, while venoconstriction may support preload. Both influence arterial pressure independently of a large heart-rate change.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"trace-the-medullary-cardiovascular-network\">Trace the medullary cardiovascular network</h2>\n<p>The lecturer distinguishes medullary <strong>pressor</strong> and <strong>depressor</strong> regions. Modern functional descriptions identify the <strong>nucleus tractus solitarius (NTS)</strong> as a major sensory integrator, the <strong>rostral ventrolateral medulla (RVLM)</strong> as an important source of sympathetic vasomotor drive, and the <strong>caudal ventrolateral medulla (CVLM)</strong> as an inhibitory relay. These networks alter sympathetic and vagal traffic to heart and vessels.</p>\n<p><strong>Tonic sympathetic vasoconstrictor discharge</strong> maintains baseline arteriolar and venous tone. When discharge increases, <strong>α₁ receptors</strong> generally increase vascular smooth-muscle contraction. Decreasing tonic drive allows vasodilatation; it does not require a separate continuously active vasodilator nerve. Some vascular beds have specialised autonomic dilator influences, but the central teaching is widespread sympathetic vasoconstrictor tone and locally regulated perfusion.</p>\n<h2 id=\"separate-vascular-tone-from-heart-rate\">Separate vascular tone from heart rate</h2>\n<p><strong>β₁</strong> stimulation of SA node/ventricular myocardium can increase heart rate and contractility. <strong>M₂</strong> vagal signalling reduces sinus rate and AV conduction. Because <strong>CO = HR × SV</strong>, an increased HR does not guarantee increased CO when reduced filling lowers SV. Because <strong>MAP ≈ CO × SVR</strong> (when right atrial pressure is small), even a falling CO may coexist with temporarily maintained MAP when SVR increases.</p>\n<h2 id=\"choose-the-correct-time-scale\">Choose the correct time scale</h2>\n<p>Neural baroreflex responses act within <strong>seconds</strong>. <strong>Angiotensin II</strong> and catecholamines influence vascular tone over seconds to minutes; <strong>aldosterone</strong>, renal pressure natriuresis and sodium balance influence blood volume over hours to days. <strong>ANP</strong> is released with atrial stretch and promotes renal salt/water excretion. <strong>ADH</strong> supports water retention and at high concentrations can contribute to vasoconstriction.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> A person receives atropine, blocking M₂ receptors. Resting HR often <strong>rises</strong> because tonic vagal restraint of the SA node is reduced.</p>\n","plain":" Neural and Hormonal Cardiovascular Regulation \n The short-term neural controller \n Brainstem cardiovascular networks (including medullary nuclei) coordinate sympathetic and parasympathetic output. Sympathetic nerves release mainly  noradrenaline  from postganglionic endings.  α₁ receptors  on many systemic resistance vessels cause vasoconstriction; sympathetic activation also constricts many veins, reducing capacitance. The heart responds mainly via  β₁ receptors , increasing heart rate, conduction and contractility. \n Vagal  acetylcholine–M₂  signalling slows SA nodal rate and AV conduction. The vagus normally exerts considerable resting restraint on heart rate, so atropine or vagal withdrawal may raise it. \n Why BP depends on two linked factors \n Approximately  MAP ≈ CO × SVR  if right atrial pressure is small. Cardiac sympathetic effects often increase CO; arteriolar α-mediated constriction increases systemic vascular resistance. Venoconstriction can also support preload, allowing output to rise when circulating volume is adequate. \n Humoral regulation \n \n  Angiotensin II  strongly constricts vessels and promotes aldosterone release. \n  Aldosterone  helps retain sodium and water over hours/days. \n  Vasopressin (ADH)  promotes renal water retention; at higher concentrations it can constrict vessels. \n  Adrenaline  acts on different α and β receptors depending on dose and vascular bed. \n  Atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP)  promotes natriuresis and tends to counter volume expansion. \n Endothelium-derived NO, endothelin and local autacoids modify regional tone. \n \n Time scale is important \n Neural regulation can act in seconds. Renal sodium/water balance and RAAS contribute importantly over longer intervals. Both levels interact rather than operating independently. \n  Check:  Why can sympathetic activation raise BP through vessels even if HR changes little? \n  Answer Arteriolar vasoconstriction raises systemic vascular resistance, while venoconstriction may support preload. Both influence arterial pressure independently of a large heart-rate change. \n\n Trace the medullary cardiovascular network \n The lecturer distinguishes medullary  pressor  and  depressor  regions. Modern functional descriptions identify the  nucleus tractus solitarius (NTS)  as a major sensory integrator, the  rostral ventrolateral medulla (RVLM)  as an important source of sympathetic vasomotor drive, and the  caudal ventrolateral medulla (CVLM)  as an inhibitory relay. These networks alter sympathetic and vagal traffic to heart and vessels. \n  Tonic sympathetic vasoconstrictor discharge  maintains baseline arteriolar and venous tone. When discharge increases,  α₁ receptors  generally increase vascular smooth-muscle contraction. Decreasing tonic drive allows vasodilatation; it does not require a separate continuously active vasodilator nerve. Some vascular beds have specialised autonomic dilator influences, but the central teaching is widespread sympathetic vasoconstrictor tone and locally regulated perfusion. \n Separate vascular tone from heart rate \n  β₁  stimulation of SA node/ventricular myocardium can increase heart rate and contractility.  M₂  vagal signalling reduces sinus rate and AV conduction. Because  CO = HR × SV , an increased HR does not guarantee increased CO when reduced filling lowers SV. Because  MAP ≈ CO × SVR  (when right atrial pressure is small), even a falling CO may coexist with temporarily maintained MAP when SVR increases. \n Choose the correct time scale \n Neural baroreflex responses act within  seconds .  Angiotensin II  and catecholamines influence vascular tone over seconds to minutes;  aldosterone , renal pressure natriuresis and sodium balance influence blood volume over hours to days.  ANP  is released with atrial stretch and promotes renal salt/water excretion.  ADH  supports water retention and at high concentrations can contribute to vasoconstriction. \n  Practice:  A person receives atropine, blocking M₂ receptors. Resting HR often  rises  because tonic vagal restraint of the SA node is reduced. \n"},{"slug":"reflexes","title":"Cardiovascular Reflexes and Cushing Response","group":"Control","source":"CIRCULATION 2 REGULATION OF THE CVS(2).pdf","pages":"18-36","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"arterial-baroreflex-a-negative-feedback-loop","title":"Arterial baroreflex: a negative-feedback loop"},{"id":"atrial-stretch-and-volume-reflexes","title":"Atrial stretch and volume reflexes"},{"id":"cushing-response","title":"Cushing response"},{"id":"afferent-nerves-and-feedback-direction","title":"Afferent nerves and feedback direction"},{"id":"atrial-receptors-and-bainbridge-response","title":"Atrial receptors and Bainbridge response"},{"id":"raised-intracranial-pressure-connect-both-reflexes","title":"Raised intracranial pressure: connect both reflexes"}],"html":"<h1>Cardiovascular Reflexes and the Cushing Response</h1>\n<h2 id=\"arterial-baroreflex-a-negative-feedback-loop\">Arterial baroreflex: a negative-feedback loop</h2>\n<p>Stretch-sensitive endings in the <strong>carotid sinus</strong> and <strong>aortic arch</strong> report pressure changes. Carotid afferents travel via <strong>Hering's nerve</strong>, a branch of glossopharyngeal nerve <strong>IX</strong>; aortic afferents largely via vagus <strong>X</strong>. They project to the medullary <strong>nucleus tractus solitarius (NTS)</strong>, which modifies autonomic outflow.</p>\n<p>When BP rises: <strong>more baroreceptor firing → less sympathetic and more vagal cardiac activity → lower HR, contractility and peripheral resistance → pressure tends to fall</strong>. When BP falls, the reflex changes in the opposite direction. It is especially useful for rapid BP buffering, such as standing up.</p>\n<h2 id=\"atrial-stretch-and-volume-reflexes\">Atrial stretch and volume reflexes</h2>\n<p>Low-pressure cardiopulmonary receptors respond to changes in central blood volume. Atrial stretch can contribute to reflex tachycardia (<strong>Bainbridge reflex</strong>) and influence water/sodium regulation, although the actual HR response depends on competing reflexes and physiological context.</p>\n<h2 id=\"cushing-response\">Cushing response</h2>\n<p>Markedly raised <strong>intracranial pressure (ICP)</strong> reduces <strong>cerebral perfusion pressure ≈ MAP − ICP</strong> when venous pressure is not higher. Brainstem ischaemia can trigger intense sympathetic vasoconstriction and systemic hypertension to defend cerebral perfusion. That high arterial pressure then activates baroreceptors, contributing to <strong>reflex bradycardia</strong>. Altered respiration may occur; hypertension, bradycardia and abnormal respiration form the classic <strong>Cushing triad</strong>, a late warning sign of dangerous intracranial pressure elevation.</p>\n<p>The stimulus for hypertension is threatened brain perfusion, <strong>not</strong> the baroreceptor reflex itself.</p>\n<p><strong>Check:</strong> Why can increased intracranial pressure cause increased BP with a slower pulse?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary>Reduced brainstem perfusion triggers sympathetic hypertension; the resultant high arterial pressure causes baroreflex-mediated slowing of the heart.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"afferent-nerves-and-feedback-direction\">Afferent nerves and feedback direction</h2>\n<p>Stretch at the <strong>carotid sinus</strong> raises firing through <strong>Hering's nerve → glossopharyngeal nerve IX → NTS</strong>. Stretch at the <strong>aortic arch</strong> sends signals through <strong>vagus X → NTS</strong>. A sudden rise in arterial pressure therefore increases afferent firing, promoting <strong>vagal slowing</strong> of the heart and decreased sympathetic resistance-vessel tone. A pressure fall reduces afferent firing, disinhibiting sympathetic activity and reducing vagal restraint. The baroreceptors sense <strong>arterial wall stretch</strong>, not blood oxygen directly.</p>\n<h2 id=\"atrial-receptors-and-bainbridge-response\">Atrial receptors and Bainbridge response</h2>\n<p>Low-pressure receptors at atrial and pulmonary venous junctions respond to filling. The lecturer describes atrial <strong>type A</strong> discharges around atrial contraction and <strong>type B</strong> discharges during filling. Increased atrial stretch can promote a <strong>Bainbridge reflex</strong> increase in HR, helping the heart handle greater venous return, and engage hormonal responses that limit volume overload. Whether HR rises in an intact person depends on simultaneous baroreflex activity, making simplistic 'atrial stretch always causes tachycardia' unreliable.</p>\n<h2 id=\"raised-intracranial-pressure-connect-both-reflexes\">Raised intracranial pressure: connect both reflexes</h2>\n<p>When increased intracranial pressure compromises cerebral perfusion, medullary ischaemia may drive intense sympathetic vasoconstriction and <strong>hypertension</strong>. Elevated arterial pressure then triggers <strong>baroreflex bradycardia</strong>; respiratory irregularity may emerge from brainstem dysfunction. This is the <strong>Cushing response/triad</strong>. It does <em>not</em> mean raised intracranial pressure always creates high BP/low HR in every case; the triad is an ominous late pattern.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Why can BP rise while HR falls? Two linked reflex processes operate: threatened brain perfusion drives sympathetic BP elevation, and arterial baroreceptors then favour bradycardia.</p>\n","plain":" Cardiovascular Reflexes and the Cushing Response \n Arterial baroreflex: a negative-feedback loop \n Stretch-sensitive endings in the  carotid sinus  and  aortic arch  report pressure changes. Carotid afferents travel via  Hering's nerve , a branch of glossopharyngeal nerve  IX ; aortic afferents largely via vagus  X . They project to the medullary  nucleus tractus solitarius (NTS) , which modifies autonomic outflow. \n When BP rises:  more baroreceptor firing → less sympathetic and more vagal cardiac activity → lower HR, contractility and peripheral resistance → pressure tends to fall . When BP falls, the reflex changes in the opposite direction. It is especially useful for rapid BP buffering, such as standing up. \n Atrial stretch and volume reflexes \n Low-pressure cardiopulmonary receptors respond to changes in central blood volume. Atrial stretch can contribute to reflex tachycardia ( Bainbridge reflex ) and influence water/sodium regulation, although the actual HR response depends on competing reflexes and physiological context. \n Cushing response \n Markedly raised  intracranial pressure (ICP)  reduces  cerebral perfusion pressure ≈ MAP − ICP  when venous pressure is not higher. Brainstem ischaemia can trigger intense sympathetic vasoconstriction and systemic hypertension to defend cerebral perfusion. That high arterial pressure then activates baroreceptors, contributing to  reflex bradycardia . Altered respiration may occur; hypertension, bradycardia and abnormal respiration form the classic  Cushing triad , a late warning sign of dangerous intracranial pressure elevation. \n The stimulus for hypertension is threatened brain perfusion,  not  the baroreceptor reflex itself. \n  Check:  Why can increased intracranial pressure cause increased BP with a slower pulse? \n  Answer Reduced brainstem perfusion triggers sympathetic hypertension; the resultant high arterial pressure causes baroreflex-mediated slowing of the heart. \n\n Afferent nerves and feedback direction \n Stretch at the  carotid sinus  raises firing through  Hering's nerve → glossopharyngeal nerve IX → NTS . Stretch at the  aortic arch  sends signals through  vagus X → NTS . A sudden rise in arterial pressure therefore increases afferent firing, promoting  vagal slowing  of the heart and decreased sympathetic resistance-vessel tone. A pressure fall reduces afferent firing, disinhibiting sympathetic activity and reducing vagal restraint. The baroreceptors sense  arterial wall stretch , not blood oxygen directly. \n Atrial receptors and Bainbridge response \n Low-pressure receptors at atrial and pulmonary venous junctions respond to filling. The lecturer describes atrial  type A  discharges around atrial contraction and  type B  discharges during filling. Increased atrial stretch can promote a  Bainbridge reflex  increase in HR, helping the heart handle greater venous return, and engage hormonal responses that limit volume overload. Whether HR rises in an intact person depends on simultaneous baroreflex activity, making simplistic 'atrial stretch always causes tachycardia' unreliable. \n Raised intracranial pressure: connect both reflexes \n When increased intracranial pressure compromises cerebral perfusion, medullary ischaemia may drive intense sympathetic vasoconstriction and  hypertension . Elevated arterial pressure then triggers  baroreflex bradycardia ; respiratory irregularity may emerge from brainstem dysfunction. This is the  Cushing response/triad . It does  not  mean raised intracranial pressure always creates high BP/low HR in every case; the triad is an ominous late pattern. \n  Practice:  Why can BP rise while HR falls? Two linked reflex processes operate: threatened brain perfusion drives sympathetic BP elevation, and arterial baroreceptors then favour bradycardia. \n"},{"slug":"gravity","title":"Gravity and Orthostatic Homeostasis","group":"Control","source":"CIRCULATION 3 Homeostasis in CVS.pdf","pages":"2-11; duplicate Lecture 5 pp2-11","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"what-changes-when-someone-stands","title":"What changes when someone stands?"},{"id":"the-rapid-compensation","title":"The rapid compensation"},{"id":"why-standing-motionless-can-cause-fainting","title":"Why standing motionless can cause fainting"},{"id":"acceleration-and-g-forces","title":"Acceleration and g-forces"},{"id":"separate-orthostatic-hypotension-and-vasovagal-syncope","title":"Separate orthostatic hypotension and vasovagal syncope"},{"id":"think-in-pressure-columns","title":"Think in pressure columns"}],"html":"<h1>Gravity and Orthostatic Homeostasis</h1>\n<h2 id=\"what-changes-when-someone-stands\">What changes when someone stands?</h2>\n<p>When a person rises from lying to standing, gravity increases hydrostatic pressure in dependent veins. Blood transiently pools in the legs and splanchnic circulation. This reduces central venous volume, venous return, ventricular end-diastolic volume, stroke volume and potentially cerebral perfusion.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-rapid-compensation\">The rapid compensation</h2>\n<p>Reduced carotid sinus/aortic arch stretch decreases baroreceptor firing. Brainstem circuits increase sympathetic activity and reduce vagal activity, producing <strong>tachycardia, venoconstriction and arteriolar constriction</strong>. The skeletal-muscle pump supports venous return when standing or walking. Over longer time periods, renal salt/water regulation contributes.</p>\n<h2 id=\"why-standing-motionless-can-cause-fainting\">Why standing motionless can cause fainting</h2>\n<p>Without muscle pumping, pooling can continue. If compensatory mechanisms fail, cerebral perfusion falls, causing dizziness, visual dimming or syncope. Lying flat restores the head nearer the heart and reduces the gravitational pressure difference.</p>\n<p><strong>Orthostatic hypotension</strong> is a sustained excessive fall in arterial pressure on standing; autonomic neuropathy, medications and volume depletion are potential causes. Vasovagal syncope has an additional reflex pattern of vasodilatation and often bradycardia, and should not be equated with all postural hypotension.</p>\n<h2 id=\"acceleration-and-g-forces\">Acceleration and g-forces</h2>\n<p>Positive head-to-foot acceleration promotes blood displacement toward the legs and can impair vision and consciousness; anti-g suits counter dependent pooling. Negative acceleration drives blood toward the head and may cause congestion or visual disturbances. Effects depend on direction, magnitude, duration and individual compensation.</p>\n<p><strong>Check:</strong> Why do soldiers subtly contract calf muscles while standing at attention?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary>Muscle contractions compress deep leg veins. Venous valves direct blood toward the heart, reducing pooling and helping maintain venous return, CO and cerebral perfusion.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"separate-orthostatic-hypotension-and-vasovagal-syncope\">Separate orthostatic hypotension and vasovagal syncope</h2>\n<p><strong>Orthostatic hypotension</strong> is commonly defined as a sustained fall of <strong>at least 20 mmHg systolic or 10 mmHg diastolic within three minutes of standing</strong> (standard clinical criterion). Mechanistically, standing pools blood in dependent vessels, reduces preload and challenges cerebral perfusion. An intact baroreflex usually responds with <strong>tachycardia and vasoconstriction</strong>; autonomic failure, certain drugs, dehydration or prolonged pooling can overwhelm compensation. The lecturer's 'postural syncope = reflex bradycardia' should not be taken as the usual response to standing.</p>\n<p><strong>Vasovagal syncope</strong> often involves inappropriate reflex vasodilatation and/or bradycardia after triggers such as pain, heat, emotion or prolonged standing. It can also cause fainting but has a different autonomic pattern. Returning to the horizontal position restores a more favourable head-to-heart hydrostatic relationship.</p>\n<h2 id=\"think-in-pressure-columns\">Think in pressure columns</h2>\n<p>Arterial and venous pressure at the ankle when standing are higher than at heart level because of hydrostatic load. At the head, the opposite tendency occurs. Dependent capillary hydrostatic pressure rises with prolonged standing, encouraging <strong>ankle oedema</strong>. <strong>Leg-muscle isometric contractions</strong> compress deep veins: venous valves channel blood centrally and reduce pooling, an everyday explanation for the soldier-at-attention question.</p>\n<p>Under high positive head-to-foot <strong>+Gz</strong>, blood shifts toward legs and cerebral perfusion can fail, producing <strong>grey-out/blackout</strong>; anti-G suits increase external pressure over legs/abdomen. With negative <strong>−Gz</strong>, head/neck vascular congestion and 'red-out' symptoms may occur. Magnitude, direction and duration are crucial; the slide thresholds are illustrative rather than guarantees.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> A person is dizzy after standing motionless, but feels better while walking. Walking activates the venous muscle pump and helps maintain venous return.</p>\n","plain":" Gravity and Orthostatic Homeostasis \n What changes when someone stands? \n When a person rises from lying to standing, gravity increases hydrostatic pressure in dependent veins. Blood transiently pools in the legs and splanchnic circulation. This reduces central venous volume, venous return, ventricular end-diastolic volume, stroke volume and potentially cerebral perfusion. \n The rapid compensation \n Reduced carotid sinus/aortic arch stretch decreases baroreceptor firing. Brainstem circuits increase sympathetic activity and reduce vagal activity, producing  tachycardia, venoconstriction and arteriolar constriction . The skeletal-muscle pump supports venous return when standing or walking. Over longer time periods, renal salt/water regulation contributes. \n Why standing motionless can cause fainting \n Without muscle pumping, pooling can continue. If compensatory mechanisms fail, cerebral perfusion falls, causing dizziness, visual dimming or syncope. Lying flat restores the head nearer the heart and reduces the gravitational pressure difference. \n  Orthostatic hypotension  is a sustained excessive fall in arterial pressure on standing; autonomic neuropathy, medications and volume depletion are potential causes. Vasovagal syncope has an additional reflex pattern of vasodilatation and often bradycardia, and should not be equated with all postural hypotension. \n Acceleration and g-forces \n Positive head-to-foot acceleration promotes blood displacement toward the legs and can impair vision and consciousness; anti-g suits counter dependent pooling. Negative acceleration drives blood toward the head and may cause congestion or visual disturbances. Effects depend on direction, magnitude, duration and individual compensation. \n  Check:  Why do soldiers subtly contract calf muscles while standing at attention? \n  Answer Muscle contractions compress deep leg veins. Venous valves direct blood toward the heart, reducing pooling and helping maintain venous return, CO and cerebral perfusion. \n\n Separate orthostatic hypotension and vasovagal syncope \n  Orthostatic hypotension  is commonly defined as a sustained fall of  at least 20 mmHg systolic or 10 mmHg diastolic within three minutes of standing  (standard clinical criterion). Mechanistically, standing pools blood in dependent vessels, reduces preload and challenges cerebral perfusion. An intact baroreflex usually responds with  tachycardia and vasoconstriction ; autonomic failure, certain drugs, dehydration or prolonged pooling can overwhelm compensation. The lecturer's 'postural syncope = reflex bradycardia' should not be taken as the usual response to standing. \n  Vasovagal syncope  often involves inappropriate reflex vasodilatation and/or bradycardia after triggers such as pain, heat, emotion or prolonged standing. It can also cause fainting but has a different autonomic pattern. Returning to the horizontal position restores a more favourable head-to-heart hydrostatic relationship. \n Think in pressure columns \n Arterial and venous pressure at the ankle when standing are higher than at heart level because of hydrostatic load. At the head, the opposite tendency occurs. Dependent capillary hydrostatic pressure rises with prolonged standing, encouraging  ankle oedema .  Leg-muscle isometric contractions  compress deep veins: venous valves channel blood centrally and reduce pooling, an everyday explanation for the soldier-at-attention question. \n Under high positive head-to-foot  +Gz , blood shifts toward legs and cerebral perfusion can fail, producing  grey-out/blackout ; anti-G suits increase external pressure over legs/abdomen. With negative  −Gz , head/neck vascular congestion and 'red-out' symptoms may occur. Magnitude, direction and duration are crucial; the slide thresholds are illustrative rather than guarantees. \n  Practice:  A person is dizzy after standing motionless, but feels better while walking. Walking activates the venous muscle pump and helps maintain venous return. \n"},{"slug":"shock","title":"Circulatory Shock","group":"Control","source":"CIRCULATION 3 Homeostasis in CVS.pdf","pages":"12-30; duplicate Lecture 5 pp12-30","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"what-shock-actually-means","title":"What shock actually means"},{"id":"four-mechanistic-categories","title":"Four mechanistic categories"},{"id":"trace-haemorrhagic-shock-in-sequence","title":"Trace haemorrhagic shock in sequence"},{"id":"why-compensation-eventually-fails","title":"Why compensation eventually fails"},{"id":"the-blood-test-trap","title":"The blood test trap"},{"id":"link-individual-signs-to-compensation-and-perfusion","title":"Link individual signs to compensation and perfusion"},{"id":"explain-fluid-shifts-and-the-haemoglobin-trap-over-time","title":"Explain fluid shifts and the haemoglobin trap over time"},{"id":"classify-causes-by-mechanism-not-only-symptom","title":"Classify causes by mechanism, not only symptom"}],"html":"<h1>Circulatory Shock</h1>\n<h2 id=\"what-shock-actually-means\">What shock actually means</h2>\n<p><strong>Shock is inadequate tissue perfusion and oxygen delivery/utilisation relative to metabolic needs</strong>, producing cellular dysfunction. A low measured BP is common in advanced shock but not required early. Compensatory vasoconstriction can maintain MAP temporarily while tissue perfusion remains inadequate.</p>\n<h2 id=\"four-mechanistic-categories\">Four mechanistic categories</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Hypovolaemic:</strong> reduced effective intravascular volume—haemorrhage, substantial fluid loss, burns.</li>\n<li><strong>Cardiogenic:</strong> inadequate pump performance—large myocardial infarction, severe myocardial dysfunction or arrhythmia.</li>\n<li><strong>Distributive:</strong> pathologically reduced vascular tone and maldistributed flow—sepsis, anaphylaxis, neurogenic causes.</li>\n<li><strong>Obstructive:</strong> mechanical impediment to filling or flow—cardiac tamponade, tension pneumothorax, massive pulmonary embolism.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"trace-haemorrhagic-shock-in-sequence\">Trace haemorrhagic shock in sequence</h2>\n<p>Blood loss → reduced circulating volume → reduced venous return → reduced preload → reduced stroke volume → reduced CO → inadequate tissue oxygen delivery.</p>\n<p>The baroreflex stimulates tachycardia and arteriolar/venous constriction; RAAS and ADH promote retention of fluid. Skin may become cool/pale due to vasoconstriction; renal perfusion may fall, reducing urine output. Respiratory rate may increase. When oxygen delivery is inadequate, anaerobic metabolism contributes to <strong>lactate accumulation and metabolic acidosis</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"why-compensation-eventually-fails\">Why compensation eventually fails</h2>\n<p>Persistent ischaemia and acidosis impair myocardial contraction and vascular responsiveness, while endothelial/microcirculatory injury can worsen capillary leakage and cellular injury. This creates reinforcing feedback loops toward progressive shock and multiorgan dysfunction.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-blood-test-trap\">The blood test trap</h2>\n<p>Immediately after whole-blood loss, <strong>Hb concentration and haematocrit can remain near initial values</strong>, because plasma and red cells are lost together. Later interstitial fluid entry or fluid treatment dilutes remaining red cells and measured Hb/Hct decrease.</p>\n<p><strong>Check:</strong> A patient is tachycardic with cold extremities but near-normal MAP after bleeding. Can they still be in shock?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary>Yes. Sympathetic vasoconstriction can preserve MAP while CO and tissue oxygen delivery are inadequate. Organ perfusion and clinical context matter more than BP alone.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"link-individual-signs-to-compensation-and-perfusion\">Link individual signs to compensation and perfusion</h2>\n<p>In <strong>early compensated haemorrhage</strong>, sympathetic discharge increases HR, contractility, arteriolar resistance and venous tone. This supports central BP at the expense of reduced cutaneous and sometimes renal perfusion, producing <strong>cool pale skin</strong>, a fast pulse and reduced urine production. Respiratory drive may increase because of stress, acidosis and chemoreceptor signals. In <strong>septic distributive shock</strong>, however, extremities can initially be warm: avoid treating 'cold skin' as a universal sign of every shock category.</p>\n<p>Low cellular oxygen delivery can increase <strong>anaerobic glycolytic flux</strong>, <strong>lactate</strong> and metabolic acidosis. Prolonged hypoperfusion damages vascular endothelium, impairs myocardial performance and weakens responsiveness to constrictor signals, creating <strong>positive feedback</strong> toward worsening shock. 'Irreversible shock' is a descriptive endpoint when injury has advanced beyond successful rescue; exact timing is not predictable from a single diagram.</p>\n<h2 id=\"explain-fluid-shifts-and-the-haemoglobin-trap-over-time\">Explain fluid shifts and the haemoglobin trap over time</h2>\n<p>When whole blood is lost, <strong>RBCs and plasma are removed together</strong>, so Hb concentration and haematocrit may be little changed <strong>immediately</strong>. Over ensuing hours, movement of interstitial fluid into vessels and IV fluids dilute the remaining red cells, lowering measured Hb/Hct. <strong>RAAS, ADH and thirst</strong> help replace circulating volume, while <strong>erythropoietin-driven erythropoiesis</strong> restores red-cell mass over weeks, not immediately. The lecturer's 12–72 hour volume recovery and several-week red-cell recovery are broad illustrative time frames, not promises for severe untreated bleeding.</p>\n<h2 id=\"classify-causes-by-mechanism-not-only-symptom\">Classify causes by mechanism, not only symptom</h2>\n<p>A large <strong>myocardial infarction</strong> reduces pumping ability (cardiogenic); <strong>tamponade, massive pulmonary embolism or tension pneumothorax</strong> obstruct filling/flow (obstructive); <strong>anaphylaxis and severe sepsis</strong> reduce effective vascular tone and can increase capillary leak (distributive); <strong>haemorrhage, burns and severe gastrointestinal fluid loss</strong> reduce effective volume (hypovolaemic). Multiple categories can overlap in one patient.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Why is a normal Hb immediately after bleeding not reassuring? It measures <strong>concentration</strong>, which may stay similar when red cells and plasma are lost together; volume and oxygen delivery may already be dangerously reduced.</p>\n","plain":" Circulatory Shock \n What shock actually means \n  Shock is inadequate tissue perfusion and oxygen delivery/utilisation relative to metabolic needs , producing cellular dysfunction. A low measured BP is common in advanced shock but not required early. Compensatory vasoconstriction can maintain MAP temporarily while tissue perfusion remains inadequate. \n Four mechanistic categories \n \n  Hypovolaemic:  reduced effective intravascular volume—haemorrhage, substantial fluid loss, burns. \n  Cardiogenic:  inadequate pump performance—large myocardial infarction, severe myocardial dysfunction or arrhythmia. \n  Distributive:  pathologically reduced vascular tone and maldistributed flow—sepsis, anaphylaxis, neurogenic causes. \n  Obstructive:  mechanical impediment to filling or flow—cardiac tamponade, tension pneumothorax, massive pulmonary embolism. \n \n Trace haemorrhagic shock in sequence \n Blood loss → reduced circulating volume → reduced venous return → reduced preload → reduced stroke volume → reduced CO → inadequate tissue oxygen delivery. \n The baroreflex stimulates tachycardia and arteriolar/venous constriction; RAAS and ADH promote retention of fluid. Skin may become cool/pale due to vasoconstriction; renal perfusion may fall, reducing urine output. Respiratory rate may increase. When oxygen delivery is inadequate, anaerobic metabolism contributes to  lactate accumulation and metabolic acidosis . \n Why compensation eventually fails \n Persistent ischaemia and acidosis impair myocardial contraction and vascular responsiveness, while endothelial/microcirculatory injury can worsen capillary leakage and cellular injury. This creates reinforcing feedback loops toward progressive shock and multiorgan dysfunction. \n The blood test trap \n Immediately after whole-blood loss,  Hb concentration and haematocrit can remain near initial values , because plasma and red cells are lost together. Later interstitial fluid entry or fluid treatment dilutes remaining red cells and measured Hb/Hct decrease. \n  Check:  A patient is tachycardic with cold extremities but near-normal MAP after bleeding. Can they still be in shock? \n  Answer Yes. Sympathetic vasoconstriction can preserve MAP while CO and tissue oxygen delivery are inadequate. Organ perfusion and clinical context matter more than BP alone. \n\n Link individual signs to compensation and perfusion \n In  early compensated haemorrhage , sympathetic discharge increases HR, contractility, arteriolar resistance and venous tone. This supports central BP at the expense of reduced cutaneous and sometimes renal perfusion, producing  cool pale skin , a fast pulse and reduced urine production. Respiratory drive may increase because of stress, acidosis and chemoreceptor signals. In  septic distributive shock , however, extremities can initially be warm: avoid treating 'cold skin' as a universal sign of every shock category. \n Low cellular oxygen delivery can increase  anaerobic glycolytic flux ,  lactate  and metabolic acidosis. Prolonged hypoperfusion damages vascular endothelium, impairs myocardial performance and weakens responsiveness to constrictor signals, creating  positive feedback  toward worsening shock. 'Irreversible shock' is a descriptive endpoint when injury has advanced beyond successful rescue; exact timing is not predictable from a single diagram. \n Explain fluid shifts and the haemoglobin trap over time \n When whole blood is lost,  RBCs and plasma are removed together , so Hb concentration and haematocrit may be little changed  immediately . Over ensuing hours, movement of interstitial fluid into vessels and IV fluids dilute the remaining red cells, lowering measured Hb/Hct.  RAAS, ADH and thirst  help replace circulating volume, while  erythropoietin-driven erythropoiesis  restores red-cell mass over weeks, not immediately. The lecturer's 12–72 hour volume recovery and several-week red-cell recovery are broad illustrative time frames, not promises for severe untreated bleeding. \n Classify causes by mechanism, not only symptom \n A large  myocardial infarction  reduces pumping ability (cardiogenic);  tamponade, massive pulmonary embolism or tension pneumothorax  obstruct filling/flow (obstructive);  anaphylaxis and severe sepsis  reduce effective vascular tone and can increase capillary leak (distributive);  haemorrhage, burns and severe gastrointestinal fluid loss  reduce effective volume (hypovolaemic). Multiple categories can overlap in one patient. \n  Practice:  Why is a normal Hb immediately after bleeding not reassuring? It measures  concentration , which may stay similar when red cells and plasma are lost together; volume and oxygen delivery may already be dangerously reduced. \n"},{"slug":"heart-failure","title":"Heart Failure Physiology","group":"Control","source":"CIRCULATION 3 Homeostasis in CVS.pdf","pages":"31-33; duplicate Lecture 5 pp31-33","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"define-the-pump-problem","title":"Define the pump problem"},{"id":"forward-versus-backward-consequences","title":"Forward versus backward consequences"},{"id":"compensation-can-become-harmful","title":"Compensation can become harmful"},{"id":"systolic-and-diastolic-problems","title":"Systolic and diastolic problems"},{"id":"forward-flow-and-backward-congestion","title":"Forward flow and backward congestion"},{"id":"what-compensates-and-why-compensation-can-harm","title":"What compensates and why compensation can harm"}],"html":"<h1>Heart Failure Physiology</h1>\n<h2 id=\"define-the-pump-problem\">Define the pump problem</h2>\n<p>Heart failure is a clinical syndrome in which cardiac dysfunction produces inadequate forward output and/or elevated intracardiac filling pressures, with characteristic symptoms and signs. Output may be normal at rest in some forms, so it should not be defined only as low cardiac output.</p>\n<h2 id=\"forward-versus-backward-consequences\">Forward versus backward consequences</h2>\n<p><strong>Forward consequences</strong> relate to inadequate organ perfusion (fatigue, poor exercise tolerance, reduced renal blood flow). <strong>Backward consequences</strong> reflect elevated pressures upstream of the failing chamber: left-heart dysfunction can raise pulmonary venous/capillary pressure, causing breathlessness and pulmonary oedema; right-heart dysfunction can raise systemic venous pressure, producing elevated JVP, hepatic congestion, ascites and peripheral oedema.</p>\n<h2 id=\"compensation-can-become-harmful\">Compensation can become harmful</h2>\n<p>Reduced effective arterial perfusion activates sympathetic output, <strong>RAAS</strong> and ADH. These initially defend pressure and perfusion, but chronic tachycardia, vasoconstriction and sodium/water retention can increase afterload and congestion. Ventricular remodelling may further impair efficiency.</p>\n<h2 id=\"systolic-and-diastolic-problems\">Systolic and diastolic problems</h2>\n<p>In <strong>systolic dysfunction</strong>, ventricular ejection is impaired and EF may be reduced. In <strong>diastolic dysfunction</strong>, filling is impaired or requires higher pressure because relaxation/compliance are abnormal; EF may be preserved. Both can cause pulmonary congestion.</p>\n<p><strong>Check:</strong> Why can a patient with heart failure be oedematous even while kidneys behave as though the body lacks volume?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary>Kidneys may sense reduced effective arterial perfusion and retain sodium/water, despite excess total extracellular fluid and high venous filling pressures. This worsens congestion.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"forward-flow-and-backward-congestion\">Forward flow and backward congestion</h2>\n<p><strong>Forward failure</strong> emphasises insufficient effective cardiac output: fatigue, reduced exercise capacity, renal hypoperfusion and in severe cases reduced cerebral perfusion. <strong>Backward failure</strong> emphasises increased filling pressures and venous congestion: left-sided dysfunction often raises pulmonary venous pressure, causing dyspnoea, orthopnoea and pulmonary oedema; right-sided dysfunction often raises systemic venous pressure, causing raised JVP, dependent oedema, hepatic congestion and ascites.</p>\n<p>These are explanatory perspectives, <strong>not four mutually exclusive diseases</strong>. Left and right failure can coexist, and left-sided disease frequently contributes to right-sided dysfunction. Right-sided failure does not simply imply lungs receive no blood; changes in pulmonary flow and systemic venous pressures depend on the underlying problem.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-compensates-and-why-compensation-can-harm\">What compensates and why compensation can harm</h2>\n<p>Reduced forward output activates <strong>sympathetic nerves, RAAS and ADH</strong>. Initially increased HR, contractility and volume may preserve perfusion, but excessive vasoconstriction raises afterload and retained water/sodium worsen congestion. Frank–Starling can help compensate early, but a failing ventricular function curve gives diminishing benefit as filling pressure rises. Heart failure can occur with reduced ejection fraction or <strong>preserved ejection fraction when filling/relaxation is abnormal</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Why might a patient with left-sided heart failure become breathless when lying flat? Recumbency redistributes blood centrally, raising pulmonary venous pressure when the left ventricle cannot accommodate/eject adequately.</p>\n","plain":" Heart Failure Physiology \n Define the pump problem \n Heart failure is a clinical syndrome in which cardiac dysfunction produces inadequate forward output and/or elevated intracardiac filling pressures, with characteristic symptoms and signs. Output may be normal at rest in some forms, so it should not be defined only as low cardiac output. \n Forward versus backward consequences \n  Forward consequences  relate to inadequate organ perfusion (fatigue, poor exercise tolerance, reduced renal blood flow).  Backward consequences  reflect elevated pressures upstream of the failing chamber: left-heart dysfunction can raise pulmonary venous/capillary pressure, causing breathlessness and pulmonary oedema; right-heart dysfunction can raise systemic venous pressure, producing elevated JVP, hepatic congestion, ascites and peripheral oedema. \n Compensation can become harmful \n Reduced effective arterial perfusion activates sympathetic output,  RAAS  and ADH. These initially defend pressure and perfusion, but chronic tachycardia, vasoconstriction and sodium/water retention can increase afterload and congestion. Ventricular remodelling may further impair efficiency. \n Systolic and diastolic problems \n In  systolic dysfunction , ventricular ejection is impaired and EF may be reduced. In  diastolic dysfunction , filling is impaired or requires higher pressure because relaxation/compliance are abnormal; EF may be preserved. Both can cause pulmonary congestion. \n  Check:  Why can a patient with heart failure be oedematous even while kidneys behave as though the body lacks volume? \n  Answer Kidneys may sense reduced effective arterial perfusion and retain sodium/water, despite excess total extracellular fluid and high venous filling pressures. This worsens congestion. \n\n Forward flow and backward congestion \n  Forward failure  emphasises insufficient effective cardiac output: fatigue, reduced exercise capacity, renal hypoperfusion and in severe cases reduced cerebral perfusion.  Backward failure  emphasises increased filling pressures and venous congestion: left-sided dysfunction often raises pulmonary venous pressure, causing dyspnoea, orthopnoea and pulmonary oedema; right-sided dysfunction often raises systemic venous pressure, causing raised JVP, dependent oedema, hepatic congestion and ascites. \n These are explanatory perspectives,  not four mutually exclusive diseases . Left and right failure can coexist, and left-sided disease frequently contributes to right-sided dysfunction. Right-sided failure does not simply imply lungs receive no blood; changes in pulmonary flow and systemic venous pressures depend on the underlying problem. \n What compensates and why compensation can harm \n Reduced forward output activates  sympathetic nerves, RAAS and ADH . Initially increased HR, contractility and volume may preserve perfusion, but excessive vasoconstriction raises afterload and retained water/sodium worsen congestion. Frank–Starling can help compensate early, but a failing ventricular function curve gives diminishing benefit as filling pressure rises. Heart failure can occur with reduced ejection fraction or  preserved ejection fraction when filling/relaxation is abnormal . \n  Practice:  Why might a patient with left-sided heart failure become breathless when lying flat? Recumbency redistributes blood centrally, raising pulmonary venous pressure when the left ventricle cannot accommodate/eject adequately. \n"},{"slug":"cerebral","title":"Cerebral Circulation and CSF","group":"Regional","source":"Lecture 6 Regional Circulation Proper.pdf","pages":"2-25; extra diagrams Circulation 3 39-41","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"supply-and-flow-regulation","title":"Supply and flow regulation"},{"id":"intracranial-pressure-and-the-monro-kellie-principle","title":"Intracranial pressure and the Monro–Kellie principle"},{"id":"csf-path-and-functions","title":"CSF path and functions"},{"id":"blood-brain-barrier-bbb","title":"Blood–brain barrier (BBB)"},{"id":"identify-the-arterial-supplies-and-vulnerability","title":"Identify the arterial supplies and vulnerability"},{"id":"meninges-csf-and-pressure","title":"Meninges, CSF and pressure"},{"id":"cerebral-blood-flow-control","title":"Cerebral blood flow control"}],"html":"<h1>Cerebral Circulation, CSF and the Blood–Brain Barrier</h1>\n<h2 id=\"supply-and-flow-regulation\">Supply and flow regulation</h2>\n<p>The <strong>internal carotid</strong> and <strong>vertebrobasilar</strong> systems supply the brain and connect through arteries of the <strong>Circle of Willis</strong>, offering potential collateral routes; the circle is variable and does not guarantee protection from stroke. Cerebral perfusion depends on driving pressure and vascular resistance. <strong>Cerebral perfusion pressure (CPP) ≈ MAP − ICP</strong> when intracranial pressure exceeds venous outflow pressure.</p>\n<p>Cerebral vessels autoregulate over a range of pressures. Increased arterial CO₂ is a potent cerebral vasodilator; marked hypocapnia constricts cerebral arterioles. Severe hypoxia can also cause vasodilatation. Blood flow is adjusted regionally to neural activity.</p>\n<h2 id=\"intracranial-pressure-and-the-monro-kellie-principle\">Intracranial pressure and the Monro–Kellie principle</h2>\n<p>Within the largely fixed adult skull, brain tissue, blood and CSF share a limited volume. Added volume can initially be offset by shifting CSF/venous blood, but beyond compensation <strong>ICP rises sharply</strong>, potentially reducing CPP and causing the Cushing response.</p>\n<h2 id=\"csf-path-and-functions\">CSF path and functions</h2>\n<p>CSF is produced mainly by <strong>choroid plexus</strong>, flows from lateral ventricles through interventricular foramina (Monro) to third ventricle → cerebral aqueduct → fourth ventricle → median/lateral apertures → subarachnoid space, and returns ultimately to venous/lymphatic drainage pathways. Typical adult CSF volume is about <strong>150 mL</strong>. It provides buoyancy and cushioning and supports CNS waste/solute handling.</p>\n<p><strong>Hydrocephalus</strong> may result from impaired flow or absorption. The subarachnoid space normally contains CSF; the subdural space is a <strong>potential</strong>, not normally CSF-filled, space.</p>\n<h2 id=\"blood-brain-barrier-bbb\">Blood–brain barrier (BBB)</h2>\n<p>Specialised brain capillary <strong>endothelial tight junctions</strong>, transporters and neurovascular unit limit uncontrolled solute passage. Glucose enters through transporters (not simply free diffusion), while many polar compounds require specialised transport. BBB integrity is important for neuronal extracellular stability and drug access.</p>\n<p><strong>Check:</strong> Why can rising ICP cause brain ischaemia even when measured arterial BP appears normal?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary>CPP is approximately MAP minus ICP. A rise in ICP reduces the pressure gradient driving cerebral blood flow despite unchanged systemic MAP.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"identify-the-arterial-supplies-and-vulnerability\">Identify the arterial supplies and vulnerability</h2>\n<p>The <strong>anterior, middle and posterior cerebral arteries</strong> supply different territories. The two internal carotids and vertebrobasilar system connect variably in the <strong>Circle of Willis</strong>, potentially supporting collateral supply; the middle cerebral artery is a large terminal branch but <strong>not itself part of the arterial circle</strong>. The actual territories and clinical consequences of occlusion vary; the lecturer includes <strong>ischaemic</strong> (thrombotic/embolic) and <strong>haemorrhagic</strong> stroke as broad categories.</p>\n<h2 id=\"meninges-csf-and-pressure\">Meninges, CSF and pressure</h2>\n<p>From outside inward: <strong>dura mater</strong>, <strong>arachnoid mater</strong>, <strong>subarachnoid space containing CSF</strong>, then <strong>pia mater</strong> adhering to brain/spinal cord. The normal subdural space is only potential. CSF is formed mainly in the ventricular choroid plexuses; normal adult total volume is about <strong>150 mL</strong>, with production several hundred mL/day. Flow proceeds lateral ventricles → <strong>foramina of Monro</strong> → third ventricle → <strong>aqueduct of Sylvius</strong> → fourth ventricle → median (Magendie) and lateral (Luschka) apertures → subarachnoid space. CSF ultimately leaves through arachnoid and lymphatic outflow pathways. <strong>Communicating hydrocephalus</strong> generally has impaired CSF handling beyond the ventricular pathway; <strong>noncommunicating hydrocephalus</strong> has an obstruction within it.</p>\n<p>CSF protects via <strong>buoyancy and cushioning</strong> and supports interstitial solute clearance. <strong>Lumbar puncture</strong> samples subarachnoid CSF below the usual adult spinal cord termination; do not infer puncture is risk-free when raised ICP or mass effect is suspected.</p>\n<h2 id=\"cerebral-blood-flow-control\">Cerebral blood flow control</h2>\n<p><strong>CPP ≈ MAP − ICP</strong> in the common approximation, and high ICP compresses intracranial vessels. Local vessels respond strongly to <strong>CO₂</strong>: rising CO₂ usually dilates and falling CO₂ constricts cerebral arterioles. Brain tissue normally relies heavily on continuous glucose/oxygen delivery, using glucose transport across the BBB and alternative fuels such as <strong>ketone bodies during prolonged starvation</strong>; the slides' suggestion of freely substituting fatty acids is not a general statement of normal brain metabolism.</p>\n<p>The <strong>BBB</strong> depends on continuous nonfenestrated endothelial cells with tight junctions, specialised transporters and associated cells. Glucose uses <strong>GLUT1</strong>, and lipid-soluble gases diffuse comparatively readily. The BBB is <strong>not</strong> the same as the blood–CSF barrier at the choroid plexus.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> A brain tumour increases cranial volume beyond compensatory shifts. ICP rises, CPP can fall, and brain perfusion may worsen even if peripheral BP is unchanged.</p>\n","plain":" Cerebral Circulation, CSF and the Blood–Brain Barrier \n Supply and flow regulation \n The  internal carotid  and  vertebrobasilar  systems supply the brain and connect through arteries of the  Circle of Willis , offering potential collateral routes; the circle is variable and does not guarantee protection from stroke. Cerebral perfusion depends on driving pressure and vascular resistance.  Cerebral perfusion pressure (CPP) ≈ MAP − ICP  when intracranial pressure exceeds venous outflow pressure. \n Cerebral vessels autoregulate over a range of pressures. Increased arterial CO₂ is a potent cerebral vasodilator; marked hypocapnia constricts cerebral arterioles. Severe hypoxia can also cause vasodilatation. Blood flow is adjusted regionally to neural activity. \n Intracranial pressure and the Monro–Kellie principle \n Within the largely fixed adult skull, brain tissue, blood and CSF share a limited volume. Added volume can initially be offset by shifting CSF/venous blood, but beyond compensation  ICP rises sharply , potentially reducing CPP and causing the Cushing response. \n CSF path and functions \n CSF is produced mainly by  choroid plexus , flows from lateral ventricles through interventricular foramina (Monro) to third ventricle → cerebral aqueduct → fourth ventricle → median/lateral apertures → subarachnoid space, and returns ultimately to venous/lymphatic drainage pathways. Typical adult CSF volume is about  150 mL . It provides buoyancy and cushioning and supports CNS waste/solute handling. \n  Hydrocephalus  may result from impaired flow or absorption. The subarachnoid space normally contains CSF; the subdural space is a  potential , not normally CSF-filled, space. \n Blood–brain barrier (BBB) \n Specialised brain capillary  endothelial tight junctions , transporters and neurovascular unit limit uncontrolled solute passage. Glucose enters through transporters (not simply free diffusion), while many polar compounds require specialised transport. BBB integrity is important for neuronal extracellular stability and drug access. \n  Check:  Why can rising ICP cause brain ischaemia even when measured arterial BP appears normal? \n  Answer CPP is approximately MAP minus ICP. A rise in ICP reduces the pressure gradient driving cerebral blood flow despite unchanged systemic MAP. \n\n Identify the arterial supplies and vulnerability \n The  anterior, middle and posterior cerebral arteries  supply different territories. The two internal carotids and vertebrobasilar system connect variably in the  Circle of Willis , potentially supporting collateral supply; the middle cerebral artery is a large terminal branch but  not itself part of the arterial circle . The actual territories and clinical consequences of occlusion vary; the lecturer includes  ischaemic  (thrombotic/embolic) and  haemorrhagic  stroke as broad categories. \n Meninges, CSF and pressure \n From outside inward:  dura mater ,  arachnoid mater ,  subarachnoid space containing CSF , then  pia mater  adhering to brain/spinal cord. The normal subdural space is only potential. CSF is formed mainly in the ventricular choroid plexuses; normal adult total volume is about  150 mL , with production several hundred mL/day. Flow proceeds lateral ventricles →  foramina of Monro  → third ventricle →  aqueduct of Sylvius  → fourth ventricle → median (Magendie) and lateral (Luschka) apertures → subarachnoid space. CSF ultimately leaves through arachnoid and lymphatic outflow pathways.  Communicating hydrocephalus  generally has impaired CSF handling beyond the ventricular pathway;  noncommunicating hydrocephalus  has an obstruction within it. \n CSF protects via  buoyancy and cushioning  and supports interstitial solute clearance.  Lumbar puncture  samples subarachnoid CSF below the usual adult spinal cord termination; do not infer puncture is risk-free when raised ICP or mass effect is suspected. \n Cerebral blood flow control \n  CPP ≈ MAP − ICP  in the common approximation, and high ICP compresses intracranial vessels. Local vessels respond strongly to  CO₂ : rising CO₂ usually dilates and falling CO₂ constricts cerebral arterioles. Brain tissue normally relies heavily on continuous glucose/oxygen delivery, using glucose transport across the BBB and alternative fuels such as  ketone bodies during prolonged starvation ; the slides' suggestion of freely substituting fatty acids is not a general statement of normal brain metabolism. \n The  BBB  depends on continuous nonfenestrated endothelial cells with tight junctions, specialised transporters and associated cells. Glucose uses  GLUT1 , and lipid-soluble gases diffuse comparatively readily. The BBB is  not  the same as the blood–CSF barrier at the choroid plexus. \n  Practice:  A brain tumour increases cranial volume beyond compensatory shifts. ICP rises, CPP can fall, and brain perfusion may worsen even if peripheral BP is unchanged. \n"},{"slug":"coronary","title":"Coronary Circulation","group":"Regional","source":"Lecture 6 Regional Circulation Proper.pdf","pages":"26-29; extra diagrams Circulation 3 42-43","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"the-myocardium-needs-its-own-perfusion","title":"The myocardium needs its own perfusion"},{"id":"why-left-coronary-flow-favours-diastole","title":"Why left coronary flow favours diastole"},{"id":"strong-metabolic-regulation","title":"Strong metabolic regulation"},{"id":"ischaemia-angina-and-infarction","title":"Ischaemia, angina and infarction"},{"id":"routes-and-drainage","title":"Routes and drainage"},{"id":"why-oxygen-delivery-relies-on-more-flow","title":"Why oxygen delivery relies on more flow"}],"html":"<h1>Coronary Circulation</h1>\n<h2 id=\"the-myocardium-needs-its-own-perfusion\">The myocardium needs its own perfusion</h2>\n<p>The <strong>right and left coronary arteries</strong> originate from the aortic root and supply the cardiac muscle. The majority of venous drainage reaches the right atrium through the <strong>coronary sinus</strong>; additional veins may drain directly into chambers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"why-left-coronary-flow-favours-diastole\">Why left coronary flow favours diastole</h2>\n<p>During systole, high left-ventricular wall tension compresses intramyocardial coronary vessels, especially in the subendocardium. As ventricular pressure falls during <strong>diastole</strong>, coronary resistance decreases and perfusion increases. Therefore markedly shortened diastole in tachycardia may threaten myocardial oxygen supply even as oxygen demand rises.</p>\n<h2 id=\"strong-metabolic-regulation\">Strong metabolic regulation</h2>\n<p>Myocardium already extracts a high fraction of delivered oxygen at rest. When its demand increases, oxygen supply is raised mainly by <strong>increased coronary blood flow</strong>. Local dilators include <strong>adenosine, NO</strong> and metabolites associated with increased activity. Direct sympathetic α vasoconstriction may occur, but metabolic vasodilatation usually dominates during increased cardiac work.</p>\n<h2 id=\"ischaemia-angina-and-infarction\">Ischaemia, angina and infarction</h2>\n<p>A narrowed coronary artery may provide adequate resting flow but insufficient reserve on exercise, producing <strong>angina</strong>. Sustained severe interruption of coronary perfusion can cause <strong>myocardial infarction</strong>, with irreversible myocyte injury. Coronary spasm, thrombosis and plaque complications alter flow.</p>\n<p><strong>Check:</strong> Why might extreme tachycardia simultaneously increase myocardial oxygen demand and reduce supply?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary>More rapid/forceful work raises demand, while marked shortening of diastole reduces the time when left coronary perfusion is greatest.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"routes-and-drainage\">Routes and drainage</h2>\n<p>The <strong>left coronary artery</strong> typically divides into <strong>left anterior descending (anterior interventricular)</strong> and <strong>circumflex</strong> branches; the <strong>right coronary artery</strong> supplies right-sided branches and often the posterior descending artery, depending on coronary dominance. Dominance is defined by the origin of the <strong>posterior descending artery</strong>, not simply by which coronary artery looks bigger. Cardiac venous blood returns mainly through the <strong>coronary sinus</strong> to the right atrium, with some anterior and smallest cardiac veins draining separately.</p>\n<h2 id=\"why-oxygen-delivery-relies-on-more-flow\">Why oxygen delivery relies on more flow</h2>\n<p>Myocardial oxygen extraction is relatively high even at rest. When heart rate, contractility or wall stress increases oxygen requirements, the principal normal adjustment is <strong>increased coronary blood flow</strong>. Local metabolites including <strong>adenosine</strong>, changes in oxygen tension, potassium and endothelial <strong>NO</strong> help vasodilate coronary resistance vessels. Sympathetic α receptors may constrict directly, while β₁ stimulation of the heart raises metabolism and typically causes <strong>secondary metabolic coronary dilation</strong>; the lecturer's wording that β₁-generated metabolites constrict coronary arteries is inaccurate.</p>\n<p>In the <strong>left ventricle</strong>, intramyocardial vessels are compressed during systole, so much flow occurs in <strong>diastole</strong>. Extreme tachycardia shortens diastole and simultaneously increases oxygen demand. A narrowed coronary artery may supply adequate flow at rest yet fail during exercise (<strong>demand ischaemia/angina</strong>); sustained inadequate oxygen delivery can injure myocytes (<strong>infarction</strong>).</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Why can severe aortic stenosis increase risk of myocardial ischaemia? High ventricular pressure raises wall stress and oxygen demand; altered pressure relations may also compromise subendocardial perfusion.</p>\n","plain":" Coronary Circulation \n The myocardium needs its own perfusion \n The  right and left coronary arteries  originate from the aortic root and supply the cardiac muscle. The majority of venous drainage reaches the right atrium through the  coronary sinus ; additional veins may drain directly into chambers. \n Why left coronary flow favours diastole \n During systole, high left-ventricular wall tension compresses intramyocardial coronary vessels, especially in the subendocardium. As ventricular pressure falls during  diastole , coronary resistance decreases and perfusion increases. Therefore markedly shortened diastole in tachycardia may threaten myocardial oxygen supply even as oxygen demand rises. \n Strong metabolic regulation \n Myocardium already extracts a high fraction of delivered oxygen at rest. When its demand increases, oxygen supply is raised mainly by  increased coronary blood flow . Local dilators include  adenosine, NO  and metabolites associated with increased activity. Direct sympathetic α vasoconstriction may occur, but metabolic vasodilatation usually dominates during increased cardiac work. \n Ischaemia, angina and infarction \n A narrowed coronary artery may provide adequate resting flow but insufficient reserve on exercise, producing  angina . Sustained severe interruption of coronary perfusion can cause  myocardial infarction , with irreversible myocyte injury. Coronary spasm, thrombosis and plaque complications alter flow. \n  Check:  Why might extreme tachycardia simultaneously increase myocardial oxygen demand and reduce supply? \n  Answer More rapid/forceful work raises demand, while marked shortening of diastole reduces the time when left coronary perfusion is greatest. \n\n Routes and drainage \n The  left coronary artery  typically divides into  left anterior descending (anterior interventricular)  and  circumflex  branches; the  right coronary artery  supplies right-sided branches and often the posterior descending artery, depending on coronary dominance. Dominance is defined by the origin of the  posterior descending artery , not simply by which coronary artery looks bigger. Cardiac venous blood returns mainly through the  coronary sinus  to the right atrium, with some anterior and smallest cardiac veins draining separately. \n Why oxygen delivery relies on more flow \n Myocardial oxygen extraction is relatively high even at rest. When heart rate, contractility or wall stress increases oxygen requirements, the principal normal adjustment is  increased coronary blood flow . Local metabolites including  adenosine , changes in oxygen tension, potassium and endothelial  NO  help vasodilate coronary resistance vessels. Sympathetic α receptors may constrict directly, while β₁ stimulation of the heart raises metabolism and typically causes  secondary metabolic coronary dilation ; the lecturer's wording that β₁-generated metabolites constrict coronary arteries is inaccurate. \n In the  left ventricle , intramyocardial vessels are compressed during systole, so much flow occurs in  diastole . Extreme tachycardia shortens diastole and simultaneously increases oxygen demand. A narrowed coronary artery may supply adequate flow at rest yet fail during exercise ( demand ischaemia/angina ); sustained inadequate oxygen delivery can injure myocytes ( infarction ). \n  Practice:  Why can severe aortic stenosis increase risk of myocardial ischaemia? High ventricular pressure raises wall stress and oxygen demand; altered pressure relations may also compromise subendocardial perfusion. \n"},{"slug":"pulmonary","title":"Pulmonary Circulation","group":"Regional","source":"Lecture 6 Regional Circulation Proper.pdf","pages":"30-32","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"one-flow-different-pressures","title":"One flow, different pressures"},{"id":"gravity-and-distribution","title":"Gravity and distribution"},{"id":"the-unusual-response-to-low-oxygen","title":"The unusual response to low oxygen"},{"id":"oedema-prevention","title":"Oedema prevention"},{"id":"pulmonary-vs-systemic-circuits","title":"Pulmonary vs systemic circuits"},{"id":"gravity-and-vascular-recruitment","title":"Gravity and vascular recruitment"},{"id":"contrast-hypoxic-responses-in-two-circulations","title":"Contrast hypoxic responses in two circulations"}],"html":"<h1>Pulmonary Circulation</h1>\n<h2 id=\"one-flow-different-pressures\">One flow, different pressures</h2>\n<p>The right ventricle delivers approximately the same minute output as the left ventricle, but the pulmonary circuit normally has <strong>far lower vascular resistance and pressure</strong>. Its thin vessels and capillaries permit gas exchange without excessive filtration. The <strong>bronchial circulation</strong> from systemic arteries supplies supporting lung tissues in addition to the low-pressure pulmonary circulation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"gravity-and-distribution\">Gravity and distribution</h2>\n<p>In an upright person, gravity changes hydrostatic pressures across lung regions. Regional blood flow varies from apex to base as vascular pressures, alveolar pressure and recruitment/distension vary; this is often conceptualized with <strong>West lung zones</strong>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-unusual-response-to-low-oxygen\">The unusual response to low oxygen</h2>\n<p>Systemic resistance vessels commonly dilate in metabolically hypoxic tissue, but <strong>small pulmonary arteries constrict in response to low alveolar PO₂</strong>. This diverts blood toward better-ventilated alveoli and can improve ventilation–perfusion matching. Diffuse chronic alveolar hypoxia, however, can raise pulmonary vascular resistance and contribute to pulmonary hypertension.</p>\n<h2 id=\"oedema-prevention\">Oedema prevention</h2>\n<p>Normally low pulmonary capillary hydrostatic pressure and efficient lymphatic drainage oppose fluid accumulation in alveoli. Raised left atrial/pulmonary venous pressure in left-heart failure increases filtration and may produce pulmonary oedema.</p>\n<p><strong>Check:</strong> Why is hypoxic vasoconstriction useful locally in the lungs but harmful when it affects the entire lung?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary>Locally it diverts blood to better-ventilated regions. Widespread hypoxia constricts many pulmonary vessels, increasing resistance and pulmonary arterial pressure.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"pulmonary-vs-systemic-circuits\">Pulmonary vs systemic circuits</h2>\n<p>Both receive nearly the same cardiac output, yet normal pulmonary arterial pressures are much lower because pulmonary vascular resistance is low. The lung has dual circulations: <strong>pulmonary vessels</strong> for gas exchange and <strong>bronchial arteries</strong> (from the systemic aortic circulation) nourishing supporting tissues. Part of bronchial venous drainage enters pulmonary veins, contributing to normal physiological venous admixture.</p>\n<h2 id=\"gravity-and-vascular-recruitment\">Gravity and vascular recruitment</h2>\n<p>In an upright lung, blood flow tends to be greater toward dependent (basal) regions because of hydrostatic pressure and vascular recruitment/distension. The classic <strong>West lung zones</strong> compare pulmonary arterial, alveolar and venous pressures; zone relationships shift with posture, lung pressure and disease. At rest pulmonary capillaries normally have low hydrostatic pressure and barrier/lymphatic protection against alveolar flooding, although pulmonary oedema can develop when those safeguards fail.</p>\n<h2 id=\"contrast-hypoxic-responses-in-two-circulations\">Contrast hypoxic responses in two circulations</h2>\n<p>When an alveolar region is poorly oxygenated, nearby pulmonary arterioles constrict (<strong>hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction</strong>) to redirect blood toward better-ventilated alveoli. This is the opposite of the typical <strong>systemic local hypoxic dilation</strong> that boosts oxygen delivery to active tissues. Diffuse sustained alveolar hypoxia can raise pulmonary vascular resistance and eventually cause pulmonary hypertension/right-ventricular strain.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Why is high-altitude hypoxia capable of raising pulmonary pressure? Global alveolar hypoxia stimulates widespread pulmonary arteriolar constriction instead of merely diverting flow within one small region.</p>\n","plain":" Pulmonary Circulation \n One flow, different pressures \n The right ventricle delivers approximately the same minute output as the left ventricle, but the pulmonary circuit normally has  far lower vascular resistance and pressure . Its thin vessels and capillaries permit gas exchange without excessive filtration. The  bronchial circulation  from systemic arteries supplies supporting lung tissues in addition to the low-pressure pulmonary circulation. \n Gravity and distribution \n In an upright person, gravity changes hydrostatic pressures across lung regions. Regional blood flow varies from apex to base as vascular pressures, alveolar pressure and recruitment/distension vary; this is often conceptualized with  West lung zones . \n The unusual response to low oxygen \n Systemic resistance vessels commonly dilate in metabolically hypoxic tissue, but  small pulmonary arteries constrict in response to low alveolar PO₂ . This diverts blood toward better-ventilated alveoli and can improve ventilation–perfusion matching. Diffuse chronic alveolar hypoxia, however, can raise pulmonary vascular resistance and contribute to pulmonary hypertension. \n Oedema prevention \n Normally low pulmonary capillary hydrostatic pressure and efficient lymphatic drainage oppose fluid accumulation in alveoli. Raised left atrial/pulmonary venous pressure in left-heart failure increases filtration and may produce pulmonary oedema. \n  Check:  Why is hypoxic vasoconstriction useful locally in the lungs but harmful when it affects the entire lung? \n  Answer Locally it diverts blood to better-ventilated regions. Widespread hypoxia constricts many pulmonary vessels, increasing resistance and pulmonary arterial pressure. \n\n Pulmonary vs systemic circuits \n Both receive nearly the same cardiac output, yet normal pulmonary arterial pressures are much lower because pulmonary vascular resistance is low. The lung has dual circulations:  pulmonary vessels  for gas exchange and  bronchial arteries  (from the systemic aortic circulation) nourishing supporting tissues. Part of bronchial venous drainage enters pulmonary veins, contributing to normal physiological venous admixture. \n Gravity and vascular recruitment \n In an upright lung, blood flow tends to be greater toward dependent (basal) regions because of hydrostatic pressure and vascular recruitment/distension. The classic  West lung zones  compare pulmonary arterial, alveolar and venous pressures; zone relationships shift with posture, lung pressure and disease. At rest pulmonary capillaries normally have low hydrostatic pressure and barrier/lymphatic protection against alveolar flooding, although pulmonary oedema can develop when those safeguards fail. \n Contrast hypoxic responses in two circulations \n When an alveolar region is poorly oxygenated, nearby pulmonary arterioles constrict ( hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction ) to redirect blood toward better-ventilated alveoli. This is the opposite of the typical  systemic local hypoxic dilation  that boosts oxygen delivery to active tissues. Diffuse sustained alveolar hypoxia can raise pulmonary vascular resistance and eventually cause pulmonary hypertension/right-ventricular strain. \n  Practice:  Why is high-altitude hypoxia capable of raising pulmonary pressure? Global alveolar hypoxia stimulates widespread pulmonary arteriolar constriction instead of merely diverting flow within one small region. \n"},{"slug":"splanchnic","title":"Splanchnic and Hepatic Circulation","group":"Regional","source":"Lecture 6 Regional Circulation Proper.pdf","pages":"33-37","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"an-unusual-route-through-two-capillary-networks","title":"An unusual route through two capillary networks"},{"id":"hepatic-microcirculation","title":"Hepatic microcirculation"},{"id":"blood-flow-after-meals-and-in-shock","title":"Blood flow after meals and in shock"},{"id":"ascites","title":"Ascites"},{"id":"two-inflows-and-the-hepatic-microvascular-route","title":"Two inflows and the hepatic microvascular route"},{"id":"digestion-shock-and-venous-storage","title":"Digestion, shock and venous storage"},{"id":"explain-ascites-without-one-misleading-protein-story","title":"Explain ascites without one misleading protein story"}],"html":"<h1>Splanchnic and Hepatic Circulation</h1>\n<h2 id=\"an-unusual-route-through-two-capillary-networks\">An unusual route through two capillary networks</h2>\n<p>The abdominal organs receive arterial blood mainly through the <strong>coeliac trunk</strong>, <strong>superior mesenteric artery</strong> and <strong>inferior mesenteric artery</strong>. Venous blood from much of the gut passes through the <strong>portal vein</strong> to liver sinusoids before draining through hepatic veins to the inferior vena cava. Thus, absorbed nutrients are delivered to the liver before much systemic distribution.</p>\n<h2 id=\"hepatic-microcirculation\">Hepatic microcirculation</h2>\n<p>Branches of portal vein and hepatic artery enter near portal tracts, blood mixes in liver <strong>sinusoids</strong>, and drains toward <strong>central veins</strong>. Zone 1 hepatocytes near inflow have greater oxygen availability, while <strong>zone 3</strong> hepatocytes near central veins are more vulnerable to hypoxic damage. Hepatocytes receive oxygen from both portal and arterial sources.</p>\n<h2 id=\"blood-flow-after-meals-and-in-shock\">Blood flow after meals and in shock</h2>\n<p>Intestinal metabolic activity increases after feeding, promoting splanchnic blood flow. This region contains a sizeable venous reservoir. During haemorrhage, sympathetic constriction can mobilize splanchnic blood and reduce nonessential regional flow, although severe or prolonged hypoperfusion risks intestinal ischaemia.</p>\n<h2 id=\"ascites\">Ascites</h2>\n<p>Portal hypertension can increase hydrostatic pressure in abdominal microvessels and hepatic sinusoids. Combined with renal sodium retention, lymph production and often low plasma albumin, this favours <strong>ascites</strong>, accumulation of fluid in the peritoneal cavity. The mechanism is multifactorial; increased protein concentration in all ascitic fluid should not be assumed.</p>\n<p><strong>Check:</strong> Why can severe shock injure the gut even while blood pressure is defended?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary>Sympathetic redistribution reduces splanchnic perfusion to favour vital organs. Prolonged local hypoperfusion can cause mucosal ischaemia despite partly preserved systemic pressure.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"two-inflows-and-the-hepatic-microvascular-route\">Two inflows and the hepatic microvascular route</h2>\n<p>Arteries from the <strong>coeliac trunk, superior mesenteric and inferior mesenteric arteries</strong> supply abdominal organs; portal venous blood collects nutrients and metabolites from the gut/spleen before delivering them to the liver. The liver receives blood through <strong>portal vein and hepatic artery</strong> branches, which mix in sinusoids and drain to <strong>central veins → hepatic veins → IVC</strong>. The <strong>portal triad</strong> contains a portal venule, hepatic arteriole and bile ductule. Bile generally travels in a direction opposing sinusoidal blood drainage.</p>\n<p>In the classical lobule, periportal hepatocytes are closer to the portal triad and receive more oxygenated inflow; <strong>centrilobular (zone 3)</strong> hepatocytes near terminal hepatic venules have relatively lower oxygen availability and are especially vulnerable to some ischaemic insults. This is why the lecturer emphasises oxygen gradients within liver acini.</p>\n<h2 id=\"digestion-shock-and-venous-storage\">Digestion, shock and venous storage</h2>\n<p>Following a meal, intestinal metabolic activity increases <strong>splanchnic flow</strong> (postprandial hyperaemia). The splanchnic venous bed is a substantial <strong>blood reservoir</strong>. During marked sympathetic activation in haemorrhage, splanchnic vasoconstriction shifts blood centrally and reduces gut perfusion; prolonged severe underperfusion can injure the bowel.</p>\n<h2 id=\"explain-ascites-without-one-misleading-protein-story\">Explain ascites without one misleading protein story</h2>\n<p><strong>Ascites</strong> is fluid accumulated within the peritoneal cavity. Portal hypertension increases sinusoidal hydrostatic pressure and hepatic lymph formation; when lymphatic drainage is exceeded, fluid can accumulate in the abdomen. In cirrhosis, sodium/water retention and lowered serum albumin often contribute. The composition and protein concentration of ascitic fluid vary with the underlying cause; high-protein fluid is <strong>not universal</strong> in all ascites.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Why is zone 3 particularly sensitive to low oxygen supply? It lies downstream in the sinusoidal pathway and receives blood with less remaining oxygen.</p>\n","plain":" Splanchnic and Hepatic Circulation \n An unusual route through two capillary networks \n The abdominal organs receive arterial blood mainly through the  coeliac trunk ,  superior mesenteric artery  and  inferior mesenteric artery . Venous blood from much of the gut passes through the  portal vein  to liver sinusoids before draining through hepatic veins to the inferior vena cava. Thus, absorbed nutrients are delivered to the liver before much systemic distribution. \n Hepatic microcirculation \n Branches of portal vein and hepatic artery enter near portal tracts, blood mixes in liver  sinusoids , and drains toward  central veins . Zone 1 hepatocytes near inflow have greater oxygen availability, while  zone 3  hepatocytes near central veins are more vulnerable to hypoxic damage. Hepatocytes receive oxygen from both portal and arterial sources. \n Blood flow after meals and in shock \n Intestinal metabolic activity increases after feeding, promoting splanchnic blood flow. This region contains a sizeable venous reservoir. During haemorrhage, sympathetic constriction can mobilize splanchnic blood and reduce nonessential regional flow, although severe or prolonged hypoperfusion risks intestinal ischaemia. \n Ascites \n Portal hypertension can increase hydrostatic pressure in abdominal microvessels and hepatic sinusoids. Combined with renal sodium retention, lymph production and often low plasma albumin, this favours  ascites , accumulation of fluid in the peritoneal cavity. The mechanism is multifactorial; increased protein concentration in all ascitic fluid should not be assumed. \n  Check:  Why can severe shock injure the gut even while blood pressure is defended? \n  Answer Sympathetic redistribution reduces splanchnic perfusion to favour vital organs. Prolonged local hypoperfusion can cause mucosal ischaemia despite partly preserved systemic pressure. \n\n Two inflows and the hepatic microvascular route \n Arteries from the  coeliac trunk, superior mesenteric and inferior mesenteric arteries  supply abdominal organs; portal venous blood collects nutrients and metabolites from the gut/spleen before delivering them to the liver. The liver receives blood through  portal vein and hepatic artery  branches, which mix in sinusoids and drain to  central veins → hepatic veins → IVC . The  portal triad  contains a portal venule, hepatic arteriole and bile ductule. Bile generally travels in a direction opposing sinusoidal blood drainage. \n In the classical lobule, periportal hepatocytes are closer to the portal triad and receive more oxygenated inflow;  centrilobular (zone 3)  hepatocytes near terminal hepatic venules have relatively lower oxygen availability and are especially vulnerable to some ischaemic insults. This is why the lecturer emphasises oxygen gradients within liver acini. \n Digestion, shock and venous storage \n Following a meal, intestinal metabolic activity increases  splanchnic flow  (postprandial hyperaemia). The splanchnic venous bed is a substantial  blood reservoir . During marked sympathetic activation in haemorrhage, splanchnic vasoconstriction shifts blood centrally and reduces gut perfusion; prolonged severe underperfusion can injure the bowel. \n Explain ascites without one misleading protein story \n  Ascites  is fluid accumulated within the peritoneal cavity. Portal hypertension increases sinusoidal hydrostatic pressure and hepatic lymph formation; when lymphatic drainage is exceeded, fluid can accumulate in the abdomen. In cirrhosis, sodium/water retention and lowered serum albumin often contribute. The composition and protein concentration of ascitic fluid vary with the underlying cause; high-protein fluid is  not universal  in all ascites. \n  Practice:  Why is zone 3 particularly sensitive to low oxygen supply? It lies downstream in the sinusoidal pathway and receives blood with less remaining oxygen. \n"},{"slug":"skin","title":"Cutaneous Circulation","group":"Regional","source":"Lecture 6 Regional Circulation Proper.pdf","pages":"38-41","coverage":"lecturer","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"skin-blood-flow-regulates-heat","title":"Skin blood flow regulates heat"},{"id":"local-vascular-reactions-after-stroking-the-skin","title":"Local vascular reactions after stroking the skin"},{"id":"shock-and-temperature","title":"Shock and temperature"},{"id":"distinguish-blood-flow-functions","title":"Distinguish blood-flow functions"},{"id":"lewis-triple-response-to-stroking-the-skin","title":"Lewis triple response to stroking the skin"}],"html":"<h1>Cutaneous Circulation and the Triple Response</h1>\n<h2 id=\"skin-blood-flow-regulates-heat\">Skin blood flow regulates heat</h2>\n<p>Skin has a rich vascular network, including <strong>arteriovenous anastomoses</strong> in some regions. Increasing cutaneous perfusion promotes heat loss, whereas sympathetic vasoconstriction limits heat transfer and helps preserve central blood volume during haemorrhage.</p>\n<h2 id=\"local-vascular-reactions-after-stroking-the-skin\">Local vascular reactions after stroking the skin</h2>\n<p>A mild stroke can initially produce a transient pale line through local vasoconstriction (the <strong>white reaction</strong>). Firmer stroking may produce the <strong>triple response of Lewis</strong>:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Red line</strong> near the stimulated area, reflecting local vasodilatation.</li>\n<li><strong>Flare</strong>, a wider surrounding erythema mediated partly by sensory axon reflexes and vasodilators.</li>\n<li><strong>Wheal</strong>, a raised swelling caused by increased vascular permeability and fluid leakage.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Histamine contributes importantly to these responses; their timing and expression vary.</p>\n<h2 id=\"shock-and-temperature\">Shock and temperature</h2>\n<p>In haemorrhagic shock, intense skin vasoconstriction often produces cool, pale extremities. In warm environments, competing requirements for heat loss and blood-pressure maintenance influence vascular tone; skin circulation is regulated for <strong>thermoregulation</strong>, not simply nutrient demand.</p>\n<p><strong>Check:</strong> What makes a wheal different from the red flare around a skin scratch?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary>A wheal is local oedema from increased permeability, whereas the flare is wider redness from arteriolar vasodilatation involving a sensory axon reflex.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"distinguish-blood-flow-functions\">Distinguish blood-flow functions</h2>\n<p>Skin circulation is unusual because its flow can greatly exceed local nutrient needs when the body must <strong>lose heat</strong>. Sympathetic-mediated cutaneous constriction conserves heat in cold conditions, whereas vasodilator mechanisms and opening of thermoregulatory arteriovenous anastomoses help increase heat loss in warmth. During haemorrhagic shock, strong skin vasoconstriction can contribute to pallor and coolness.</p>\n<h2 id=\"lewis-triple-response-to-stroking-the-skin\">Lewis triple response to stroking the skin</h2>\n<p>A firm, blunt stroke produces a <strong>red line</strong> at the site (local capillary/arteriolar dilation), a surrounding <strong>flare</strong> (wider arteriolar dilation partly due to local sensory-nerve <strong>axon reflex</strong>) and a raised <strong>wheal</strong> (local oedema as permeability rises). Histamine contributes to the reaction. The lecturer separately describes a brief <strong>white line response</strong> with gentler stroking, traditionally attributed to local constrictor response; distinguish it from the red line/flare/wheal response.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Which component of the triple response is chiefly an oedematous swelling? The <strong>wheal</strong>; the flare is spreading redness.</p>\n","plain":" Cutaneous Circulation and the Triple Response \n Skin blood flow regulates heat \n Skin has a rich vascular network, including  arteriovenous anastomoses  in some regions. Increasing cutaneous perfusion promotes heat loss, whereas sympathetic vasoconstriction limits heat transfer and helps preserve central blood volume during haemorrhage. \n Local vascular reactions after stroking the skin \n A mild stroke can initially produce a transient pale line through local vasoconstriction (the  white reaction ). Firmer stroking may produce the  triple response of Lewis : \n \n  Red line  near the stimulated area, reflecting local vasodilatation. \n  Flare , a wider surrounding erythema mediated partly by sensory axon reflexes and vasodilators. \n  Wheal , a raised swelling caused by increased vascular permeability and fluid leakage. \n \n Histamine contributes importantly to these responses; their timing and expression vary. \n Shock and temperature \n In haemorrhagic shock, intense skin vasoconstriction often produces cool, pale extremities. In warm environments, competing requirements for heat loss and blood-pressure maintenance influence vascular tone; skin circulation is regulated for  thermoregulation , not simply nutrient demand. \n  Check:  What makes a wheal different from the red flare around a skin scratch? \n  Answer A wheal is local oedema from increased permeability, whereas the flare is wider redness from arteriolar vasodilatation involving a sensory axon reflex. \n\n Distinguish blood-flow functions \n Skin circulation is unusual because its flow can greatly exceed local nutrient needs when the body must  lose heat . Sympathetic-mediated cutaneous constriction conserves heat in cold conditions, whereas vasodilator mechanisms and opening of thermoregulatory arteriovenous anastomoses help increase heat loss in warmth. During haemorrhagic shock, strong skin vasoconstriction can contribute to pallor and coolness. \n Lewis triple response to stroking the skin \n A firm, blunt stroke produces a  red line  at the site (local capillary/arteriolar dilation), a surrounding  flare  (wider arteriolar dilation partly due to local sensory-nerve  axon reflex ) and a raised  wheal  (local oedema as permeability rises). Histamine contributes to the reaction. The lecturer separately describes a brief  white line response  with gentler stroking, traditionally attributed to local constrictor response; distinguish it from the red line/flare/wheal response. \n  Practice:  Which component of the triple response is chiefly an oedematous swelling? The  wheal ; the flare is spreading redness. \n"},{"slug":"fetal","title":"Fetal Circulation and Birth Transitions","group":"Regional","source":"Lecture 6 Regional Circulation Proper.pdf","pages":"42-45","coverage":"lecturer supplemented","priority":"core","sections":[{"id":"why-fetal-flow-takes-shortcuts","title":"Why fetal flow takes shortcuts"},{"id":"what-changes-after-the-first-breaths","title":"What changes after the first breaths?"},{"id":"fetal-haemoglobin","title":"Fetal haemoglobin"},{"id":"trace-the-preferentially-oxygenated-fetal-stream","title":"Trace the preferentially oxygenated fetal stream"},{"id":"what-closes-after-delivery","title":"What closes after delivery?"}],"html":"<h1>Fetal Circulation and the Transition at Birth</h1>\n<h2 id=\"why-fetal-flow-takes-shortcuts\">Why fetal flow takes shortcuts</h2>\n<p>Before birth, gas exchange happens in the <strong>placenta</strong>, not the fluid-filled lungs. Oxygenated blood returns through the <strong>umbilical vein</strong>. Some passes through the liver, while a substantial fraction bypasses hepatic sinusoids through the <strong>ductus venosus</strong> to the inferior vena cava.</p>\n<p>Because fetal pulmonary vascular resistance is high, right-heart flow partly bypasses lungs by two pathways:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Foramen ovale:</strong> right atrium → left atrium (favoured by fetal pressure relations and directed IVC flow).</li>\n<li><strong>Ductus arteriosus:</strong> pulmonary trunk → descending aorta.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Two <strong>umbilical arteries</strong> carry fetal blood to the placenta. Oxygen-rich and oxygen-poor streams mix, so fetal saturation varies between vessels and chambers.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-changes-after-the-first-breaths\">What changes after the first breaths?</h2>\n<p>Lung expansion and increasing alveolar oxygen lower pulmonary vascular resistance, increasing pulmonary blood flow and left atrial return. Umbilical cord clamping removes placental low-resistance flow and increases systemic vascular resistance. Rising <strong>left atrial pressure relative to right</strong> functionally closes the foramen ovale; increased oxygen and reduced prostaglandin influence constrict the ductus arteriosus.</p>\n<h2 id=\"fetal-haemoglobin\">Fetal haemoglobin</h2>\n<p><strong>HbF</strong> has higher oxygen affinity than adult HbA, in part because it binds <strong>2,3-BPG</strong> less strongly. This helps fetal blood acquire oxygen across the placenta. HbF declines after birth as adult haemoglobin replaces it over months.</p>\n<p><strong>Check:</strong> Why does the first breath increase blood return from lungs to the left atrium?</p>\n<details><summary>Answer</summary>Alveolar expansion and increased oxygen markedly decrease pulmonary vascular resistance, allowing more right-ventricular output through the lungs into pulmonary veins and left atrium.</details>\n\n<h2 id=\"trace-the-preferentially-oxygenated-fetal-stream\">Trace the preferentially oxygenated fetal stream</h2>\n<p>The placenta oxygenates fetal blood. The <strong>umbilical vein</strong> brings comparatively oxygen-rich blood toward the liver; some flows through the <strong>ductus venosus</strong> to the IVC. Preferential streaming across the <strong>foramen ovale</strong> supplies left atrium → left ventricle → ascending aorta, supporting perfusion of the developing heart and brain. Much right ventricular output enters pulmonary trunk and passes through <strong>ductus arteriosus</strong> into descending aorta, bypassing the high-resistance unventilated lungs. <strong>Umbilical arteries</strong> return lower-oxygen blood to placenta.</p>\n<h2 id=\"what-closes-after-delivery\">What closes after delivery?</h2>\n<p>First breaths expand lungs and increase alveolar oxygen, sharply reducing <strong>pulmonary vascular resistance</strong> and increasing pulmonary venous return. Cord clamping removes the low-resistance placental bed and increases <strong>systemic vascular resistance</strong>. Higher left than right atrial pressure functionally closes the foramen ovale, while rising oxygen and lower prostaglandin signalling promote <strong>ductus arteriosus constriction</strong>. Anatomical closure/remodelling follows functional closure on a different time scale; persistent patency is clinically relevant.</p>\n<p><strong>HbF</strong> holds oxygen more avidly than HbA partly due to lower 2,3-BPG affinity. HbF does not invariably disappear by an exact fourth postnatal month; replacement is gradual over months.</p>\n<p><strong>Practice:</strong> Why does pulmonary blood flow rise after birth even as systemic resistance increases? Lung expansion/oxygen directly reduce <strong>pulmonary</strong> resistance; placental separation independently raises <strong>systemic</strong> resistance.</p>\n","plain":" Fetal Circulation and the Transition at Birth \n Why fetal flow takes shortcuts \n Before birth, gas exchange happens in the  placenta , not the fluid-filled lungs. Oxygenated blood returns through the  umbilical vein . Some passes through the liver, while a substantial fraction bypasses hepatic sinusoids through the  ductus venosus  to the inferior vena cava. \n Because fetal pulmonary vascular resistance is high, right-heart flow partly bypasses lungs by two pathways: \n \n  Foramen ovale:  right atrium → left atrium (favoured by fetal pressure relations and directed IVC flow). \n  Ductus arteriosus:  pulmonary trunk → descending aorta. \n \n Two  umbilical arteries  carry fetal blood to the placenta. Oxygen-rich and oxygen-poor streams mix, so fetal saturation varies between vessels and chambers. \n What changes after the first breaths? \n Lung expansion and increasing alveolar oxygen lower pulmonary vascular resistance, increasing pulmonary blood flow and left atrial return. Umbilical cord clamping removes placental low-resistance flow and increases systemic vascular resistance. Rising  left atrial pressure relative to right  functionally closes the foramen ovale; increased oxygen and reduced prostaglandin influence constrict the ductus arteriosus. \n Fetal haemoglobin \n  HbF  has higher oxygen affinity than adult HbA, in part because it binds  2,3-BPG  less strongly. This helps fetal blood acquire oxygen across the placenta. HbF declines after birth as adult haemoglobin replaces it over months. \n  Check:  Why does the first breath increase blood return from lungs to the left atrium? \n  Answer Alveolar expansion and increased oxygen markedly decrease pulmonary vascular resistance, allowing more right-ventricular output through the lungs into pulmonary veins and left atrium. \n\n Trace the preferentially oxygenated fetal stream \n The placenta oxygenates fetal blood. The  umbilical vein  brings comparatively oxygen-rich blood toward the liver; some flows through the  ductus venosus  to the IVC. Preferential streaming across the  foramen ovale  supplies left atrium → left ventricle → ascending aorta, supporting perfusion of the developing heart and brain. Much right ventricular output enters pulmonary trunk and passes through  ductus arteriosus  into descending aorta, bypassing the high-resistance unventilated lungs.  Umbilical arteries  return lower-oxygen blood to placenta. \n What closes after delivery? \n First breaths expand lungs and increase alveolar oxygen, sharply reducing  pulmonary vascular resistance  and increasing pulmonary venous return. Cord clamping removes the low-resistance placental bed and increases  systemic vascular resistance . Higher left than right atrial pressure functionally closes the foramen ovale, while rising oxygen and lower prostaglandin signalling promote  ductus arteriosus constriction . Anatomical closure/remodelling follows functional closure on a different time scale; persistent patency is clinically relevant. \n  HbF  holds oxygen more avidly than HbA partly due to lower 2,3-BPG affinity. HbF does not invariably disappear by an exact fourth postnatal month; replacement is gradual over months. \n  Practice:  Why does pulmonary blood flow rise after birth even as systemic resistance increases? Lung expansion/oxygen directly reduce  pulmonary  resistance; placental separation independently raises  systemic  resistance. \n"}];
